      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-18_WidnesExaminer_art0006" n="art0006">
        <head>Mc DOUG A LL'S PATENT SELF-RAISING El.O Ult ATMTSII eb</head>
        <p>Mc DOUG A LL'S PATENT SELF-RAISING El.O Ult ATMTSII eb</p>
        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</p>
        <p>STUMP ORATOR: What is better than to die rat one's country?" Pensioner: To live on her." "WHAT is conscience-money, pa?" "Con. ecience-money ? It is the trifle your mother:leaves in my pocket when she cleans out all the rest." " WELL, Bobby, whst do you want to be when you grow up?" Bobby (suffering from parental discipline): "An orphan." WHEN a girl gets married the women inquire "how her mother took it," as though it was a funeral.</p>
        <p>"I HEAR you've got a very industrious hue. band." "Yee, mum ; he's always finding some. thinz for me to do."</p>
        <p>"You don't agree with Shakespeare that the child is the father of the man?' "Not always. She is sometimes the mother of the woman."</p>
        <p>JIN : " What's Winkers doing' -f:OTE; living now ?" Blinks : " Oh, anything that his rich wife tal,q HE : "I want to know, once for all, who is the master of this house I" She: "You'll be much happier if you don't find out."</p>
        <p>Boy : "Papa, where's Atoms ?" Papa : "Athens, you mean child." Boy: "No, papa— Atoms, the place where people are blown to." TEACHER : "Mary, make a sentence with 'dogma' as subject." Mary (after careful thought): "The dogma has three puppies." Mks. JINGSO (visiting; time, 11 p.m.) : "Has your husband any shortcomings?" Mrs. Hingso (looking furtively at the clock): "No, it's his long, comings that worry me." Alms. WicKwram "Why don't you exercise the talents Providence has bestowed on you, instead of begging ?" Beggar: "Lady, Providence has give me the best beggin' talents in the profession." " I HAva discovered another clue," said the de- tective. " What a grand thing it would be," re- turned the man who had employed him, "it clues were criminals."</p>
        <p>"Tine worm will turn," she said. "Of course," he replied. " The worm is built on a plan that makes turning comparatively easy. It's more or 1e8,4 of a turn itself."</p>
        <p>SUE : "Did you ever kiss a girl when she wasn't looking ? ' He: " Yes ; but only once. She made me do it over again when she was locking." JINKS "Why don't they make bicycle wheels iwitn wooden spokes, the same as carriage wheels?" Binks : "Why, no one could tell which were the spokes and which were the legs." "BY jove jest look at that hat skimming into the water !" "By-the-way, where is your hat? Did you leave it in the cabin ? " "No ; I dis- tinctly remember having it on when I came on deck. reat Scott! that's my hat I" PROFESSIONAL: "Please gimme sixpence, sir, to buy some bread." Muggine "Why, I gave you sixpencenot half-an-hour ago." Professional (taking in the situation): "Yes, sir, I know, sir; but —l'm a terrible bread-eater."</p>
        <p>SAXO:C PASSENGSR (on Highland coach): "Of coarse, you're well accpainted with the country round about her. Da you know Glen Accron ?' Driver: "Ay, weal." Saxon Passenger (who has just about the estate): "What sort of a place is it ?'' Driver: "Wall, if ye saw the de'il tethered on'f, ye'd just ray, 'Pair brute !' " YOUNG AUTHOR: "Tell me frankly what you think of the manuscript of my book? I want to get it in shape for publication, as I have several other iron ain the fire." Critical Friend : " Well, that being the case, I would advise you to use the manuPoript for fuel. It might at least help to heat the other irons."</p>
        <p>A COUNTRY parson's gardener was dismissed by his master for stealing. The following is the entry in the diary: "The Scripture enjoins us that if a man take away thy coat, let him have thy cloak also.' This mot fling, having found out John had been stealing my potatoes, I have given him the sack."</p>
        <p>YOUNG LADT : "I suppose you have had some narrow escapes, Lieutenant Dashaway?" Lieu- tenant Dashaway " Y-a-a-s. Awfully narrow eecapo once, bah Jove !" Young Lady (breath- leseily): In what way?" Lieutenant Dashaway "Vewy near ordahed on active service, don'tcher- know, but the war came to an end before we em. barked."</p>
        <p>THE safest plan to pursue at a reception where the waiters and male guests are clad in swallow- tails is to jam your hands into your trouser's pockets and jingle a few coins when you meet a stranger. If his eyes begin to bulge and his hand commences to travel towards you he's a waiter. Thiq is a straight tip. Sliy; had just accepted him, and they were bliss- fully di.icussing the "might-have-beens." "Dar- Beg," he inquired, in the tone of one who knows what the answer will be—"darling, why didn't you accept that little donkey of a dude?" "Because," she answered, dreamily, "I loved another." "How's this? You're already advertising again a dog lost. That's the third dog you've lost in a mouth 1" "Oh, it's just my luck I Since my daughter has bcen taking singing lessons I can't keep an animal in the place 1" Ths addresses of a young man having been de. dined by a young lady, he paid court to her sister. " How much you resemble your sister," said he the evening of his first call. " You have the same hair, the same forehead, and the same eyes—" "And the same noes 1" she added quickly. EMPLOYER : "Why did you let that collector in here? Why didn't you tell him I was out of town?" Office Boy: "I did tell him so, but 31sit then you yelled: Here, Jimmy. Confound you, atop argotic' out in the hall and bring them letters in to me. rm waitie l'"</p>
        <p>KNEW TEE SIGNS.—The postman left a letter at the house of Mr. Binks-Jones, whose son is at college. Mr. B. J.: "Here is a letter from Charles." Mrs. B. J. : "Read it." Mr. B. J. (reading) : " My dearest, darlingest mother—'" Mrs. B. J.: "Great heavens! The young noun-. drel needs more money again already !" KITCEIENMAID (at china shop) : •• What ! Only a balt.crown tip? During the past year I have br.iir.sn three soup tureens, twenty-six cups, thirty- five saucers, five meat dishes and fifty-six plates." Bhopman "Well, then, here's another form ; and don't. forget me, you know." " Yonsia man, my daughter is too young to marry. A girl of her age cannot be sure of her own mind in a matter of such importance." "I fully realise that," replied the young man, who had jtlab secured the fair one's consent. "That's wby I don't want to wait." MAN nit HARD LUCK: "I am reduced to the painful expedient of asking you to buy the diamonds in my wife's jewellery and to replace them with -imitations." Jeweller (examining the jewels) : "a Your wife evidently has preceded you in evolving that clever plan." "As I" sighed she. "I wish you owned a yacht. It would be just lovely togo for a cruise." " sorry I can't oblige you," replied the young man, "but the best I can offer is a little smack." And the silence was punctured b; a sound resembling the explosion of a pop-gun. "DON'T waste your time lopping off the branches," said the woodman to his son, "but lay the axe at the root of the tree." And the young man, being a dutiful and obedient son, did exactly BA his father bade him, and then went out fishing, and enjoyed himself. Thus virtue was rewarded— until he met his father again.</p>
        <p>"THEY say that electricity is a sure antidote for the sting of bees." said the electrieal enthu- siast. "Yes I've tried it," said the student. "Really ? How did it work?" "Well, a ben stung me, and it hurt me so that I applied the electricity. After that I went over and sat down in a hornets' nest to see if I couldn't get over the effects of the cure." "ALL bosh," said Smithers, "about this wire- less telegraphy," as he threw down tho evening paper. "I don't quite understand it myself,' said Smilhers' wife. " Why, I tell you it isn't practical. They propose to send messages on air waves. Now, weppoBing the wind doesn't happen to blow. Without waves the thing won't work. All bosh AY a school inspection some of the boys found a difficulty in the correct placing of the letters "i" and "e" in such words as "believe," "receive," etc. When the inspector said blandly: "My boys, I will give you an infallible rule, one I in- variably use myself," the pupils were all attention, end evep the master pricked up his ears. The in- spector continued : "It is simply, this: Write the i' and 'e ' exactly alike, and put the dot in the middle over them."</p>
