      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-18_WidnesExaminer_art0008" n="art0008">
        <head>LADIES' LETTER.</head>
        <p>LADIES' LETTER.</p>
        <p>MY DEAR NELLIE,</p>
        <p>The toque is always with us, perhaps because ot Its usefulness and its becomingneas to almost every face. Its style is practically always the same, though varying in size. This year it is large and turban-like, and made of velvet or xibeline cloth swathed in soft folds tilted up a little on the left side. A charming little toque was of castor brown drap de feutre, folded and twisted to suit the picluant face of the wearer, with two "donkey's ears" in felt, painted with mauve orchids, and knotted with a twist of shade mauve panne, as sole ornamentation. Another pretty toque was in silver grey drap de feutre, the edge scalloped out like leaves, each scallop lined with pale blue velvet, and resting on a wreath of ivy leaves and berries, the crumpled crown being caught in the middle with a very large oblong enamelled buckle. Large antique buckles are used very considerably in mil- finery this autumn, and a novelty is dull steel and jet mixed together, which look particularly well with black bats. All black bats, by the way, con- tinue to enjoy the same popularity that they have had all through the summer, made in black velvet silky beaver, or black satin, and trimmed with enormous ostrich feathers. A novelty in trimming, which looks especially well in black, is a sort of thick meshed net, between two thicknesses of which are laid strands of chenille; this arranged on the soft beavers and draps de feutre of which so many of the hats are now made looks very striking, and promises to become exceedingly popular.</p>
        <p>Cloth coats lined with fur are to be much worn. They are lull three•quarter length or more, and the newest model has a removable shoulder.cape of the unlined cloth. The favourite colours for these coats are smoke-blue, fawn, iron•grey, and tan, and they are fastened with big horn buttons, plain or ornamented. To wear with untrimmed cloth coats a great variety of collarettes and pelerines is being provided. One of the most fashionable is a tippet of sable or marten, pointed at the back, but with long square stole-ends in front that are finished with many tails below the level of the knee. A large square buckle is placed at the waist-line in front, this being usually either steel or cut jet, although enamelled buckles of a more fancifrl type ore sometimef! seen on ermine peleriuer.</p>
        <p>The admixture of lace and fur does not seem so general this season, but a pretty idea, though an extravagant one, is the conjunction of white pleated tulle and white fox. This makes a most dainty boa, having, at intervals down the long stole ends, rosettes of black comet ribbon. With an all black gown, or a white cloth dress the effect is very smart, and where smoke and log cannot work havoc, this may be a detail of the toilette well worth considering. When the weather is warm a person may be comfortable with just a light shoulder covering. Quaint taffetas or velvet pelerines are being ievived, or shoped bands of silk, gsuged en either side, and bordered with gathered velvet frills, forming a ruffle round the neck, and continuing down the front and all round the outer edge, just covering the shoulders. These pelerines reach to a few inches below the waist in front.</p>
        <p>The evening cloaks which are at present worn follow the same fashion . tytose for day wear, the majority being with nape backs. Ermine promises to be the fashionable fur for i•ach coats, worn as broad stole revers down the fronts on either side, and as deep cape collars on the shoulders. These capes have replaced the high collars ot former days, though there are a few people who prefer thent on account of their cosy warmth, but with the low style of hair dressing they are very uncomfortable and -make the hair untidy. A most lovely opera coat was in a rich violet shade of taffetas with deep flounce round the feet, fully gauged at the tap. A deep ermine collar fell over the shoulders, slashed up into revers to show an under-collar of mauve chiffon pliss4, bordered with a ruching of vieux-rose rose- leaves. The ermine was continued down the front, the inside edge of the coat being lined with a aaa• cede frilling of the chiffon. Your loving JENNIZ</p>
        <p>OLD FALSE TEETH BOUGHT.—Many people have old or disused false treth. Send your teeth to R D and J Fraser. Ltd., Princes-street. Ipswich (este). 1833), and they Wilt rsmlt the utmost value by return or make you best offer. Largest and oldest bu--- of old false teeth in the world,</p>
        <p>I AR( 1.1 au_ ITALIAN wood, ornamented; 121. 6d. SONS. ST. GEORGES' CRESCENT. TOP TREET. u.IVIEPOOL. Established 1848.</p>
