      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-18_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAI.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAI.</p>
        <p>London has scarcely proved so cheery of late, as those concerned could ish. The fogs and mists, arriv- ing somewhat before their time, came, after the glorious month of September, almcst as a shock, and the conse- quent gulping down of smoke and smuts has, by the majority, been resented. Then _conversation generally speaking, has been gloomy and depressing to a degree. Smallpox, scarlet fever, diphtheria and the merits and demerits of vaccination, are not, to speak mildly, lively topics, yet these are almost the only ones that have claimed any interest lately. There are however signs of improvement in this direction, and doubtless era long the early winter season, so much less formal, yet to many so much more enjoyatle, than the season proper, will be in full swing. 'I he Marquis of Bute will not come of age until next yeas, but he has already been received in audience by ths King. At one period there was more than a coolness between the Royal Family and the Crichton Stuarts, owing to the death of Lady Flora Hastings, which sad event occurred at Court in the early days of Queen Victoria's reign. This was in the Elms of the grandparents of the present Marquis, and so bitter was the feeling that when the Queen visited Cardiff, the blinds were drawn at Cardiff Castle. All this ill-feeling however has long since passed away, and though the youthful Marquis will not have attained his j majority at the time,there is no doubt be will be presentl at the Coronation next year. The Earl Marshal bas appointed special secretaries to deal with the arrangements for the Coronation, and the Court dressmakers have suggested that one of their number should be consulted upon the orders to be issued for feminine attire. It is no doubt easy for the Duke of Morfrlk to issue such general order, as that trains are to be of a certain length, that rotes are to be of crimson velvet, trimmed with ermine or miaiver,but it will be impossible to appear in a long trained robe over a full Court dress, and unlees these other matters are explained in proper detail, they are likely to lead to a good deal of contusion. It will also be impossible to wear a diamond tiara, with a coronet ornamented with cnly silver balls, or gold strawberry leaves. No doubt the order for the ex - elusion of jewels, emanates from the Throne, but it has disappointed the jewellers, as it will discourage orders for iresh gems or resetting. The cost of a coronet in silver gilt is from £lO to £2o—in gold it ranges from *l'Bo to £lOO. A few in already happy possession of this all important item, will simply have the ezpense of the " doing up" process, but this, in the instance of the silver-gilt variety, is likely to prove almost as costly a proceeding, as tbs building of a new one. Some surprise has been expressed that mitiver ehould have been raised to the dignity eta Royal fur, by being sanctioned in the (seders for the Coronation rubes. Ermine has alwas s been one of the furs used in blazonry, and when displayed by Royalty or judges, or magis- trates, it is supposed to be emblematical of purity. But it is always scarce, and it is just now, almost impossible to obtain, as it is being stored up for the Coronation. Hence no doubt the falling back upon miniver as the next best resort, but which however is quite an inferior fur, derived from the rabbit or squirrel. The rational dress reformers will rejoice over the agitation abroao against the trailing skirt. In come towns in Germany no woman with a skirt reaching to the ground is permitted to enter the public gardens, whilst in some parts of Switzerland it has been made a misdemeanour to wear a trailing Out. The movement has also ez'ended to America and in several towns the authorities are beginning to interfere in these matters. At Bayonne in New Jersey, the lccal Board if Health have asked for powers to make the lengthy skirt an cffelce punishable with a fine. There is hardly any greater cause of:contagion they say,than "these iniquitous dresses with long trains, %bleb gather up the germs of every descrig- tint), carry them into the household and breed disease." Probably tbe time may come when Parliament will decide what we racy, or may not wear, but, se far, all ' the attempts to frighten women into short gowcs have made little or no progress in this country. Those of us who have put off the re-modelling of furs, on the score of the modes not seeming settled, should no longer delay. Already the furriers are exceedingly busy, and unable therefore to give that attention to di tail obtainable earls? in the season, moreover ideas as to the correct styles for the comirg winter are now quite fixed. Tippets of the old world variety, coming well on the shoulders, with long stole ends, &amp;awe: together by means of a flat fur bow, will be the prevail- ing shoulder wrap, and this with a little addition can easily be evolved from the one time fashionable, tightly- fitting cape, that reached almcst to the waist, back and front%</p>
        <p>_ Or such a cape can be uthised in yet another way by letting in small gores, thus rendering it fuller at the edge and adding a wide slightly gored, flounce of fur. e Hi:collars, except on cvenirg wraps are happily no longer seen, in this direclion we have learned wisdom. Regarding muffs, these promise to assume gigantic pro- portior s and are puckered at the ends' like a bag. Dainty Haines, in all the most approved colours, are being shown in leading shops, ready to slip into the muff. thus it will be an easy matter to complete any scheme of colour our wardrobes may contain. The flower of the moment is tho dahlia,not the heavy double variety, beloved of the average gardener and— earwigs ; and again not the single dahlia so greatly preferred by the artistic among us ; but a forced " cul- tivated "blossom, which comes out a curious blend of , the two varieties long familiar to us. Very pale pink is the favourite colour in .these new blooms, among table decorators when daylight functions are in question and one can scarcely wonder that this should be so, seeing that this particular shade lends itself so well to many an exquisite, scheme. But with artificial light the very pale pink blossom-, merge almost into white, of a somewhat grubby hue and are therefore discarded in favour of deep red, or mauve, the menu, and name cards being ornamented with hard painted dahlias of corresponding tint, the lamps or candle shades taking of course, the same bue.</p>
        <p>Miss Rosa N. Carey 's new work " Bert) of Grace" (Macmillan) for which we have waited with so keen an interest, proves to be as charming, bright, and healthy reading, as those with which she has delighted us in the past. Once again from cover to cover this favourite and distinguished novelist holds cur closest attention, and as usual, leaves us fseling the better for it. Mine Carey's first serious attempt in fiction was, as is well known, " Nellie's Memories," and among otiv.r of her books, with which the majority of us are so familiar, may be mentioned "The Old Old Story," " Robert Ord's Atonement," "Heriot's Choice," and "Not Like Other Girls." Possibly few books have helped women in the streggle of life, as this last named work had un- doubtedly done. . _ . _ _</p>
        <p>The cult of the motor and blcycle has brought a large number of new customers to our country wayside inns, where one cannot help remarking there is often room for Improvement. To the generality of women, Who are fond of country cycling, refreshment does not mean alcoholic liquor, sod meat feminine votaries of the wheel are aware of the difficulty that is often experi- enced in obtaining anything else. Those who have been wider afield on their cycling tours cannot fail to have noticed bow much bettcr these things are ar- ranged—say in France—where in the most out of the way places it is porsible ta obtain a well served meal such as over here we should only look for at large hotels, and at ansiderably higher cost,</p>
        <p>MADGE.</p>
      </div>
