      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-18_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>EXAMINER, FRIDAY, OCTOBER 18 1901</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, OCTOBER 18 1901</p>
        <p>(Copyrig7i4.)</p>
        <p>A • QUEEN'S SOLDIER.</p>
        <p>By WALTER WOOD,</p>
        <p>Author of "Barrack and Battlefield," "Famous British Warships," "Through Battle to Pro- motion," "A Captain in the Fusiliers," "A Soldier of the Legion," &amp;c.</p>
        <p>SYNOPSIS OF PRECEDING CHAPTERS: Mark Beim, the son of a substantial farmer in Yorkshire, volunteers for service in the Yeomanry, about to be despatched, to South Africa.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-18_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>CHAPTER 111.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER 111.</p>
        <p>THE GOING OF THE TRANSPORT.</p>
        <p>A month had passed, and Trooper Bean bad said farewell to all his people at the "Green." ' He was glad when he had turned his back on the farm and the weeping women there; for, in spite of the tradi- tion of the Benns that evil things were bad enough in themselves without making them worse by shedding tears over them, the mother and sister had given way at last on seeing the khaki-clad form stride over the gravel. Some unknown fear possessed them, some awful dread of what the future had in store. The make-believe of soldiering had passed with the putting aside of the gorgeous trappings of the "noodle "—the brass helmet with its waving plume and the scarlet tunic; and the stern reality had come With this strange uniform of khaki and the wide sloucllP hat. Almost uncon- sciously they likened Mark to a man who had cast his Sunday clothes away and had put on garments for sombre, ceaseless work. In that short month Mark had been through his preliminary training, and, with his friend Brigg, .had come home to say "Good-bye." On the morrow he and his comrades were to go to London and sail thence to the Cape. The excitement and anticipation of the voyage were some recompense and relief for the misery which his partings had caused, and he was glad that Brir would be at the little station to return with him to the temporary headquarters in South Yorkshire. He was very grateful that Liz had borne herself so well, and puzzled, too, more particularly as her fathgr had been, in an unaccountable fashion, almost cheerful over the departure, and had assured Bean that he would see Liz a good deal sooner, very likely, than he fancied. Beyond that he refused to commit himself.</p>
        <p>"Keep your pecker up, Mark, lad? keep your pecker up," he said, encouragingly, as with moist eyes he shook hands. "There's one good thing about this war—they'll keep all the Yeomanry well back, they'll never send them for'ard -till all the proper soldiers get done for, and that'll be a long time yet." -</p>
        <p>"If I thought that, if I believed it was a mere sham for us, I wouldn't go even now," said Mark. "You'll never believe, I suppose, that I've really fought, even if I do fight, unless I come back on crutches ? "</p>
        <p>"Well, it would be some sort of proof, wouldn't it?" said Jackson. "That's the way Toni Merry, the Lifeguardsrnan, came back from the Crimea. He was a boy, was Tom. And didn't they make a fuss of him, too But .it sp,oilt him; he never did a day's work after that. Drink did for him what the Russians had left undone. But what we did for Tom Merry's nothing to what we'll do foz you, Mark. Go, and come back quick and safe. lf I were younger I'd like to go to South Africa too. I once went part way, as far as London: although it does seem queer for farmers to go fighting farmers instead of soldiers being set at soldiers, as they're paid to do." He waved his hand as a joint adieu an)fl benedic- • tion, and watched Mark go down the quiet lane which led to the station. It surprised and puzzled him to see how soon, in his strange uniform, Mark was lost to sight, and how difficult it 'was to dis- tinguish him from the dusty road in which he walked. • On one point Mark was feeling sore—he had not seen his father to bid him good-bye, and he took it that the old man's hostility was to endure to the bitter end. But even while he was thinking in this harsh strain a hand was laid upon his shoulder, and he heard the farmer's voice. It was somewhat hard, but the trooper was quick to see that it was only so because of the old man's resolve not to allow himself to be overcome by such a separation as this. "I've just come up to say good-bye, Mark, an' that I sent Prince on to London yesterday. I've ' settled it all. They'd have liked to buy him, but I said I'd make Government a present of him provided they'd keep him just for you. He'll be ' a bit of company." Words failed the trooper. He grasped, his • father's hand long and hard. "There's Brigg coming. I'll go," said the farmer. "Well, good-day, lad. Remember this: Whatever happens, be a Bean." The farmer drew his hand away and left his son. Mark had not said so much as a single word, but the old man understood, and there was something of a smile of satisfaction on his rugged brown face , as he walked back sturdily to Hill Top Farm. They had not, and he was thankful for it, parted in bad blood. With a more cheerful heart, too, Mark, in the company of Brig, returned to the headquarters of his corps, and the nextiday left to embark at the Albert Dock in London. A small number of the Yeomanry only went to the dock to join the trans- port, the majority being ordered to sail from Southampton. Mark and his comrades reached the dock the day before the ship was timed to sail, and, like them, for they came without exception from *the inland towns and country places, was fascinated by what _ he saw. The sea and ships were almost things unknown to them, and as their military life was also freshl:and novel there was little time for them , tohidcwhetlhl they yegretfully ,upon the homes and people to w The Yeomen were the first of all the troops to reach the swhieira,e usayhired transport which was not yet quite ready for sea, And on board o which a swarm of men and women laboured. Minters, joiners, crew and women were at work inside and outside the steamer. On the horse-deck the concrete floor was littered with the implements of peace; on the troop-deck were jovial painters, plumbers, joiners, charwomen; none of them ' seeming to exert themselves, but all progressing. The spick and span deal tables and seats, jutting geometrically from the transport's sides, were tenantless; o helmet-room was 'bare; the armoury kneW not rifles; the guard-room was held by a painter, splashing his pigments generously, the cell was without a prisoner; the door of the officer's bathroom refused to yield to a pull • a cook sat meditating in the troop-galley, and the vast maw of the transport was receiving carcases of sheep and oxen, boxes of groceries and provisions in an almost endless procession, tons upon tons of things by way of' food which men and horses need when sailing on the business of a distant war. But within twenty-four hours there was no cook in meditation; the armoury and helmet,room were glutted; the guard-room and the troop-deck rumbled with the sound of voices; there was a smart mounted orderly of the Royal Horse Artillery riding to and fro; a preposterous policeman at the dock-gate who turned away people who had a right to enter, and admitted without question those who y, and doing it, too, with humanity which the obese gate- had no business; a stalwart Royal Reserve man, tried to imitate; there were gate- keeper neo ei pin intelligence tr emml ilggi ethanty ew epalonl Idihe ahevudemut rat heavy diagoons, whose time to go to the front had not yet come, and who looked with the unfavourable eye of the trained critic on the raw Yeomen; khaki-clad gunners who were to leave in the course aopf pareatniatley, ()l so—khaki 110 been hard to get, for one of them was wearing three shades in his putties, breeches and jacket—and Army Service Corps men and garrison artillerynfen. Bean marvelled at the number and variety of the troops and horses on board. That nearly a thousand officers and men and five hundred horses oould find a home on board for at least throe weeks, was to him, ignorant as he was of the sea and ships, a marvel, and in the contemplation of It, he forgot entirely for , the present both the Green from which he came and Hill Top Farm. It was due to his lifelong work in the fields and open air that he had a strange stifled feeling on passing; along the horse-deck, and deeper sympathy for the horses that were to be imprisoned in the narrow boxes than he had for dose who, like him- self, were to be crowded on the deck below, further from the air and sky. It seemed to Mark that here on board was that which was almost a complete small army itself; for there were cavalry, artillery, infantry, Army Medical ,Corps, Army Service Corps, shoeing smiths, civil surgeons, stretcher-bearers, 0.2 d other "details" in bewildering variety. Up and down the gangway stepped the soldiers, some of get a snatch of food in the adjacent sped, and some, who had just them on fatigue, some of them to detrained, filing on board with all that they renuired as fislitina men to take the field. Outside</p>
        <p>the gate and the high railing which ran along the side of the dock a few feet from the steamer's stern was apatient crowd, standing there until the last. Amongst them, here and there, were weeping women, rocking sweltering babies to and fro, and half-smotherino, the infants' wails. These were women married' off the strength, or not married at all, .who had come to see the fathers sailing for the war.</p>
        <p>Into this crowd there slid' at times, down one of the stern hawsers, a soldier in khaki, who, having received a farewell kiss, or, more often, a parting, gift of fruit or whisky, shinned up the rope agairil laboriously, and was triumphantly hauled on board by laughing comrades hanging over the taNrail to receive him.</p>
        <p>It was a busy, bustling time, but even when Mark had watched it all by the hour it was hard for him to believe that they were British soldiers actually on their way to war; that crowding over the steamer's rail were figures which were bidding an unconscious farewell ; that here were fellow's in the very pink of life's condition who would cross the seas and march up country, and would not return.</p>
        <p>It amazed him to see how some old soldiers took the embarkation—as a mere matter of business, a piece of work which ought to be gone through with as little emotion as a field-day. "Talk about a pack o"ounds let loose on a fox," he heard a corporal just home from India say, in deep disgust, "they aren't in it with the young uns who've been let loose in the shed to get some grub. They rushed the table, an' down the 'ole lot went." But a friendly veteran—a Royal Reserve man—excund the lads, claiming that some of them were amongst the best that ever broke bread, and behaved like angels in comparlson with the way in which men acted when he himself first stepped on board a transport. Mark felt rrrdieeful to the Reservist for his championship of the wild recruits, one or two of whom were hurried to the guard-room and the cell forthwith.</p>
        <p>Hour succeeded hour, still the soldiers crowded the deck and hung on in the rigging. There were pitiful snatches of song, and a mouth-organ thinly wailed a few bars of "Auld Lang Syne."A gunner reared a snatch of "Sweetheart May," and the chorus, disjointedly unmusical, rumbled over the deck. But it was at best a broken effort, for on the quays men and women were now waving and shouting their farewells. .Steam hissed from beneath the transport's counter, hawsers were dragged about, capstans clanked, troops crowded to the rail and swarmed up the rigging afresh, for at last a move was being made. So slowly that she scarcely seemed to move, the ship warped out into the middle of the dock, stern on to the lock and the Thames. On her left was A sister transport, making ready, away ahead were the black funneled P. and 0. boats, about her were peaceful steamers getting rid of all that they had brought from distant shores. There were little saps aboard, picking up hints as to the British way of getting soldiers off; and round and about were friends of those who went.</p>
        <p>It was three o'clock. The transport drew twenty- five feet aft, while there was a depth of seventeen only at the sill; but the troops did not know this, and wondered at the delay. For four hours more the ship lay motionless. Off and on, there had been drinking of beer, singing of snatches, waving of handkerchiefs, and shouts of "So-long" and "Good-bye." At times there were reports of guns from across the river, and panic cries were raised that the Boers had come to England, and were upon the neighbouring shore. But even that dis- play of humour was but moderately successful, and diolnot_cause more than a short and feeble laugh.</p>
        <p>Watchful eyes were kept upon the tide, and wlien at last the outer boom was drawn away, and the upper gates were opened, they knew that the end of the little shore drama was coming, and that the hired transport, by number 184, wbuld soon be upon the high seas, in the trooping steam-lane. The sun had been coy during the afternoon, but as the transport was hauled down the lock the clouds passed over, and there was a:great solemn red ball beyond the masts and smoke-stacks of the P. and 0. boats, There were last little friendly acts—a man rushed up with a - large glass bottle, half full of drink, and a line was made fast and the gift was hauled on board. It was one of many like it that had been hauled on deck since morning, and a few of the troops shewed that they had dOne more than justice to the liquor. But there was nothing to object to. The men were not posing as saints; and allowances were made for the occasion. Slowly, very slowly*, the transport went towards the Thames. The outer gates of the lock were opened, a little tug made fast to the giant's stern, and the vessel was drawn out to the river. Over her rail peered many faces—the faces of dare-devils, who did not care ; the faces of other men who in their secret soul did care, who smiled grimly; and there were other features full of sadness. Some of the me ri cheered hoarsely; others did not make a sound. These latter, Mark observed, were mostly wearers of the diced glengarry, for amongst the transport's details• was a draft of Highlanders. There were the Imperial Yeomen, smart, clean, grave, awed ; and near them a group of smiling officers. One—a Highlander—was smoking a cigarette, and looked as cheerful as if he were going on a pleasure trip. There were smiles and tears, hopes of a quick and safe return, and one or two farewells of exaggerated cheerfulness—amongst them that of an East Ender, who shouted: "Think o' me to-night pl'yin' the Dead Mawch 'for yer "—an effort with which he was so well pleased that he repeated it. The great hull had been drawn past the watchers on the dock, the bows were headed down the river, the tug churned the muddy water, and the four masts and single funnel, broadside on, with the painted number of the transport showing large, forward and aft, stood out in sharp relief against the sky; the Admiralty flag astern was blown out by the breeze, there was a last long cheer from ship and shore, a parting wave of hat and handkerchief, and the deep roar. of the transpdrt's siren as she went towards the sea. Other sirens took the signal up, and the ship began her journey South as people rushed away to struggle for a seat in the dirty train that stood at the platform of the dismal station which they call the Gallions. All these things had been noticed in a slow, half-comprehending way by Mark; they had been noticed, too, by another Benn who had been watching her for all the hours the transport had I,2ean making ready finally, had been lying idly in the dock awaiting the tide, and had been slowly getting to the river. From nine o'clock in the tlorning until seven at night, when the transport vanished in a thin mist which was coining up the Thames, Farmer Bonn had looked on all that passed. But he kept himself out of sight, mostly on the little wooden bridge that crosses the railway, and did not make his presence known to Mark; nor did his son see him. _</p>
        <p>It was the old man's way of seeing his trooper off to the war, and as he saw the last of the transport, he felt that the reconciliation was complete, and that he could wait in peace for Mark's return. Ile had made the journey to the capital in the night, without sleeping; throughout the long, hot day he had been standing near the transport; now, without thought of sleep or rest, he went to King's Cross Station, and travelled home again in the night. Not till after sunrise did he reach the farm ; then he ate his breakfast solemnly, ,and resumed charge of his haymaking. All that his wife could induce him to admit when he walked upstairs to bed after his long 'spell of wakefulness, travel and toil, was that he felt a bit drowsy. If Mark it the war could not resolutely go through the stern privations of campaigning, it was not because he did not come of an enduring race.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-18_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>CHAPTER IV.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER IV.</p>
        <p>FROM PEACE TO WAR. 7.Cooper Bean on board the transport made him- self at home and comfortable. There were amongst his comrades more than one who, now that the land was far behind, and all the excitement of training, equipment and despatching ended, would have seized the chance, if they had had it, to return to civil life. They had enlisted not so much because they wished for war, as that they had not been able to keep from following the lead of the more adventurous spirits. But they had started on a journey from which there was no return at will; they must fulfil their contract with the nation, and that contract bound them to servo for one year, or for the war. Only in the event of war being over in less than one year would the Imperial Yeoman, have the option of being discharged at once or of completing his one year s service. Remembering this, the waverers, within themselves, hoped that the war would have ended by the time they landed, or that the Yeomanry would be kept on the lines of com- munications. Already they had been undeceived as to the nature of the work which they had under- taken. In the excitement of enlistment and training it had seemed to them that the eyes of the country were upon them, that wherever they went they were looked upon as heroes, men who had given up all for patriotism's sake. There were the joys of public attention, notice in the streets; a farewell banquet, a civic good-bye, and a roar of obeerini</p>
        <p>from a vast crowd as the train steamed off to join the transport.</p>
        <p>But the grim awakening had come within the first twenty-four hours. There had been scant food and poor, and it was as hungry and weary men that the Imperial troopers got alongside the trans- port. It was discovered, too, when the ship had got to sea, that the medical arrangements on board were .defective and inadequate, and that sick soldiers were unable to receive the comforts and attention they required. Before Las Palmas was reached two had been buxied at sea, the victims of, pneumonia. It was when the second had been cast overboard that Benn heard one of the waverers say to another: "I wish to God I'd never started on this awful business."</p>
        <p>No such feelings prompted Troopers Benn and .9rigg, or the most of those who were on board. Mark marvelled that even here, amongst men equipped for war, and going out on its serious inissipn, there should be those who would not recognise the realness of their task. To sail and land, to see a strange country, to watch the regular troops get to the front to do the fighting, and for themselves to step in and maintain the positions which had been won by fire and steel, this was the utmost that smite of his companions hoped for and expected.</p>
        <p>"It's got to be drummed into some of 'em here, Jack, as it's got to be knocked into some of 'em at home," he said to Brigg, "that the thing isn't a sham in any way. I suppose they'll learn it some time."</p>
        <p>"r suppose so," answered 13rigg. "But I fancy some of 'em will need a lot of teaching." "As for me," said Bonn, "I don't see how we can expect to do the same in a ,place like this as we can at home. I suppose such things as these chaps dying can't be helped, any more than they can help the way the horses live. That upsets me more than the shifts we're put to ourselves."</p>
        <p>"I suppose not," replied Brigg, dubiously. "But, all the same, I don't reckon that it's a fair thing to starve us on the way to the ship, and then let us jump up if we happen to get poorly. They didn't say anything of risks like that when we joined, did they?" "No," Benn admitted. "I suppose it's like a chap who's got a horse to self—he'll leave you to find out the bad points yourself, if he can." "What strikes me," added Brigg, "is that if we're to get this sort of thing in England, and not far out of it, when we're supposed to be having the best of everything, it'll be,pretty lurid in South Africa. Eh, Mark?" "Well, Jack," said Benn, slowly, '"it's only rhat you can expect. You aren't sorry you :came, are you ?" he asked, with a quick look. "Sorry I By George, not" returned Brigg. "I'm only saying this to you by way of chat. wouldn't go back now at any,price till I've seen the business through." "No more would I," said Mark, quietly. "And I don't think there are six men on the ship who'd turn, either, even if they could get off now . and go back," asserted Brigg „_. . .</p>
        <p>"If it came to the point, I d'On't think there are two," said Mark. "Did you," he asked, changing the subject abruptly, "notice anything queer about old Jackson ? You saw him tie day'we came away, didn't you?" "Yes, I saw him, but there wasn't anything very queer, except he was cheerful." "He was in good spirits," agreed Mark. "It struck me there was something unusual about him." "He wasn't down in the mouth a bit," con. tinued Brigg. "He looked on the whole thing_as a bit of a trip at the expense of the country. The •ald Chap is positive, you know, that the Yeomanry will never see any real fighting.. He believes that we shall be kept well away Wm the front, guarding lines of communication, and that sort of thing. If he hadn't thought that, I'm sure he Wouldn't have been in such good spirits at the idea of your coming." "But that would hardly make him so cheerful," said Mark, puzzled even yet by the light-hearted way in which the old farmer had said good-bye. "I don't know what was the matter. He talked so strange, too. There was something in it I didn't understand." _ . _</p>
        <p>751, T; said Brigg. "Didn't he-tell'yon ?" I "Tell what?" demanded Mark. "What was there to tell ?"• _</p>
        <p>I "You're going:to marry Liz, aren't you?" inquired Brigg.. "Yes, of course. What of that ?"_</p>
        <p>"Wasn't it coming off pretty soon ? " "At Christmas," answered Mark. "That was the arrangement. Of course, it'll be a bit later 'no, although I needn't • wait long when I get back. Everything'll be ready for it. You see, it's been an understood thing for years, ever since Liz and me were boy and girl." "Well, I hope it'll come off at Christmas," said Brigg, heartily, "and that I can be your best man. We'll do the thing properly, eh, Mark ? In khaki, you know, in our fighting kit. Why not? J. rather like the idea myself." Mark shook his head. "That's for fighting in, pot for getting wed," he answered. "It would look like shewing off, and I hate that, so do you." I "But you might get married in khaki away from home," said Brigg. "You know these, things do happen." "Not with chaps like me," said Benn quietly. "I've made a bargain, and I shall stick to' It." "Ah, but soldiers often come across a woman they fancy in the country where they're fighting, and they get married—and settled, too." There was a curious look on Brigg's face as he spoke. It was as if he might have been enjoying a joke which he relished all the more because Mark did not share in it, and therefore unconsciously improved it. t "As for me," asserted Mark, "I shall neither look at a woman nor marry one out there. I'm fixed up at home." "Don't you be so sure on that point," rejoined Brigg. "You evidently don't know what old Jackson meant when he said you might see Li 2 ooner than you thought." "I'll confess I don't," said Mark, "Do you?" I Brigg folded his arms and smiled quietly. He leant against the steamer's rail, near which they had been standing, and did not reply. "Come," said Mark, "what's the joke ? If it has anything to do with me and Liz and her father I think I ought to know it." ; "But either Liz, or her father, or the old woman who's Jackson's sister—queer old stick—should have told you," said Brig. "I can't. I promised rim I wouldn't. But I m surprised you didn't guess. I saw through it in a jiffey." "But you're so keen—l'm not," said Mark, in simple admiration of his friend's acuteness. Brigg was softened almost to the verge of revela- tion. Ile was on the point of telling all he knew when Mark added. "Of course, if you've given your word I'm not going to persuade you to go back on it." "Well, look here," knaid Brigg, "I'll meet you half way. If you don't hear or•understand by the time we get to the front, I'll tell you. Is that fair ?"</p>
        <p>--- - "Yes, perfectly," answered Mark. "That'll keep me from worrying you if I felt disposed to bother." . . '</p>
        <p>Whatever the secret was Brigg •ivas not asked to reveal it. From that day until the transport reached Capetown no further words were spoken concerning either Liz or her father; although Bonn might have noticed, if he had been somewhat sharper, that his comrade always eagerly took the opportunity of, asking questions about, or hearing details of, the sister whom Mark had left at home. It did not, however, occur to the young fanner that Brig could by any possibility be in love with Janet, his sister. Certainly Brigg was not a frequent visitor at Hill Top Farm, and it had been always • understood that as he was such a sharp xoung man in commerce—his father was the head 1/ a profitable little brewery, and he was his i?-iher's chief losistant—he would, perhaps, look a n'lnde higher than Janet when he was searching a wife. But in the joy of reaching port and the hurry and bustle of landing and getting the horses ashore Mark forgot the Green, the farms and the people there; and Brigg thought only of his duty as a soldier and of what the country had in store for them. So far they were concerned only to know that they had actually passed from peace to war. They did not even know to which part of the country they were to be ordered or what tluor real task was to be.</p>
        <p>The transport was made fast astern of one of the mail boats which had left England five days later, MA had been berthed two hohrs earlier. Some of her passengers were still on board, watching the disembarkation of the Yeomanry 'and details. , For the present the Yeomen, having filed ashore, were drawn up alongside the transport, towards the bows, and near the stern of the mail-boat from hich the passengers were watching. Brigg was adjuiting his ammunition bandolier, and in doing so had turned his head towards the mail-boat. Benn, impassive at his side, was looking at the transport, trying to picture the delight of the horses when they were on land again. In the act of tying a strap Brig.. looked quickly tin at the passengers, conscious that a handkerchief</p>
        <p>was being waved violently to gain the notice or bitaself or someone near him. He looked, and saw that the signal was made by a woman—young and attractive, too—dressed as &amp;- aurae. He lowered his gaze and went on with his task. Apparently he was satisfied that the signal was not meant for him. Certainly he did not remember that he had any nurse amongst his friends or acquaintances. Then he raised his head again, this time with a • jerk. He had distinctly heard a well-remembered voice cry "Mark!" As he looked a broad smile of joyous recognition appeared upon his face. He lifted his hat and waved it vigorously in reply. At the same time he violently nudged tha unconscious Benn. _</p>
        <p>"Look, Mark!" he cried, and his comrade turned round. _ _ _</p>
        <p>"Why—bless my soul!" exclaimed Benn. He was not easily disturbed, but he looked startled now.</p>
        <p>"I must be dreaming," he added, 'rubbing his eyes. Again the handkerchief was waved, and a voice, an unsteady voice, cried "Mark!" "There's no dreaming, it's her, right enough," Brigg assured him heartily. "Do you see now what the old man was driving at ? " "Liz here," said Mark, almost stupidly. "Liz actually come out—and like that, too." "Why, yes, it's plain enough," Brigg assured him. "I was let into the secret a bit, not all,. but I didn't expect her so soon. But don't you see how she's calling for you? Aren't you going? If you don't there's some other fellow will think she means him, and go instead." "Of course I'm going," said Mark. "Here, hold my rifle till 1 come back. It isn't fifty yard* away." He handed his weapon to Brigg—they were both in the front rank—and stepped forward. He had taken several paces up towards the mail-boat when his Captain spied him. "Hullo, there 1" he cried. "You, Benn ; fall in. You can't do that sort of thing now, you know." _ _</p>
        <p>Three months before Mark would have refused to notice such an order. He halted instinctively, remembered that he was a mere Yeoman no longer, but a Queen's soldier, flushed a little with pique and disappointment, then returned stolidly to the ranks and took his rifle from the hand of Brim. The Captain had spoken truly. He could not, do this sort of thing now. (To be continued.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-18_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>"TEMPERANCE" HUSSAR.</head>
        <p>THE "TEMPERANCE" HUSSAR.</p>
        <p>Strange Experiences. "The Army is not bad, if you look after your money when you get it, and keep steady," said a trooper in the 18th Hussars. "SpEaking for myself, I'm a tee- totaler !"</p>
        <p>There was a stir, as the smart well-knit young man looked round the room and delivered himself of this remark. "You mustn't think, however," be went on,. "that teetotalism will do everything for a man. As a matter of tact, I am nominally on the sick list at preseat." "You don't lock like it," said his friend. " I should not have thought you troubled the doctors much." "Perhaps not now," said Herbert Pickering (for that Is the speaker's name). "but I have been ill since Christ- mas, with a bad attack of gastric catarrh. I was for three weeks in the hospital attended by the regimental doctor, and while I was at home at 5, Luton Grove. Liverpool, two other doctors treated me far my com- plaint, but I could not detect even a elipht improve. relent, nor did I feel any better or stronger." " What were your symptoms, then ? " I had violent pains in the stomach after partaking of food, and these were generally followed by nausea and vomiting. In fact, so painful and disagreeable were the symptoms thata often did not want to eat at all. The food which I ate did not do me any good, and my general health. in consequence became very much run down. I was getting very down.hearted,when a friend of mine recommended me to try Dr. Williams' pink pills for pale people. He told me the pills had cured him. and as I eeemed likely to continue in my then exhausted state of healtb,l decided to take my friend's advice."</p>
        <p>" What was the result ? " " The first box of Dr. Williams' pink pills did not seem to have much effect ; but I did not give them up,. and after the second and third boxes I began to feel much better. I have taken the contents of seven boxes altogether, and, no doubt about it, they have done me a world of good.' " And you feel all right now ?" " Pirst-olase. thank you. In fact, I am going tcl rejoin my regiment." The manly straightforwsrdneii of this statement carries conviction in every word ; and indeed Mr Pickering's experience is that of thousands who have given similar evidence. Lack of power to digest and extract nourishment from food taken was the miserable state of thousands of haggard men and women whose whole lives were embittered by perpetual gnawing pain and a general disturbance of the nervous and digestive systems, yet who are now whole and sound, through the samo means which made • new man of Mr Picketing. The stomach requires " tore," or strength to digest food, and this very strength is supplied by Dr Williams' pink pills for pale people, which enrich the blood in a manner unequalled by any other medicine. These pills are world-famous through their wonderful success in all disorders arising from imnoverished blood arremia, rickets, scrofula, "decline," aonsumption, indigestion, palpitations, rheumatism, sciatica, St Vitus' dance, paralysis, locomotor ataxy, neuralgia,and all disturbances of the nervous system. The price is two shillings and ninepencs for one box, or for six bmtme thirteen and nine,sent direct by the manufacturers, Dr Williams' medicine company, Holborn-viaduct, London, or obtainable of medicine dealers. Unfortunately many intending purchasers are deceived into accepting worth- less eubstltutee, persistently offered by tradesmen bent on large profits, but the true virtues of the genuine pills have never been imitated, and customers may find safety from deception by mentioning the name in full Dr Williams' pink pills for pale people, and by noticing that name on the packet handed to them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-18_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>STRUGGLE IN A POLICE CELL.</head>
        <p>STRUGGLE IN A POLICE CELL.</p>
        <p>Penketh Man Injured.</p>
        <p>At the Widnes Police Court on Monday, Joseph Mitchelrnore, of no settled abode, was charged on remand with wounding, with intent to do grevioue bodily harm, one Richard Kenwright, Pentreth, on the 12th Mat, in a cell at the Widnes Police Station, Ken- wright being at that time a prisoner on the charge of drunkenness. Mitchelincre had at the time of the assault been arrested on a warrantHpharged with neg- lecting his wife and two children so that they had been chargeable to the Prescot Union since June 29th, 1899. Kenwright was firat of all formally charged with drunkennes and dismissed in consideration of the fact that he had been confined sic ce the night of the 11 h inst. Superintendent Keighley. stated that at 12 15 a.m. On Thursday, the 12th inst., the prisoner Mitchelmore, was placed in the same cell as Kenwright, the latter being at that time quite sober and was lying down covered with the three rugs provided to each prisoner. Mitchel- more was provided with rugs, but when locked in he sought also to take those of Kenwright, and an alterca- tion tcok place in which Blitchelmore struck Kenwright very violently on the bead with a tin can provided for drinking purposes, and also otherwiae injured him. The result of the blow was that Kenwright's head was severely cut and Dr. Creighton Hutchinson Lindsay bad to be summoned. The prisoner was charged with intent to do bodily harm, but if the Bench desired it could be/ \ reduced to one of common assault in order that the ease' . might he dealt with in that court.</p>
        <p>The Bench agreed to the suggestion' and evidence bearing out the above statement was given. Dr. D. Hutchinson Lindatav said be was called o'oo one o'c'ock on:the morning, of the 12th inet.and examined Kenwright's head. H•fcuud a wound about 3i inches long penetrating to the skull and a %valid on the bidic of the right wrist about 1i inches long. Keawright wag now out of danger and doing we 1.</p>
        <p>- The Mayor: 'Did you consider him to be in danger after the injuries ?—Witness ; Yes. The charge of wife and family neglect againstMitcbel- more was then investigated. Mr. Rant!, relieving officer, stated that the prisoner forrnsily lodged with his family at Widnes. In June. 1899, he left Widnes and got employment at Liverponi. For a short time, he sent his wife money, but soon after: • wards he discontinded doing so. The same raOtirP (June) his wife applied for relief, stating that sho vra6 destitute. He found this to be so, and, in, conscquernr•• heetent her with her children to the Woikhouse. Tbe.YE cost the Guardians £7B ss. The prisoner had been °- sea, and had travelled all over the world. were Two previous convictions against the prisoner mentioried. t t The Mayor announced' that the prisoner wouldtnt.- be e santenced to two months' bard labour on the first charfr, and for the second diet:ice, which did not seem to ba...7 a single redeeming feature, to the maximum peeeltV three months' hard labour, the sentences to run CO' iecutively.</p>
        <p>The Blyth police report that the skeleton of a ,111,11,0 , has been unearthed on the links there during moval of sand. 'fled A " conscientious objector." at Stowe) baying deck to pay the exemption lee Was on Wednesday orderedT. have his child vaccinated within three months'. Tbe f bS ending solicitor mentioned tbat tba drciaion would appealed against, on the ground that no fee vms srue.ads tioned in the Act of Parliament, and that Mr Long no right to impose one.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-18_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <p>. ~ • ',,..' ..,..' .. .. - . . 1,, • ... :. , ',- . , ' . . :... ~. ~, • , . . . , ~ . . . „. .-.•-,, ~ ~.• , , .. , . . , r: .7 - i ....... , •:. , , ... ' ' '.- - -, ' ,',.• ','• , i .. „ ~ . 3 ' 4. . . . , : .IL . .4 . ~ f, 4 , , , I/ , . ~ • , • .;'• --,. ••,- ~ .:.''.-• . • 1., . , ~1,,, , • ~, .. ..f • , .: •1 .. i... • -1 !. . -;.. .. . . '• '4,1 .. . • ' t ...-' ' • . r . • I , 1 . 0 r• ' , ... '.• .... . ' i '' i • , .... *, . • 1 : 1 . , . . 1 '4'41:4..1 . " ~• : .+, ... • ..F ‘,l</p>
