      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-18_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>PLUCKY WOMAN. Remarkable Case in Liverpool.</head>
        <p>A PLUCKY WOMAN. Remarkable Case in Liverpool.</p>
        <p>I striking instance of what a plucky woman can do I •in an uphill fight with difficulties has come to light in Windsor-street, Liverpool. Mrs. M'Culloch, who lives at 'No 19, in that street was lett a widow sometime ago, witheight children to take care of, and, untortunately er her, her husband when he died was not insured, making her lot harder than it would otherwise have been. Mrs M'Culloch put up such a splendid fight against adverse conditions that her case is considered a surprising one by the neighbours, and an excellent example to others placed amongst great difficulties. A Liverpool reporter obtained some inside light on Mrs MtCulloch's rather pathetic case, She is fifty-sevsn years old, and is assisted in the work cf keeping a laundry by he daughters. Being of a frank and cheery disposition, with a kindly smile and a shrewd, pleasant countenance, Mrs M'Cul- loch readily responded to the reporter's suggestion that she should tell her story in her own way. " I lost my husband four years ago," she said, "and a sad loss it was to me. I did not know what worry was until that time, because my husband always kept the house well. But he never would insure his life, and always said to me. ' Oh, you will be all right.' as he was not going to die just yet. Well, he became ill, and with nursing him for six months my health utterly broke down when he died. " I have eight children living, and several of them being at home I had to look about for some means whereby I could keep the home together. That is how I came to start this laundry, which is hard work, but my daughters assist me with it." " What did you suffer from. Mrs M'Culloch ? " asked the reporter. "Indigestion and lo‘s of appetite, general weariness, dizziness, and a. disinclination to do anything, I felt so weak and ill. Indeed, I never expected to live to see this day, after what I have suffered. Often in going upstairs I have had to sit down, I was so weary, and • have frequently had to lie down for hours at a time, I felt 40 weak and ill. Last summer I had to give up work altogether, and my daughter carried on the laun- dry in absence 4 but, of cow so, I could not help thinking what would become of them all if anything happened to ma. At that time I did not think I would ever be cured,ai I was daily becoming weaker. Last year I made up my mind to go on a visit to Ireland, as I thought it would do me good, but after dressing and having a cab at the door I was es ill that I dared not go away, as I was afraid I would never come back alive. There was a curious nervous feeling on me, so that I really could not go away. After that I became worse." " Did you never try anything for your illness ?" " Oh, yes I had been under several doctors for a long time, but did not seem to get any better. And I have taken hundreds of pills of all kinds, until I was sick and weary with the sisht of them, and none of them did me good or made me feel stronger, At last I was induced to try Dr Williams' pink pills for pale people, and I bless that day from the bottom of my heart. They have certainly made a new woman of me. I never thought it possible that anything would do me so much good as Pr Williams' pink pills have done. lam now as well as I ever was in my life, my appetite is good, and I enjoy my food. Work is no longer a burden to me, but a pleasure, and even after a hard day in the lanndry I do not feel nearly so tired as many a younger woman. I attribute it all to Dr Williams' pink pills" "The nervous feeling left me; the Pains in my Vssmach disappeared, and I was able to take my food without suffering those distressing pains, and what is more, I obtain nourishment now from my food." " How many boxes have you taken altogether ? " "Five boxes so far, and lam still taking them. I would not be without them for anything, and am going to continue taking them some time longer." The pills are praised by all class of people, from the lowest to the highest in Lancashire, for •the way in which they have cured paralysis, 'locomotor ataxy, rheumatism, and sciatica; also all diseases arising from impoverishment of the blood, scrofula, rickets, and chronic erysipelas, con- sumption of the bowels and lungs, amnia, pale and sallow comnlexion, general muscular weakness, loss of appetite, palpitations, pains in the back, nervous head- ache, and neuralgia, early decay, all forms of female weaknesses and hysteria. These pills ars a tonic not a purgative. They are genuine only with the full name —Dr Williams' pink pills for pale people—and are sold by chemists, and by Dr Williams medicine company, 46 Holbcrn viaduct, Lor dan, E C., at 2s 9a a box, or six boxes for 13s 9i. Pink pills sold loose or from glass jars are not Dr Williams' pink pills ; accent them only in the pink closed wrapper as above described.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-18_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>SOLDIER'S LAST HOURS.</head>
        <p>A SOLDIER'S LAST HOURS.</p>
        <p>Say, are you dreaming of me, love, As the dark hours of night slowly glide ? Are you dreaming that lam now with you ? Ai s you thinking that I'm by your side ? In ro,•aee you are eleepirg thia night, love, While I on the battle-Wd lir; 77- In fancy I hear your voice calling, As I lay my head down, love, to die,</p>
        <p>While around me the forms of my comrades, In death's last embrace near me sleep ; How slf4wly the time seems to pass, love, Which once seemed so q lickly to leap. I fancy I see yonr bright form, love.. Which, alas ! I shall ne.erive again ; And the tears fill my eyes, love, this minute, As I lie on this hard bed of pain. I fancy I sea my dear home, I,ve, So far o'er the deep mighty sea ; I see the dear face of my mother, And I know she is thinking of me. I hear the deco boom of the cannon— Which alone breaks the stillness of night— Like thunder it rolls o'er the plains, love : How I yearn f)1. the sweet morning light ! It tells me of comrades now falling, Like mine their last baths i 3 o'er ; God grant that their soul may find rest, I've, Where sorrow and pain is no more. The dark clouds of night seem to vanish, The skies are illumined with light-- 'rig the light, love, of Heaven, I know it, Oh, God! what a beauteous sight ! I am dying, farewell, I am dying ; I see that great throne, love, on high ; And the beauteous form of God's angels Deszend from their homes in the sky. Oh ! comfort my heart-broken mother,. And give her one kiss, love, for me ; Farewell, we shall meet once again, love, And from pain and from serrow be free. ARTHUR 3 HAYES.</p>
        <p>Channl-qtreAt. Suthnrt, Ot-ter 13th, 1901.</p>
        <p>',MICR: Is THE :BEST: CYCLE? WRY</p>
