      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-18_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>RUGBY FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>RUGBY FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>Brighouse Rangers v. Runcorn. This match was played at Brighouse on Mmday on the bane head enclosure* The visitors played up the sl me, but with the wind in their favour, in the first half. The Rangers were prominent in an attacking movement first, Baker making a good run. woile Saville after a smart passing bout, was brought down close to the line by Houghton. Other movements of a fluctuat- ing type were followed by Houghton being pressed back over his own goal line. From the resulting scrummage Jolley attempted a foolish punt, and the ball being charged, Miller scored a try for the home side, from which Saville kicked a clever goal. This point was equalized three minutes later by the prettiest bit of football seen in the match. Jolley got the ball out 30 Warder, who tran-ferrei to Butterworth. The last-named sprinted up the field with Moran on his right, and Wallace was evidently waiting for the pass, but Butterworth trickily tloriv him off the scent, and doubling inside, completed a masterly effort by scoring a try between the posts, from which Farmer landed a goal. The lead was subse- quently taken by Runcorn, by means of a fair catch on , the part of Richardson. Farmer with a good shot placing a goal, The visitors' line had a narrow escape just before the interval, Houghton kicking the ball dead as , Balzer, who had done a smart piece of following-up, was about to fall upon It. At half-time the score was—Runcorn, two goals and one try (seven points), Brighouse Rangers one goal and one try (five points). Each slue had to play a man short in the second half. Wallace, the Brighouse custodian, could not resume owing to a bad kick on the elbow, while soon after the restart a kick on the leg placed Walker, one of the Runeorn -forwards, hors de combat for the rest of the match. The play in the s,cond period degenerated into a wearisome eXhibition of punt-and- catch Football. A. couple of Mart hits of work,however, increased the Runcorn score to subitantial proportions. In the one case. Butterworth, receiving the ball in front of the Brighouse post;, and finding his progress barred, dodged round and dropped a clever goal ; in the other Moran got a pass from Jolley, and though he knocked the ball forward at the first attempt, he secured it bc- fore it could fall, and running over scored a try, this being one of the instances in which the new role as to accidental knocks forward was brought into operation. The plac?-kick ray Farmer proved a failure. The final score was—Runcorn three goals and two tries (12 points), Brighouse Rangers one goal and one try (rive points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-18_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>Lancashire County Trial Match.</head>
        <p>Lancashire County Trial Match.</p>
        <p>Trial team.; selected from Lancashire club,: met at Wigan on Wednesday afternoon, several alteeittiona being necessitated. The sides were finally completed as follows : ....</p>
        <p>Probablea.—D Smith (Salford), back, S Williams (Oldham), R Wilson (Broughton Rangers) R J Messer (Swinton), and V 'lampoon (Swinton), three-quarter backs) ; A Lees (Oldham) and W James (Broughton Rangers). half-backs ; P runner (Salford). J Ferguson (Oldham), W Briers (St Helens), F Treweeke (Barrow), J R Hilton (Wigan), G Wilkinson (Oldham), J Preston (Swinton), and G Ruddick (Broughton Rangers), forwards. Possibles.—J Hallam (Warrington), back ; A Hogg Broughton Rangers), C Civil (O 'ham), D Isherwood (Warrington), and F Ganiey (Leigh), three-quarter backs; J Molyneux (Leigh) and J R Lawton (Ordharn) half backs ; I' Brown (Wigan), C Thompson (Brought m (Range). J CarneV (St Helens), H Ball (Wigan), F Lee (St Helens), J Norbury (Leigh),J fl Wilkinson (Oldham), and J Evans (Swinton), forwards. At the outset the Possibles put on a bold front and tried forcing tactics, but though S Williams was pre- vented in the rush from getting in a return kick, Wilson, the speedy Broughtonian, changed the venue. Hallam was given quite a warm time, and he proved his worthiness in no uncertain fashion. Owing mainly to his sturdy punting the Possible s were able to return to their opponents' quarters, where had Isherwood taken a by no means indifferent pass, he must have , scored . The mica was taken full advantage of by the ' little Salford back. Smith, sato sent the ball well into the opposite half. Hire Wilson got hold near the centre line and running splendidly, circum- vented Hallam and scored beneath the posts %V James added two points quite easily. But a few mir.utes had elapsed when James landed a second goal from a penalty kick. This wiry and wary little indi- vidual was soon again in the van, for be wriFrg,led over the Possibles' line and then made a fair attempt to land a'third goal, hut failed. Following an in incursion into the Probables' ground, Ferguson scored from a pass by Vernon Hampson, J.-mes adding the customary g al. A few minutes before the interval was signalled Moly- ' neux,scored a try and kicked a goal, so that the Prch- ' ables then led by 15 points to five.—ln traversing the second half of the journey the Probables again asserted their superiority, and Hampson, Tunney. and Messer scored tries:And James added two more goals. Hogg scored a try from a pass by Molyneux, but instead of keeping on right ahead he preferred to pass, and then got in where a goal Wok was difficult, and where as a fact, it failed. The try which lamp- son scored was the result of extremely smart dodging in and oat of a crowd of opponents. The end earns</p>
        <p>with the Probables winners by 28 points to 8.</p>
        <p>Tali COUNTY MANI.</p>
        <p>In selecting the --ai-;p-erTd-e-ci -t.e-arr-1,- the committee did not disappoint any ot the good judges who saw the play. Barring accidents these chosen players will repre- sent Lancashire : Smith (Salford), Fish (Warrington). Bob Valentine (Swinton), Whoa (Brouguton Rangers), and Williams (Oldham) ; Willie James (Broughton Rangers), and Davies (Swinton) ; Ferguson (Oldham). Tann -y (Salford), 'freweeke (Barrow), G Wilkinson (Oldham), Ruddick (Broughton Rangers), Briers (St. rielens), Pres- ton (Swinton), and Roberts (Leigh). Reserves : Thomas (Olduam), Williams (Salford), llogg (Broughton Rangers), Bo wker (Barrow), and Hampson (Swinton), Lawton Oldham). J Wilkinson (o,dham), Aspey (Widnes), Lee (St. Helen), and Ball (Wigan). THE CHESHIRE FIFTEEN.</p>
        <p>The Cheshire County Committee on Wednesday night selected the following Cheshire team to oppose Lanca- shire : Sam Houghton (Runcorn) ; Hodgson (Stockport), Butterworth and Warder (Runcorn), and Robinson (Stockport) ; JLoiley and Richardson (Runcorn) ; Walker Lightfoot, Farmer, Lan eley, Woods (Runcorn), °ram. Trotter, Simister (Stockport). Reserves . Wilkinson (Runcorn) ; Edmund (Birken- head), Wilkinson (Rune.orn) ; Crockar (Stookport) ; Brown (Birkenhea3) and Fivargue (Stockport).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-18_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>'77._ Sankey Green A,F.C. v. Cable Works. This match took place on the Catll Works ground on Saturday. Up to the fiairh the visitors Uhl the upper hand and came out winners by three goals to none.</p>
        <p>Wilderspool Central v. Lovely-lane. At Wilderspool. The home team started, and kept the ball in the visitors' territy for some time, goals bsing narrowly averted. Woodhead dribbled well, but his shots went %Ode. Even play was witnessed, and at the interval there was no score. The secor.d half saw a series of attacks on both sides, but the goals remained intact. Minards placed the ball to Baxter's feet, who put in a fine shot, which was rushed through. Final Wilderspool Central, one goal ; I.3vely-lane, nil.</p>
        <p>Birkenhead, Liverpool, and District Competition. Orford Barracks v. Birkenhead Half Holiday. Toe above toatng met on the 10th int. at Warring- tozi„ and an exciting ga,ne resulted in a draw of one goal each.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL LEAGUE. Position of the clubs up to 03tobcr nth, 1901. Goals _</p>
        <p>Playel Won Lost Dwn FMTAkst Pts. Bewsey AU Saints Burtonwoocl Latchford Runcorn Parish Church St. Austins . Hartestown Amateurs 5 ... 2 ... 2 ... 1 ... 9 —.ll ... 5 Warrinsuna Trinity Latenford Britannia 4 . 2 . 2 . 0 6 . 7 4 Frodsharn Parish Church 5 ... 2 ... 3 ... 0 ... 9 -.12 ... 4 St. Mary's 4 . 1.. 2 .1 .. 5 5.. i Itichmonds A.F.O 5 . 1 . ; . 1 8 ...12 ... 3 Sankey Albions Stockton Death Daresbury 4.1 . 3 . 0 5. 9 ... 2 Guardian A.F.C.</p>
        <p>Warrington Trinity v. Runcorn Parish Church. At Warrington. The visitors commenced the attack, aided by a slight wind. Minty pr eased for a time, but could not get through. Runcorn broke away and almost , scored, Jones saving the situation by conceding a corner, but this was got away. Biker passed several !opponents and put in a beautiful centre, from which Shearer sent the sphere into the net five minutes from the interval. Half-time i—Warrington Trinity. one goal ; Runcorn Parish Church, nil. On the restart, Warrington went away and Machin scored a second point before five minutes had elapsed. Runcorn now began to press, but were driven back time after time. After a passing miavement by the home forwards the ball was sent flying into the net. Meachin added a fourth point, and the firlil score was as follows : Warrington Trinity, four goals ; Runcorn Parish Church, nil. "Warrington Guardian" v Richmond Engineers. The engineers hid all the best of the gam a and won by five goals to one. , Latchford v Earlestown Amateurs. At Latchford. The visitors were the first to press, but were driven back. After a passing movement by the , home forwards Newall headed the leather through the • goal. The Amateurs pressed again, and a score seemed ' imminent, but Winstanley removed the danger. Valen- tine tried hard to equalise the score but failed. Half-</p>
