      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-18_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>Chorley v. Warrington.</head>
        <p>Chorley v. Warrington.</p>
        <p>At Chocley on Sat urday, before a ,neagre attendance. Chorley opened up hill and presaed ea,.erly, play settling in the visitors' quarters. Bannister showed good form. The visitors became dangerous, but Chorley recoverod, and Bannister ran up the ft :d and kicked outside the posts, but immediately afterwards succeedei in scoring with a splendid sho'. From the crritre Warrington again got down, with no result. In.;erval Chorley, one ; Warrington, nil. Rnuming, Cherley had the downhill advantage, and after a fine sprint Bannister was robbed by Lynes, but promptly recovered and struck the post. The Warrington goal underwent a terfect bombardment, hut ,•.tie danger was cleared. Some neat passing followed, Morton and Haddington shooting cleverly. Bannister again scored, and despite rOme really clever play by Cook at back and Aspinall in goal the home team always held the upper hand, and I' dding another goal won by three goals to nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-18_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES. Bold-street wesley Guild.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES. Bold-street wesley Guild.</p>
        <p>Mr. Littler presided over Mon day evening's meeting of the guild. To p rogramme included a solo by Mrs Littler, and a stump sermon by Mr McMasters, the remainder of the time b Ping devotcd to one minute impromptu speeches by the members. C msiderable amusement was caused by the nature of some of the subjects and the manner in which they were dealt with.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-18_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>Open Meeting of the Wycliffe P.S.A.</head>
        <p>Open Meeting of the Wycliffe P.S.A.</p>
        <p>Society.</p>
        <p>A very successful open meeting of tne Wycliffe P.S.A Society was held on Sunday afternoon. The church was filled with members, and their lady friends. Mr E 8 Garland, of Warrington occupied the chair, and in re- ferring to the usefulness of the society be said that by its means many a home had been made the brigh`er and happier and many a life had been rescued from the degrading influences which were always at work in their manufacturing towns. He conratulatei the members on the success which still attended the society. Pro- ceeding, Mr Garland delivered a short address upon .• Mutual influence," showing how everybody, °in and young alike, were constantly exerting some influence, dither for better or worse, upon those with whom they I were daily coming into contact. Let them make sure ' that their influence was being exerted in the right direction. Mr Walley, at Manchester, was the speaker and he gave an interesting discourse Upon the words 'Come unto Me all ve that labour and are heavy laden I and I will give you rest. Take My yoke upon you and learn of Me, for My yoke is easy, and My burden is light." During the meeting three mu,ical selections " The Mighty Lord," " Portent in Christ," and " The Hallelujah Chorus," were exce!lently rendered by the members of the Warrington Male Voice Choir, under the conductorship of Mr S Hassell. Mr Thomas lit.wthorn and Mr Healey also took part in the meeting. The Rev Jelin Tongs announced that five more members had been enrolled that afternoon and the membership now oxceeded that of the previous half year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-18_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>PADGATE,</head>
        <p>PADGATE,</p>
        <p>CAB ACCIDENT.—A heavily-laden cab,containing three men, three women and two children in the inside, and two men on the box with the driver, was proceeding down the station incline in the direc•ion of Woolston,at noon on Monday when the bolt holding the shaft broke and the shaft left the socket. The men on the boa narrowly escaped precipitation into the roadway, hut fortunately the driver was able to check the progress of his horse, and af:er assistance had been obtained from the station, the party were able to proceed on their j mrney.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-18_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>WAS !T A POISON SNAKE?</head>
        <p>WAS !T A POISON SNAKE?</p>
        <p>Sometime e John Garrett would go from morning till night without bite or sup. This was three or four years ago. Every morning he got up with a disagreeable, gnawing pain in the stomach or abdomen—ho couldn't quite locate it, but he felt it all the same. Still be kept on working. He meant to see a doctor but neglected it. So a year ran away. By then the complaint had got hold of him as a wild grape vine does of an old tree. Ile was a man full of rosin as an unvunned cellar is of mou'd and crawling thi :gs. Often they kept hot flannels on him for twelve hours before he got a bit of ease ; sari for three weeks straight away be was in rack- ing pain. At intervals it made the sweat run off him in streams. Then he went to bed, John dio,and a doctor took him in hand, as a pilot does a ship when tee navigation is growing ugly. "My doctor," Garrett, sacs" was the best of men,but he didn't seem to know what ailed me." Nor did the other medical men who followed bine. On this our poor brother's heart sank within him, as King David's did when he heard of the fate of Absalom. Haying nothing to do except to think, he thought he would go to a London hospital and ask the learned men there what was the matter with him. They said his right kidney was diseased and the left one affected. ! They feared they couldisic help him but would take him in for a month if he cared to star. He said he didn't care to stay, and went home to D afford. Quite right. As a place to die in the hop country beats a hospital Then be told his home doctor what the London doe- tore had said. The horns doctor agreed with the London doctors and told him he would soon j3lO the silent maj silty—who are done with diseases. The end of Garrett's Cory. written by him at his home, 13, Wellington-road, Thrtford, Kent, January 7th,l9ol,,runs this way: I kept out of bed and moved about all I could. I turned up at the works sometimes, but trom January 1900 to Easter I did not earn enough to keep myself. More than once they sent me home. "For three years I lingered along in this fashion when '3 Newspaper about a stevedore one Sunday I read in Vey:. who was handled like me and load been cured by Mother Seigel's Syrup ; and without saying a word to anybody I got that medicine and began taking it. "The first bottle pricked up my appetite and I actually felt hungry a strange sensation for me. I slept better. The pain was not so sharp and the kidney secretion was clearer and of a better colour. The second bottle did me more good tltl, and after the third I felt like a king, I mean a healthy and happy king, it ttere is such a being. "To-day I feel as well as ever I did. Saturday last I felt a bit out of sorts, from indigestion probabl), and took a dose of Seigel's Syrup ; and to day I am right as rain. You may publiih my tale."—John Garrett. Did this man have Bright's diseaFe ? You have heard i what the doctors said. Let us leave the point to them. Whether the snake that bit U 9 was a poison sn ske or not is of no importance after we have got rid of the effects of the bite. Anyhow, whatever Garrett had, Mother Seigel's Syrup cured him ; and the rest is nothing. He has been employed at the Daily Telegraph paper mills for twenty•sis years, but does not look his sixty ryears, hie real age. Meeting him to-day you would not -imagine he had ever been ill at alt. Inc our Carnegie Competition we have awarded One Guinea to the following : John M Brownlee, Hat boar Square. Portpatttek,N. B. Margaret C3rnall, 7, Newland Villas, Reepham, Norwich. P Heraty, 13, Lance-street, Everton, Liverpool. Thos. Ovenden ,Post Office,Bearsted, Maidstone, Kent. Further awards will be announced in the newspaper , from week to week.</p>
        <p>Good Templars Meeting at Widnes. The monthly meeting of the No. 1 convention of the 1.0.Cf.T., was held on Saturday in the Mission Hall, West-itreet,Widnes. There was a good attend- ance of members from Warrington, Earlestown, Leigh, Ashton, St. Helens and other parts of the area. The meeting was opened at 3-30 by Bro Lindon, C C T (of St Helens) he being supported on .the platform by Bros Room D Coun., and Walker D T., PG A S., Liverpool After the minutes of the last meeting bad been read and confirmed, the C S ' Bro Smith (Ashton), read his monthly report,show- ing the lodges to be doing a good work. Attar some remarks by the members the report was passed. Bro Lindon V D, then read a paper on the work of the Order of Good Tamplars for the past 50 years,in which he brought to notice some very interesting facts about the order :—Bros Sheath, V D (War- rington) Parr (Leigh) Newman DSJT (Earles- town) and others gave interesting items of good work dons by the prder. Bros Room and Walker gave very good addresses. A vote of thanks to the 4Vidnes members brought a very pleasant session to a close.</p>
        <p>NONE NEED DESPAIR 1 While Mellor's Unique system of treatment for Diseases of the Eye exists there is hope for everyone. 27,000 cases cured. Pamphlet free.—MELLOR, Eye Specialist, 286, Oxford-street, Man- chester.— (Advt.) (8)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-18_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>NEW POLICE BUILDINGS.</head>
        <p>THE NEW POLICE BUILDINGS.</p>
        <p>OPENING CEREMONY YESTERDAY.</p>
        <p>Friday an interesting public ceremony was witnessed of more teen passing im- portance in the history of Warrington. At noon the Mayor (Alderman Roberts), formally opened the magisterial portion of the hand- some new police buildings which have been erected in Arpley-street and Wilson Patten- street. This is the only part of the extensive block of buildings which is yet ready for use, and it will be some little time before the interior arrangmente are completed. The Police Court is practically finished, the furniture being all ' that remains to be added. The court room is lofty ana spacious, and it is certain that it will be sufficiently large to meet the demands of Warring- , ton for many years to come. Close at hand is a well appointed magistrates retiring room corn- mending a view of Wilson Patten-street. The cells are not quite ready for the reception of prisoners, and vile will necessitate the use of the present Bridewell as a lock-up. Prisoners will be conveyed to the new Court house each morning for trial until the premises are complete. The first sitting for th transaction ofibusiness was held on Saturday morning. All who inspected,the buildings on.Priday were agreed as to the general utility and enevenience of the premises for the purposes for which they are designed. The new buildings have distinct architectural features, and must rank as the most imposing of the public buildings of War- rington. They reflect infinite credit upon the archi- tects who designed them (Messrs Cackett and Burns Dick, Newcastle-en-Tyne), and also upon the builder (Mr C W Davenport), and the Clerk of Works (Mr H Hallard). Fri,iay's ceremony was attended by almost the whole of the members of the Corporation, the principal officials, and the magistrates. The Mayoress and Miss Roberts were also present. A large number of the general public witnessed the interesting proceedings. All having assembled at the Wilson Patten-street entrance, Alderman Fair- dough, deputy chairman of the Watch Committee, handed a key to the Mayor, who opened the door and entered the buildings. The distinguished company followed, and immediately proceeded t 3 the court room. The magistrates having taken their seats on the Bench, the Mayor delivered a short speech. He said that it was perhaps desirable to explain that some two months ago the architects of the build- ings were asked when they thought it was probable or possible that the court might be opened for public use. Their reply was that probably the first week in October mighs be considered as a date on which the work would be sufficiently advanced to permit the opening to take place. Acting upon their opinion the Watch Committee decided to open the premises that day, and they had done him the honour, which had a certain amount of added interest in the fact that it happened to be his birthday—(applause)--to ask him to perform the opening ceremony. Mr Monks had just asked him a question he would rather answer privately, (Laughter). Ile thought there could be no two opinions about this at any rate—that it was quite time Warrington had new police premises. (Hear, hear). So far as the buildings in Irlam-street were concerned, Sir Herbert Croft had repeatedly referred to them, and be might say that the Corporation had been in almost yearly dread of being severely mulcted in heavy penalties for not providing new premises earlier.Tne interests and convenience of the public and of the justices also-required their serious con- sideration, Everyone knew perfectly well that I whenever any matter of exceptional public interest had to be considered at the Police Court in lrlarn-streer, tke place was so densely crowded, so uncomfortable and so insanitary, as to be almost positively to some extent, at any rate, a danger to health. He did not think they had removed one moment too soon into the present premises. He thought they would also agree that that room, at any rate, reflected great credit on the architects who designed it, and on the builder who had carried out their instructions. He sincerely hoped the premises would not merely be used as a court of law, but as a court of justice in the truest sense of the term,and that those of them who were:vested with the re- sponsible dignity of carrying out the law, would always be well advised in what they did, and that they would endeavour, so far as they possibly could,while having a due regard of what was neces- sary to the safety of society to remember the indubitable fact that humanity was a very frail thing. While they were determined that the har- dened criminal should receive very little considera- tion he hoped they would always take every possible opportunity of giving those who came before them for the first time,end had made perhaps the first serious mistake of their lives, an early opportunity to make reformation. In conclusion he thanked the Deputy Mayor and the Watch Committee for the honour they had conferred upon him, and his fellow justices and townspeople for , honouring him by their presence that morning, He begged to declare the court open. (Applause.) r Mr F MONKS, ..I P, moved a vote :of thanks to the Mayor for accepting the invitation of the Watch Corn mittee and presiding at the function. In doing so he said he thought the duty ought to have devolved upon Mr J,lin White who was his senior on the magistracy. Be was sure they would all agree with the remarks of the Mayor with reference to the uses to which toe room would be put, and toe spirit in which the magistrates should administer the laws of the country. Mr Joae FAIRCLOUGEL seconded. _ _</p>
        <p>The motion was carried with acclamation. The MAYOR said he was very much obliged for the vote of thanks, which he appreciated quite as heartily as they had given it to him.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-18_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>Dinner at the Town Hall.</head>
        <p>Dinner at the Town Hall.</p>
        <p>Interesting Speeches.</p>
        <p>Following the c,remony at the new buildings lunch was served at the Town Hall, to a large cmnpany which included the members of the Corporation the County and Borough magistrates and other guests. Mr Powis catered as usual, and gave every satisfaction. There was a short toast lis:, following tbe lunch in the course of which several interesting speeches were made. His Worship the MAYOR. presided and gave sucem- sively the toasts of " The King," " The Queea,the Duke and Duchess of York, and members of the Royal ' family,"making use of appropriate and loyal sentiments on each occasion. Toe toasts were musically honoured the company singing the National Anthem after drink- inv to the health of His Mijesty. . .</p>
        <p>hopo</p>
        <p>The MAYOEL then toasted The New Police Build- ings" coupling with his proposition the name of Mr F Monks, J P., as senior magistrate He coull not, he said, all w the occasion to pass with- out making some slight reference to the new buillings as an architectural feature ot Warrington, and he thought the opinion of the people of Warrington was that they were a very fine conception from an architec- tural point of view, and extremely convenient for the purpose of practical utility. They would perhaps have eujoyed the opening proceedings more if matters had been in a more complete state, but they must remember that when they got an archi• tent and builder on the job thay knew when they started and they knew when they finished— when the bill was presented—but they could have no idea of the length of time between the two occasions Hear, hear, and laughter.) There were always unfore•</p>
        <p>een delays, and there had been in this case, but he bought toe C ourt ro om would do very well to go on with until the rest of the buildings were finished. Alluding to Mr Monks, the Mayor expressed the sincere hope that the senior magistrate would be with them for many years to assist in the administration of justice in the new premises. (Hear. bear.) His wise counsel,long experience, and kinuliness of heart were very import- ant in helping men who had not had the same experiene to do the right thing at the right time in regard to the people who came oefore them. (Applause.) Mr F MONKS responded to the toast, saying that it was extremely difficult for him to find words to express his feelings after the very flattering way in which the Mayor had intro iuced the toast. He must thank them all as a Corporation for the very spacious and handsome buildings they had provided for the administration of justice. One gentleman with whom he had discussed the matte" expressed the opinion that the new buildings would suffl oe for the next 700 years, which was a long time to base a prediction upon, but he was certain at any rate that those who had had to attend to business in the old courthouse would be glad to get out of it. (Hear, hear.) He hoped they would always have justice tempered with the spirit of mercy. So far as his ex- perience went he thought the Bench to which they ' belonged was animated by that spirit. Mr Monks con- cluded by thanking the company warmly for their ex pression of kindness, and ventured to join the Mayor in his hope that he (Mr Monks) would live for a good few years yet. (Applause.) The MAYOR also had the duty of proposing the health of "the Architects and Builders," and in doing so said that Mr Burns Dick knew how fully they appreciate the skill and artist:taste be had displayed in connec- tion with these fine b uilding s, which would help to adorn the town for many years to come. Mr Dick's efforts had been heartily appreciated by the Watch Committee in particular and the whole of the townspeople in general. What was due to Mr Burns Dick and his assistant Mr Hindle it also became him to say of the builder. lie was sorry that Mr Davenport had had so long an illness which prevented him being with them. When a illatt• was on the right side of 40, an illness lasting 16 weeks was a very serious manner. However the job itself reflected the greatcst credit upon all concerned and he coupled with the toast the names of Messrs Burns Dick and Davenport. (Applause.) Mr BURNS DICK in reply thanked the Mayer, Corpora- tion, and all with whom he had come in contact for the great kindness shown to him. He particularly thanked the Watch Committee for entrusting him with so im- portant a piece of work, the merits of which they would be better able to judge when the new premises were being fully utilised. His labour as architect had been very materially lightened by the manner in which the requirements and conditions of the work had been originally drawn out. No one could have given clearer instructions to an architect than Mr Longdin did. (Applause.) Mr Burns Dick then paid a tribute to the services ot Mr Hindle, Mc Hallard (clerk of the works) and Mr Davenport, and concluded by again expressing his gratitude for the honour done him and the many kindnesses shown. Councillor BENNETT p oposed the " County and Borough Magistrates," coupled with the names of Mr W Long and Mr John White. He did so with great pleasure as they had felt some solicitude for the health of the magistrates owing to the insanitary conditions under which they had carried out their duties in the past. He thought the County and Borough Magistrates had administered justice faithfully and well. They bad sometimes been unpunctual—(laughter)—but having regard to the place in which they had to meet it was surprising that they turned up at all. (Hear, hear.) In the future they would be able to administer justice under different and pleasanter conditions. Like the police headquarters on the continent the Warrington police buildings might almost be a " palace of justice." Be trusted that the motto to be borne in mind there would be</p>
        <p>"And ever the troth comes uppermost And ever is justice done." (Applause.) Mr W LONG, in responding to the toast, thanked the Mayor and the Watch Committee for so kindly including in their list of invitations their neighbours of the County Bench. (Hear, hear.) It gave them very great phasure to be present and rejoice with them in the opening of these splendid Courts, which won l i be an ornament to Warrington as long as Warrington listed. They in Cheshire could not boast of anything so splendid and be could almost wish they were under the aegis of the Warrington Corporation and Watch Committee. (Anolause). Mr Jonst WHITE also split., and said he rose with somewhat of a guilty conscience because his attendance at the Borough Court during the last twelve months had been very irregular. Under the new regime and in these alluring new fbuildings be hoped t 3 be a b!tter attender than in the past.fle did not claim to knoW,how borough magistrates were selected ,and made—laughter —but if it was anything in their favour to be a repre- sentative body be thought the:Borough Bench was repre- sentative of all sections of the community. (Hear, hear). On every occasion when he had been at Court he had noticed that the magistrates present seemed actuated by the desire to do justice to all parties. He felt sure that in the future the same token of purity would be apparent in connection a ith the duties Of the borough magistrates, (applause.) Colonel FITZHERBEHT proposed the toast of " The Army " and remarked that the military forces in the town owed an immense debt of gratitude to the Mayor and Corporation of the Borough. Ho could not speak too highly of the kindaess and support which had been ext, nded to the units of the army locally and to the wives and children of those concerned ia the war. Nobody knew what would have happened if this long and continued effort at relief had not been made. It was no dashing down of a few pounds in a moment of enthusiasm but a long and sustained endeavour to provide for th ose left behind. The Mayor and Corporation had redeemed the implied pledge that on woman or child in Warrington should want during the trying time which they had passed through. He had a small scheme in his mind to benefit the condition of the soldier. They wanted to start a little e.lub for the soldiers of the town—a place where they could go and enjoy themselves—sod they wanted to throw it open to all, militia, volunteers and regulars. (Applause). He hoped he would not be denied their assistance and support. The volunteer corps iii Warrington owed a great deal to the Mayor and Corporation fo Warrington. The volunteers had done well out noth- ing thrived except under good conoitions. (Hear, hear.) It was a great pleasure to him to be associated with so patriotic and so generous a Corporation and so thoroughly patriotic a town as Warrington. (Applause.) The DEPUTY-MAYOR responded and said it was very nice to be patted on the back, because Corporations as a rule did not get patted on the back. He quite agreed with Colonel Fitnerbert'a remarks ye a Soeial Club and the scheme would have his warm support. c Applause.) _ _</p>
        <p>This concluded the spoochnstking. and soon afterwards the compsny dispersed after having enj)yed a very pleasant Ceremonial.</p>
        <p>The First Prisoner Dischargea.</p>
        <p>The first prisoner to be tried in the new Court, at the sitting on Monday morning, was Joseph Webb. forge labourer, Widnes, who appeared on the charge of being drunk and disorderly in thg Market-place on Saturday night.—P.C. Whittaker sail he was on duty near the Royal Exchange Inn, when he noticed the defendant in the midst of a large crowd sf people. He was making use of bad language. Witness requested him to go home, bat he r2r.u3ed. Two men offered to take him away, bet he would not allow them, and be had to be locked up.—The Chief Constable said the prisoner had be n before the Court once previously. He was scarcely compos mends, and this being the first case in the new Court, he asked the permission of the Bench to with- draw the charge.—The magistrates agreed and the prisoner was dismissed with the advice from the Chair- man (Mr 1, Monks), to " go home and do bcter." Alterations Suzgested</p>
        <p>A. number of alterations in the Court arrangements were suggested at the conclusion of the business on Monday. The Chairman (Mr. F Monks) was of the opinion that the dock ought to be made smaller and brought cicser to the Bench.—lt also went against his feelings to see the prisoners come up as if they were criminals—The Chief Constable said the same manage- ment existed in ether towns.—Mr Monks : That does not matter. They are not criminals in the sense that prisoners are at the Assizes. Continuing, the Chairman said he could hear the witnesses, but not the prisoners in the dock. —Mr A. Browne suggested that a</p>
        <p>-rail eh-odd—be--placed in front of the present dock. That seemed to him to be the most convenient conise to adop,.—The Chief Conatable : I believe that is an arrangement that is in =temptation. —The reporters were asked to give their cpiuion with regard to the arrangements for their accommodation,and said it was difficult to hear all that was said at their desks. It was suggested that the desks should also be raised and this alteration is in contemplatiod</p>
        <p>Bravery Rewarded.—Attempted Rescue from</p>
        <p>Drowning. At a meeting of the Ruyal Humane Society on Tuesday testimonials were awarded to William Chumley, aged 13, and Villiam Melling, for an attempt to save George Evans who was drowned while bathing in the Sankey and St Helens canal at Warrington on August let. It will be remembered that Chumley who resides in Edmund-street saw Evans bathing in the canal. He was trying to swim across, tut seemed to be seized with cramp, and commenced struggling. Chumley endeavoured to rescue him, and plunged into the water without divesting himse fof his clothing. He got hold of deceased's foot but was forced to relinquish his hold as deceased was pulling him under. Melling, who is employed at Messrs Houghton's wireworka, , and lives at 12. Sutton-street, came upon the scene after Evans had sunk, and encumbered with his clothing, dived into the water twice, but did not succeed in finding he boy. The body was ulti- mately recovered with the aid of grappling irons by P.C. Woods.</p>
      </div>
