      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-18_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>EXAMINER.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER.</p>
        <p>0010BUt 18 Ta 1901.</p>
        <p>One passage in Lord Lord Rosebery Sok ing a Problem. ROSEBERY'S address at the Birmingham Insti- tute calls for special notice, as it affords the clue to much that is inexplicable in his lordship's pre- sent attitude of retirement from the activities of political warfare. It is that in which he said there are few things the nation requiries more than independent thought. It points to Lord ROSEBERY'S opinion, and one which it is not diffi- cult to endorse, that even reading, that source of so much knowledge, may become injurious if pursued as an aim instead of being but the means to an end. That end is, of course, informing the mind to enable it to think out its own problem of life and duty, and to form an independent judg- ment upon the facts, political and otherwise, with which it comes into daily contact. If this is so how much more injurious is the holding on to worn-out shibboleths which have "ceased to be real and essential." The plea his lordship puts forth for independent thought seems to be the apology and explanation he offers for his own conduct. It does seem strange that withdrawal from the party whose very existence is a noble testimony to the power of free thought should have been felt to be necessary, but it is sufficiently explained by the circumstances attend- ing Lord ROSEBERY'S retirement. He thinks the time has come for a reconstruction of the political basis, but his position as a leader, morally, of the party to which he belonged seemed to call for his adhesion to views which were not the result of his own thinking, but in Many cases the views of other people who had not thought as much or penetrated as deeply into the causes of things, He, therefore, found a necessity for breaking the last links that bound him to party reiponsibility lin order that he might formulate anew his own political creed, apart from the disturbing element of opportunism or the claims of partisans who might influence his decision on some points because of mutual sympathy on others. He urges that all men, according to their opportunity and capacity, should go and do likewise, and in the result he I looks for the inspiring force which a people trained I in political thought will be in a position to exert upon the future rulers of the Empire. Thus the practical issue of an apparently unpractical posi- tion is made clear. Lord ROSEBERY is palpably convinced that the Liberal party as such is hope- lessly beyond the help of mere peddling reforms based upon even the high traditions of the party. These after all are but traditions, and, therefore antiquated in form if not in essence. What he craves for is the quick penetrating thought and nervous force which characterises the American of the United States, and enables him by a kind of intuition to " spot" what is most useful to him and tends to advancement. To find the basis of I this his lordship is prepared to go back to the ele- mentary process of ploughing and sowing,knowing well that the course he advocates will in due time I bring forth fruit. If a demonstration of the sound- ness of his position were necessary, we have only to refer to the recent history of Toryism in this country. A few decades ago Conservatism was a decaying force. It clung to its traditions, and was dying of inertia. But a force awoke within its ranks making for new conditions, which were gradually accepted, and when trouble arose on the opposite side of politics there was a regenerated living organism ready to absorb the discordant elements thrown, out. Hence arose the :present powerful Unionist party. What the late Lord RANDOLPH CHURCHILL, the present Leader of the House of Commons and others associated with them in the Fourth Party did for Conservatism, Lord ROSEBERY proposes to do for Liberalism. His method is different, and the development probably will differ also, but that the end will be as effectively achieved there is very little diffi- culty in believing.</p>
        <p>TIIE Licensed Victual The Trade and License lers seem to be taking Reform.</p>
        <p>alarm at the frequent I references of commendation which are being made by public men in various walks of life to the Re- formed Public House movement. Their National Defence League, ever on the watch where the in- terests of the trade appear to be threatened, re- cently wrote to Mr JUSTICE BaucE at Leeds com- plaining of certain remarks made by his lordship at the Leeds Assizes, attributing most of the crime of the country to public-houses,and protest- ing that such remarks reflected unjustly upon the conduct and character of license-holders. It is a delicate matter to deal with such subjects wh en the conduct and character of individuals have to be impugned, although the League did not liesi-</p>
        <p>tate,on the strength of statements by certain chief' constables, to transfer the gravamen of the charge to the keepers of clubs. His lordship however, 10 a reply which he has just sent to the secretary of the League, lets it be clearly understood that, while he never said mcst of the crime of the country was attributable to public-houses, what he did say, and repeated, was that no inconsider- able number of crimes spring from the intemperate use or intoxicating liquors; and he challenged the League to deny that the temptations to indulge 10 intoxicating liquor to an undue degree are too often afforded by the public-houses. He adds that long experience in courts of justice compelled him to say that the temptat:ons were not few nor infrequent. One cause of temptation was that many public-houses have become wholly devoted to the sale of intoxicating liquors, while other beverages, such as tea or coffee, cannot be pro- cured easily or at all. Food is not offered, or not offered in an attractive form, so that a poor uls° who wishes to resort to a public-house for company or refreshment is subjected to temptation which he cannot resist • Such testimony, carefully guarded and based upon known fact and experience, is most valuable in arriving at a judgment upon this important question. License-holders are entr</p>
        <p>with a valuable: and strictly guarded mon)p0191 in the interests of the public who desire refresh' moot; but the monopoly is worked in the interests of the license-holdors, who have frequently bile to provide places of wholesale refreshmont, and have substitute° therefor places where tempts" tions are presented to the use of only one class of refreshment, which though lucrative as a source of revenue to the publican, is degrading in its effects upon the purchaser. If the drink-selh were an ordinary trader he might please hi._ as to the stock he keeps; but his trade has beeo called into existence to meet a public necessity', It has failed to meet it in any proper sense, therefore the community has a grievance whil it is entitled to urge upon the attention (-of Government and of Parliament against those WI</p>
        <p>glad to see ordinary license-holders supplyil victuals and wholesome drinks, and anticipat the action of philanthropists by raising the eh;</p>
        <p>actor, improving the accommodation and extent- ing the usefulness of their houses. SJ should we all. It would certainly be something gaiced if the managers of our large and attractive gm-palaces were to refuse to make them the means of degrad' ing the poor, and turn them into places of whole' some refreshment. It is possible something of the kind may be tried; but it is scarcely human natur,e to look for much success so long as the profits ot the establishmeat go to pay dividends to sha... holders in large concerns whose only thought 15 the return they get for the money they have invested.</p>
        <p>What do the public know of the drama ? is led to ask that question by an article from pen of Mr Bram Stoker in the first number of Playgoer,which has made such a promising t ing. Mr Stoker is describing the art of Terry, and mentions an interesting fact to . how the artist subordinates individuality to character. One night, in a great rnanufactul city, Miss Terry was playing the part of Madi Sans—Gele with oven more than her usual She was lost in the assumption so completely it was real to her, andAhe ex-washerwoman, her mind harassed and worried by th 3 trying L'' (el' ditions of her artificial Court life, instinctively r 0 turned to the habits of her youth. In a morello of abstraction, finding a roll of soft stuff acro.s . her arm, she instinctiNely began to wring id out. The action was entirely unpremeditater; and the audience:recognised its sincerity,and wary., so delighted that ever since it has been made 11,, - of the " business." It would be easy to multiP •:, illustrations of this kind—how for the time li81'10: the play dominates the performer. These spontif, 0 eous evidences of concentration of thought on fl:x part are, however, obtained even by the 10°,4 talented only after the most thorough study. c application, not only as regards the broad pr.iLlps, ples, but also as regards the particular niec-re Young girls who,because they have a pretty Ogu,e, and some vivacity, think that they could win fnl on the boards, should bear this fact in mind ; 'la, t playgoers, if they realised how much the art f`f h,gp the artist, would be a little more considerate—tr' , would avoid arriving late, or talking and oti's'ay 1 wise interrupting. The men and women in fVeir... of the footlights are -often very rude in indifference. The best way to encourage the dr3 oci ;, is to take a kindly yet critical interest in it, arg, to appreciate the exacting character of the Mr. Stanley Jones may suggest that it beco'--0, mechanical, but therein he only shows his 0 limitations.</p>
        <p>"Many happy returns of the day," to Alclernlaiii Henry Roberts, J.P., Mayor of Warrin La birthday Last Friday was his Worship's b rtay an , tof days ought to be, it was a day somewhat " bv the common. Alderman Roberts signalised it h'e formally opening the new police buildings a by t nting t Council made it a red-letter day y prese whey t the Mayor a handsome silver rose boWI• v gtoilb latter, by the way, was in lieu of the custoulaarLl golden key presented to individuals who eciPs4 public buildings, and the substitution 01 worc useful and ornamental an article as, tom given to his Worship speaks well for the goo° -the and commonsense of the Council. In rnakingAl presentation at the lunch in the Town</p>
        <p>d v 1 1</p>
        <p>t t T t ,$. 1 t</p>
        <p>rusted ca ci ,ier tmsall and ich tho having a public trust which brings them large profits, have failed to implement their part of the contract. Mr JUSTICE BRUCE points to the advantage promised by EARL GREY'S scheme for Reformed Public Houses ; but he fails to see whY any monopoly should be established in what be' considers a hopeful experiment. 113 would birig tar" Of tar&amp; Mr. Stead's description of President Rooseve3 as "MrßerefordG al andds t Joonhen, B 1 \ rr sßbhooi di eeds , d oLwonr d until C h a yourles can get the residual essence into an American Dutchman" is likely to be remembered. The re; semblance to Mr. Gladstone is, perhaps, the lre‘l direct, but there is something distinctly Gis stonian in the Presi'dent's voracious appetite '- reading and his power of writing voluminously any conceivable subject; his interest in the til°l.a . and semi-religious aspects of politics, and, /LW; all, in his capactity for becoming, almost witheuk r effort on his part, the centre of every gres controversy which happens to be raging. Theli he has alt Mr Rhodes's passion for empire' making and expansion, Lord Charles Beres; ford's personal gallantry and popularity as • fighter or as a critic of military or naval adminir tration ; and Mr. John Burns's ceaseless activiti and intense devotion to matters which the averd age politician dismisses with a wave of the hall, as parochial. The picture, however, so admiratq drawn by Mr. Stead's hand—for the subject is oil which suits singularly his somewhat flamboysl3.4 style—suggests deeper reflections. Such a ma° invaluable, indispensable in certain moments crisis, but can his hold upon the public mind be.o considered permanent? It is inevitable that. 4, the position of supreme power he should (Melt, animosities. Men who have hitherto fanned b!1 popularity because it served their purpose wir, not endure his authority should their own arnbio tion or their own interests be affected. Vir°. yeats hence the chief of the Rough-riders will hall: a nasty obstacle to encounter in the form of; Presidential Election' which even at this silo" distance may leave him lamenting, like inseci another ex-favourite, the perfidy of friends the fickleness of the many-headed. Oee tile becitor sho- tb° verit the: • v• Al</p>
        <p>Respectable footballers cannot fail to very sin- crely regret the oc;turrences that occasionally cast discredit upon the football field. Another case has been reported in which the referee was attacked by a player who had reported him for misconduct in a match. When play was over the aggrieved footballer followed the referee, 17nocked him down, and kicked him as he lay on the ground, rendering him unconscious. The county association that instituted the prosecution did so with a desire "to suppress brutality and ruffianism. 4 It is very necessary, if football nas ro ha regarded as a national institution, deserving of Public patronage and support, that there should be Prosecutions in all such cases, and that exhibitions of temper and demoralisation should be put down With determination and a strong hand by all local associations. Instances of lack of discipline and attempts to substitute might for right in the foot- ball field, are not only toocommon,but are increas- ingly so. Nor can it be said that when offenders are convicted, that they are punished too severely by any means. It would be possible to say more, Oat for the sake of the game and the respectable body of players, it is perhaps best to ourselves set au example of forbearance.</p>
        <p>An unfortunate feature of the later stages of the Grimsby Labour Dispute has been that members of the Town Council feel they have reason to com- plain about the importation of"large bodies of police' into the town. They hold that there was no real necessity for the step taken at all. But this nften happens' when the trouble is over, and things have passed off mare peaceably than expected. It is .. sometimes forgotten that the appearance of a 1'• strong force of police may of itself tend to keep .' the rough element in order, whereas the absence of a strong body i 3 the roughs' opportunity, and when : the riot is over and the mischief is done, what do i the same people say about the higher authority then ? " All's well that ends well," is the kind of view and interpretation the public generally have bc,en consoling themselves with.</p>
        <p>t; E • • ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-18_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>MATTERS OF MOMENT.</head>
        <p>MATTERS OF MOMENT.</p>
        <p>A Provincial Letter.</p>
        <p>There are at least two important matters in rtgard to which the country may congratulate itself this week. 1r Chamberlain has been telling the</p>
        <p>temperance people at Birmingham that in the last seventy years there has been a great and decided decrease in the evils resulting from drunkenness ; While the annual:report of the Local Government, Board just issuad,shows that the presentgamount of Pauperism is less than it has ever hitherto been, and the demand for labour during the year has been exceedingly good. There are thirty-seven thousand fewer paupers in 1901 than there were in 1881, arid the prediction so confidently made in 1894 that if the control cf poor-law elections were placed in the hands of tte working-classes it would be foilowed by reckless expenditdre and an increase of Pauperism, nas not been fulfilled. On the contrary, th.lare has been less pauperism since the passing of Sir Henry Fowler's Act than before. Indeed, there has been a gratifying decrease, not only in the number of able-bodied adult paupers, but also anaolig children ; so tout tee Act promises well for the future. lany of those who are included in the able-bodied class are suffering from mental weak- n.ess, rind it is once more pointed out that a con- siderable number of persons of a low type, who in former years would have bean allowed freedom and gone through their lives as village" softies," now help to awed the ranks of pauper lunatics, under a more humane system.</p>
        <p>---- " The Red Brotherhood," what a title for pro- fessed crusaders against the innocent looking, and too commonly inmcently regarded, cigarette ! But the objectors to juvenile smoking may be presumed to kno w what they are about in adopting a style that is suggestive at first ot the stiletto and secret societi.es, and it is, perhaps, no more startling than was that of the " Salvation Army.' or the " Halle- lujah Lasses," at one time. Anyhow, the warden of the"R.E.I.)," not "R.B."has issued from St Hugh's, Penzance, quite a budget of literature directing attention to the movement, which the public are assured deals with the juvenile smoking question in a practical, simple, and temperate way. What- ever difference of opin:on may exist as to smoking for adults, there is practically no doubt that smoking by growing boys is highly injurious, Lads may now purchase quite a packet of " cigarettes " for the nimble p enny, and consequently are not long in contracting the habit by imitating their senior. It has more than once been sugg-sted that the Chancellor of the Exchequer might realise a substantial sum by imposing a small tax upon ev"TY packet of cigarettes, ant if the Warden of the R m n Nr; ,--'" D. could only enlist the sympathy of Sir . ""2"el ilicks-Beach, the latter might not only Prove a valuable member, or " brother," but as an ~ , ." advertising medium " would straightway ensure Ithe success of the new movement. It may interest the reader to know that the " brotherhood " pro- mise not to smoke before they are sixteen years of ' , age. May they succeed, is one's sincere wish,</p>
        <p>Trade and Company Combinations are 1-ec3ming almost every-day affairs. Evidence has for many days been accumulating that, a sharp struggle. is Impending, in the tobacco trade. It is a subject Which is of considerable importance to some of our large towns and cities. For if the newly constituted American syndicate were successful in carrying Out its threats to create a " corner" in tobacco-leaf, n important English industry might suffer much injury. It has been stated that the British amal- Rainated companies, however, contemplate the ac- quisition in America, the West Indies. and Brazil, of tobacco-growing plantations. thus carrying the war into the country of the " enemy "; and if this be true, • hat promptitude with which the American Challenge has been responded to by the combina- tion of English manufacturers must render the Operations of the American tl invaders futile at least for the ensuing twelve months. During that period there will be an opportunity to examine the situation in all its bearings. Row far the move- ments of concentration on this side of the Atlantic may be carried, remains, as yet, to be seen,although it is very clear that centres of the tobacco indudry, ...such as Liverpool, cannot regard the struggle with indifference. Enormous _tobacco _ interests are</p>
        <p>affected in various parts of Englaud, and there will be no little anxiety on the part of those engaged in the trade to obtain definite knowledge as to whither defensive methods are tending.</p>
        <p>It is no doubt Well to take some of the rumours that are ail)at with a large grain of salt. Yet a few months s,nce no one really anticipated that the leading English tobacco firms would deem it need- ful to enter into a combination,while such an amal- gamation as that of Cravvsliay-Guest could not have been thought of by members of the iron trade. In the same way there may be more certain railway rumours than those immediately concerned are ready to admit. As matters stand, the rumour is Maintained that there is an arrangement between some of the big railway companies, and it is not at all difficult to believe. Toe allegation that the North. Eastern add the Great-Northern had agreed to comnine was promptly contradicted ; yet the denial was followed by a report that although there was no amalgamation, an arrangement was Probable on the principle of community of interest and that the North British, the Great-Eastern, and the Midland were likely to join in the arrangement. Again there was a denial, so far as the latter com- pany was concerned, but the" World" newspaper has gone on seriou4ly discussing the subject, and at the same time pointing out how " natural " such an alliance between V.e five companies would te. It IS easy to exaggerate the advantages that would result from the amalgamation of some of our big railways, but American methods would not prove .so successful on this site of the water as in the States; while there is no doubt whatever that it Would be to the disadvantage of the public in the end, travelling and non-travelling, that there should be any such gigantic combination as that Which nevertheless continues to be talked of.</p>
        <p>As bearing upon English Trade, it may have been observed that certain organisations of work- en in Holland are not at all averse to indulging the proverbially foolish idea of killing the goose that lays the most valuable eggs. In their blind hatred of this country, a committee of workmen's Ivisociationa has this aveßk addressed from Amster- (lain a manifesto to cognate societies at ports of not °IIIY Holland, but France, Germany, Sweden, Nor- ay, Denmark, Belgium,and Italy urging the meria- t,'Fi to refuse to unload British vessels alter the rirtY•first of December nest. The most striking ,!iature of the scheme is its audacity and vindic- ,Zijeness. It is certainly doomed to failure, since those Who are pulling the strings from Amsterdam already failed to embarrass English trade at wae Dutch ports, and it is not at all likely that they</p>
        <p>' will fare better in the international sense The agitators are very shortsighted, and utterly fail to perceive that any paralysis of Great Britain as a carrying p vver, would operate disastrously on the trade of ihe countries participating in the con- spiracy.</p>
        <p>erman Sutton spoke in warmly complimentary terms of the valuable services rendered to the town by the recipient during the past'two years, ,and the Alderman's sentiments were cordially en- dorsed In reply Mr. Roberts made one of the neatest impromptu speeches we have ever heard even him deliver, and alluded in modest language to the singularly gratifying fact that he had been Mayor of Warrington in the last year of one cen- tury and the first year of another, in the last year of one reign and the first year of the next, and also in the last year of the old borough and the opening year of the town's existence as a County Borough.</p>
        <p>The new Bishop of Liverpool has been insisting that a clergyman has a perfect riglic to hold his own political views, but no right to proclaim his pulitical views from the pulpit of the Church of England; for the Church of England is not the Church of a party but the °much of a nation. "God forbid," he exclaimed, " that the Church of England should ever identify itself with a single party." That is all vary well as a protest ; but, as a matter of fact, the great majority of the established clergy are firm supporters of the Conservative party; and most of the Bishops, as Peers of Parliament, vote with a Conservative Government, and against measures proposed by Liberals. -Speaking generally, it may be said that, in re,prd to national education,the whole weight of the Church is thrown into the scale of anti-Liberalism. And, in return for its support, a Conservative Govern- ment gives State-doles to the clorgy,and sacrifices the interests of Education to the interests, or supposed interests, of the E3tablishment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-18_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>RUNCORN NOTES • ---- [BY A CORRESPONDENT.I FOOTBALL. _ .</head>
        <p>RUNCORN NOTES • ---- [BY A CORRESPONDENT.I FOOTBALL. _ .</p>
        <p>Oldham ground is one of the unlulkiest bits of turf the Runcorn team visit. On more than one occasion I have accompanied the Linnets to the home of spindles, seen them have all the bast of the play, and yet return home defeated through sheer bad luck, while fortune had bestowed every pomible favour on their opponents. History re- peated it4elf on Saturday. It is wonderful how some scribes of the football press qlare tbeir coa- sciences to write the utterly mut intentionally misleading balderdash they do. I always thought that impartiality was the reporter's raost prized posse,sion, but some of the pressmen of Oldham seem entirely devoid of the qualification.</p>
        <p>However, there was 6ne veracious chronicler present on Saturday and according to his descrip- tion Runcorn were the attacking force almost throughout the eighty minutes ot play, and it was one of those marvellous topsy-turveydom displays which occasionally occur in football that the side defending like grim deatn should capture the spoil in the shape of two points on the League table. I don't approve of referte baiting, nor of much of the criticism of referees, but, in this case it is clear that a lagging referee lost Runcorn the match. A splendid but of passing ended in Butterworth grounding under the posts, but the referee disallowed tie score because he " thougnt " —thought forsooth—that the ball had been passed forward. As Warder hal to turn completely round to throw the ball back, this was somewhat remark- able, and as the referee was not up with the play- ers it was impossible for him to tell the direction of the ball.</p>
        <p>The only Poor consolation left is a " moral victory."</p>
        <p>As a matter of fact Rarscorn beat their great rivals in every depirtment of the Rama. Forward the Runcorn eight excelled themselves and in the first half especially gave the oppoling pack no quarter. Consequently the backs had every chance to shine and did so with great effulgence and the Oldham line was tottering to its tall ifouch a simile will bold good times without number. Butter- worth was iu excep'ionally brilliant form and on three occasions fiiled by inches to secure the coveted try. ftforan threw a certainty away by knocking on.Jolley and Warder had hard lines time and again. In fact Richardson scored the only try an I Farmer failed to augment from a very easy place and several easy penalties and marks went the same way. In toe one burst they hal during the afternoon - Oldham scored, kicked a goal and won the match. Such is fate. Old iam received such a shaking they will not forget in a hurry and when the final whistle blew there was a sigh of re- lief from the spectators and players that might have been heard a mile away.</p>
        <p>Blithely went the green 'tins to Brighouse on Nlonday to tackle the R Inger; of that ilk and won very comfortably indeed, although they were feel- ing the eff :lots of the Saturday's struggle. The die- parity between Yorkshire and Lancashire Club football coatinues to be still a source of great sur- prise.</p>
        <p>The School Board election is slowly but none the less surely becoming the all absorbing tvic of local interest. So far the campaign semis to rest with the progresiive—or as Canon Wood has once been pleased to call them—" progressist " candidates. Everything indicates that the fight will be a keenly contested one. Ere long the voluntarist machinery will be in full swing and then we may expect a sturdy good humoured battle over which, when the fortunes of war decide the victors, the vanquished and the conquered can shake hands in true British fashion: The irnportanle of the issues at stake—both Imperial and !civil —are S) pro. nouncd that the electorate will do well to study thelntricacies of the Education qUestion May the best system win.</p>
        <p>The Halton Parish Council are still at a standstill revecting their action in refusiog Sir Richard Brooke permission to appropriate certain land at\ present belonging to the Council. Sir RiChard —through his agent Mr Evans—has, unwisely for his popularity, demanded certain waste land which he contends belongs to him. Apparently the councillors appear to be hopelessly at sea respecting the legality of his claim but have unanimously re- solved to leave the matter in the hands of the County Council. to far no reply has been made to their overtures but doubtless the next meeting will elicit a definite statement of opinion on the question.</p>
        <p>Query What constitutes a man a lunatic? What traits must a man display to prove that he is mad ? This puzzling question vexed the spirits of the officials at the Police Court on Wednesday. An unkempt and savage looking fellow was charged with being a lunatic, and the evidence clearly showed that the man if not a lunatic was certainly a mad fool, which amounts to the same thing. The constable informed the magistrates that the man was repeatedly threatening to take his mother's life ; had made the neighbours leave tha street ' where he lived owing to his uproarious conduct ; and on the date in question was most violent in his behaviour to the constable who pluckily went to the rescue of prisoner's mother. let in spite of all these tt lags the doctor, though previously consent- ing, refused to certify that he was a dangerous lunatic stating that he was but rather weak intel- lectually. The bench of course, could not make an ' immediate order for the asylum and the prisoner was sent to the workhouse for seven days where be may be further medically examined. If the above dangerous signs are merely indicative of a weak intellect what,in the name of all that's honest are the symptoms of lunacy. Must as a logical ' deduction the man do some diabolical action before he can I:ie put in a strait wastvat ? Perhaps when he has murdered someone or set half the town on fire the doctor in iusstion will realize that he is something more than intellectually weak. There are more paradoxes in the reasonings of doctors than are dreamt of in our philosophy.</p>
        <p>WESTON WESLEYAN BIBLE AND MUTI7AL</p>
        <p>PROVEMENT CLASS. The first meeting of the above class was held on Wednesday evening. There was a very large at- tendance, Mr J Smith gave a cortHt solo and the phonograph was manipulated by Mr A Cross. A singing bee and games were afterwards indulged in. The refreshments were in charge of Misses N Savage,, E Mort, B Cottrell, A Price, C Ball, who admirably fulfilled the duties enttusted to them. The following officers were elected :—President, Rev J B Foster; vice president, J Spruce; secretary, A Cross.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-18_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>APPOINTMENT.</p>
        <p>Mr Samuel P Stevens, preventive office, Liver- pool, has been appointed to succeed Mr Nicholls aa second preventive officer at Runcorn. BRUNSWICK MUTUAL IMPROVE3IENT CLASS. On Wednesday under the presidency of Mr Simister a paper was given by Mr Allred Dodd on .iennyson's "In Memoriam." Messrs, ofi ayes and 1) Moore spoke in the discussion which followed. MERSEY MISSION T. 3 SEAMEN, RUNCORN. In connection with the above mission the annual harvest festival was held on Thursday and Sunday. The interior had been very neatly and artistically decorated by the workers of the mission and in a prominent position was hung a hymn board recent- ly presented by Mr J K Wingfield, MP. On Thurs- day the preacher was the Re v NJ Poole, and on Sunday the pulpit was occupied in the morning by the Rev Scholes in the afternoon, and in the evening by Mr 3 Millar, evangelist, Widnes. Special music was sung by thechoir. The offertories were devoted to the choir tund.Mr 14 Shaw presided at the organ. RUNCORN SONS OP TEMPERANCE.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the above order was held on Saturday evening in the Greenway-road School, under the presidency of Mr Thomas Heesom. Mr Thomas Whyte gave a short address, dealing with the position of the cades. One pleasing feature was that they are increasing in number, there being 144 en the book, with £lO2 23 53 in the bank. One of the auditorF,Mr Alfred Noden,gave his rep3rt, Istating that there were now in the adult order 322 members. There had been throe deaths during the quarter, ..C3O 63 had been paid for sickness, and is had been paid for accidents.—The three deaths were deplored, but in other respects the re- port was deemed satisfactory. CAMDEN WESLEYA'N CHAPEL, RUNCORN. The anniversary in connection with the above church was held on Sunday. The Rev H Morrison officiated in the morning.and at the evening service the preacher was the Rev II Morrison. The choir rendered with good effect the anthems " If ye love Him," and " Conquering Kings'—The services were continued on Monday, a concert being held at which Professor Ainsley, Miss Thomas, Alt CM., .Nlessrs It Johnson, Houghton, and Misses Timper- ley, Inman, Harrison and Woodward contributed vocal and instrum4atal sele3tims.—A tea pa ty was previously held at which Mesdames Beckett, Morris, Temperley, Harrison, Marsh and Misses Gleave, Woodward, Hughes, and Mr S Bazley were the tea-makers.</p>
        <p>ST. PAUL'S WESLEY GUILD. ..111111 The above Guild entered on its seventh session on Thursday eve,ling week, when a social and musical gathering was held in the Guild Parlour, Brunswick School. Mr E Taylor presided, and the at:endance was highly satisfactory. Mr W A Lightfoot provided the following prozramme. Duet," Life's Dream is o'er," Miss Maggie Hill and Miss McDonald ; reading,3lr William Jones ; song, "Maid of Malabar," Miss Maggie Hill ; pianoforte solo, Master W Williams, Eistelfodd prize winner, (encored) ; song, Miss Taylor ;:reading, Mr William Jones. The accompanists were Misses Baker, and Holt, and Mr W A Lightfoot. During an interval refreshments were partaken of. The question of forming a reading circle in connection with the Guild also came up for discussion, It was eventu- ally decided to do 80, the majority of members favouring this new departure, The arrangements are in the hands of the literarY committee.</p>
        <p>lIALTON WESLEYAN FOREIGN MISSIONS. The foreign missionary anniversary was cele- brated on Sunday] when the Rev C T Winter, a missionary from India whose services had been secured by Mr R ii Posnett, preached imitable ser- mons in the morning and evening. special anthems were rendered by the choir, Mr Sutton presiding at the organ. A public meeting was held on Mon- day night Mr Boston presiding, supported by the Rev C T Winter, H Morrison, It H Posnett, J Looker, and W Mocrei, secretary. The general re- port was submitted by the Rev H B Morrison and the local report by Mr Moores which showed': a gratifying improvement. Mr Winter afterwards gave a stirring address on the work accomplished in India dealing particularly with the famine. Toe collections at the services amounted to • .Ca more than last year.</p>
        <p>PROMOTION OF A RUNCORN CUSTOMS OFFICER Tll.l many friends in Runcorn and district of Mr J if Liamilton, the superintendent of Customs, will learn with pleasure that he has been promoted to the important position of collector and surveyor at Berwick-on-Tweed, the dutiee of which office he takes up in the course of the next few days. For the 'past 12 years Mr Hamilton has been the head of affairs at Runcorn, and his courteous and genial manner have endeared him to all who have had the pleasure of his acquaintance, Outside his purely official duties he has always shown a dis- position to help forward any movement for the development of the trade of the port and the town, For many years he has been a sidesman at the Runcorn Parish Church, and has taken an active interest in all parochial matters. While the an- nouncement of his promotion will be received with much pleasure, as being a most deserving one, Mr. Hamilton's departure from Runcorn will occasion much regret among those with whom his official position brought him in contact. RUNCORN POLICE COURT. it the Police Court on Saturday before Messrs F J Norman and A R Norman Henry Hassell for begging in Church-street on the previous day was sent to gaol for seven days.—At the same court William Disley was charged with stealing 231b5 of coal belonging to Messrs Simpson Davies on Saturday moring.—P C Gregory said that at 1.30 that morning he found the prisoner on board the boat "James," belonging to Messrs Simp- son and Davies, moored in the Bridgewater Canal, filling a sack with coal. Witness asked him who had given him pernais,ion to take the coal when prisoner replied that he was taking a bit to last him to Manchester.—Prisoner was captain of the flat "Sarah " belonging to Abbott and Co., which was moored near the other boat.—He took him into custody.—When charged prisoner replied that he had nothing to simile had workel for Abbott's about 13 years.—Mr Higgins, representing prosecu- tors, said he did not kEow the prisoner. They bad suffered considerable loss by coal being taken from their boats.—Prisoner was fined 203 or 14 days' hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-18_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>WANDERING LUNATIC..</head>
        <p>A WANDERING LUNATIC..</p>
        <p>At the Runcorn Police Court, on Wednesday, before Mr E Imison, Joseph Rowson was charged with being a wandering lunatic.—P.C. Bowyer said that on Tuesday night when on duty in Princess- streA he saw the prisoner's mother, who was crying out that her son had threatened to take her life. There had been numerous complaints about the prisoner, one that night from two ladies in High- street, who complained that prisoner was cursing and threatening his mother. He took him to the doctor's, who said he would certify him as a lunatic, and afterwards brought him to the police station. Several neighbours had left the neighbourhood in conseluence of his behaviour.—Mr Cooke, relievinz officer, said that prisoner's mother was afraid of him. He had been bothered with him for the last few years, and had been repeatedly to his mother's house about him. She had only 3s a week from the town to support her, and the great skulking fellow would do nothing to maintain her, Some times he stood a whole day against the chimney jamb and would not utter a word. Dr Bethune, assistant to Dr McDougall, said that he had seen prisoner several times. He was weak intellectually but he did-not think he was a lunatic nor were there any dangerous symptoms.—lnspector Egerton said that prisoner had deserted from the army two or three times. He had not been in the asylum. His manner was strange when brought to the station.—After consultation between the various officials it was decided to send the prisoner to the workhouse for 7 days where it is understood a medical examina,iln will mice place as to the state of his mind,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-18_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <p>m E EXAMINER Fill! )A V OCTOBER 181901.</p>
        <p>PURCHASE BY A RUNCORN COMPANY.</p>
        <p>The following appears in Friday's "London Gazette" under the head of "Voluntary Winding-up Notices ":—Matthews, Lancashire, Cheshire, and North Wales District White Lead Co. (the com- pany having sold and conveyed its undertaking and business to the Runcorn White Lead Company, Limited) A WOMAN'S ANKLE BROKEN AT WIDNES.</p>
        <p>At the Widnes Petty Sessions, yesterday morn- ing, Joseph Hursfield, a lamplighter, hying in James-street. was cbarg,ed with doing grievous bodily harm to Mrs Hatton, of James-strAet by striking her in the face which caused her to fall and break her ankle.—Superintendent Keighley said that on the 30th September. Hursfield was quarrelling ,with Mrs Hatton' married daughter in the street, and on the mother going to her daughter's assistance he struck her in the face and knocked her down. She •jell to the flaor, and when picked up was unconscious. On coming round she found her ankle broken.—A. re- mand was asked for until next Thursday, which was granted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-18_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>NOTES ON UAIVIES AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON UAIVIES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>RUGBY FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>By " Ovalist."</p>
        <p>There was nothing unexpected about the result of Saturday's match at Warrington, but Swinton's victory over the " Wire-pullers " was none the less a. bitter pill for the home supporters. Time was when Swinton found it just as difficult to win at Wilderspool as War- rington have found it difficult to defeat Runcorn, but for the last three seasons they have rudely ignored pre- cedent and trampled on the tender susceptibilities of the Warringtonians. The Blues' victories have always been by very narrow margins and have generally been gained with the aid of more than a little luck, and Saturday's game was no exception to what is fast be- coming the rule. Swinton won by a try to nothing, after having by far the worst of the game.</p>
        <p>I Such a result was-expected before the commencement ' of the match for Warrington placed a shockingly weak team in the field. No less than five good men were absentees, Fish being the most missed The others were four forwards, Boardman, Eden, Morrison and Fell.To find substitutes was no easy task and the fifteen which finally turned out was a very "scratch " organ- ization. Nevertheless the substitutes did remarkably well, particularly the forwards, who made up for lack of weight by superhuman exertions backed by great do. termination. Ratcliffe did many smart things but lie is not a Fish. Still he might easily have won the match if Isherwood had only entrusted him with some of the numerous penalty kicks.</p>
        <p>Right at the commencement of the game the War— rington men quite startled their despondent supporters by the bold front they showed. The front rank bustled the Blues off the ball in a twinkling and Swin- ton soon had to defend for all they were worth. It took them some time to clear out the invaders, but when they did Warrington had their hands pretty full. Though Dai Davies was absent from behind she pack the Swinton backs were very active and Lewis twice came as near scoring as he is ever likely to do without suc- ceeding. The first time he passed everybody but Hallam, who grassed the flier by a tackle which will live long in the memory of those who witnessed it. Next time Lewis got clear away. It seemed all over, though the Warrington men were coming across with every nerve strained to intercept the would-be scorer. But at the psychological moment when he had rounded the opposition Lewis stumbled in his stride ; and the ever-persevering Allen made a mighty effort and a huge shout arose as he got one hand on Lewis's shoulder and propelled him in touch. But for that stumble Warrington would have been two tries behind instead of one. Still it was all in the game, and half- time came with the score sheet blank.</p>
        <p>After listening to the usual fatherly admonitions during the interval Warrington went off again deter- mined to win. They swept the Blues back like so much chaff before the wind and initiated a hot attack on the line. Time after time they were robbed on the point of success, and once when Jolley crossed the line after a strong run by Cunningham it seemed particularly hard luck that the referee should disallow the try. Swinton showed their gratitude by immediately beating War- rington back. Valentine and Hampson got hold at centre and the latter raced in splendidly. This was Swinton's last visit to the Warrington half but it sufficed. The homesters made Titanic efforts to get even and Harris got in at the corner only to have his efforts nullified by the verdict of the referee. It was a very near thing but I believe the wing man was over the line when he grounded. Anyhow Warrington were not meant to win and they did not,</p>
        <p>Apart from having two tries disallowed, Warrington should easily have emerged triumphant. They had numberless kicks at goal from easy positions and why Isherwood should keep Hallam on was a mystery. Ratcliffe has far the better reputation as a goal-kicker especially for Straight shots and I feel sure he would have landed at least two goals had he been given half the chances Hallam got. It is about time serious atten- tion was paid to goal-kicking. Nothing seems to be done to encourage any of the younger players to prae• tise the art. Why if Dunbavin had been on the Warrington side on Saturday he would have won the match half-a-dozen times over. The ability to kick goals is not to be sneered at, as Saturday's match in- contestably proved.</p>
        <p>Warrington's defeat has placed thNn half-way down the competition table. Is this going to be their per- manent place I wonder 1' There does not seem much hope of getting 2 points out of Oldham to-day, but after Runcorn's fine feat last Saturday there is hope. It is not very long since Oldham came to Warrington with a record of ten successive victories and no defeats, but they went down at Wilderpool notwithstanding supreme confidence and the redoubtable Jack Hurst. More unlikely things might happen than that history should be repeated and Warrington once more spoil the Oldham men's unbroken sequence of wins. I fervently hope so. •</p>
        <p>-- In addition to Saturday's games there was a Northern League match on Monday, when Runcorn journeyed to Brighouse and defeated the Rangers by 3 goals, 2 tries (12 points) to 1 goal 1 try (5 points).</p>
        <p>The table up to date is as follows : For Against _ _</p>
        <p>I P WL D ~. G. 1' PTS ..• G T - PTO Oldham Broughton Rangers Hull - 41... 9 11-51 ... 3 Runcorn Leigh... 6 3 3 0 ... 9 7-39 ... 3 7- 27... 6 Hunslot *swlnton 4 3 1 0 ... 5 5-25 ... 3 2-12 ... 4 "Salford *Warrington Bradford Huddersfield Batley ... 1 6-20 ... 8 13-55 ... 2 Brighouse ... 5 3-19 ...15 2)-90 ... 2 afaxord m . Swint, and Warrington has° exell had IW)plio*aiintslf deducted for :re4acoh4ofoo..).e 2rut,13,-. 7 ... 9 10-48 ... 0</p>
        <p>--- There was little to choose between the teams on the day's play, and if anything the losers were the better lot. They showed the most dash and only lacked finish Swinton were not much better in the latter respect though they did get one good try. Valentine was the mainstay of the team and he bids fair to become as fine a player as his famous brother. He has a grand trick of bursting through the opposing backs and then giving his wing man a long pass. The latter's speed then does the rest. Warrington's centres might take a leaf out of young" Val's " book. That short dash and long pass to the outside man is almost irresistible. At half back, Warrington were slightly the best, Allen playing a really fine game, and Hockenhall improving much as time went on. Williamson on the Swinton side proved a smart understudy to Davies but his partner, Critch was not of much aspstance. Both sets of threequarters played raggedly at times, Harris heing, the best of the lot. He seems to make headway each week. Hallam made one mistake, but otherwise did splendid work.</p>
        <p>The County game at Broughton on the 26th should be an excellent one for Cheshire can put a good side in the field this season. The selections will be found in another column, Runcorn as usual supplying three- fourths of the team. Their combination may stand them in good stead but the Lancashire side is too brilliant to be beaten.</p>
