      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-18_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>RUNCORN COUNTY COURT.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN COUNTY COURT.</p>
        <p>THURSDAY.—Before his honour Judge Bowen Rowlands. illisllaND OR WIFE P--Messrs Toulman and Son, sued Mrs.Moores ttegent-street for £2 lls, for goods supplied in 1893. Defendant was represented by :Mr. Lake, soticitor. and plaintiffs by their traveller Mr John Ashton.—Plaintiff stated that his firm dealt in toys and fancy goods which;were supplied to Mrs Moores apart trom the jewellery business of her husband's. 114 had called every month in the year for tlae money and she had refused to give It to hirn.—By Mr. Lake: He had been with the firm Id months and had not supplied the goods to her personally. They were delivered by another traveller who had left the firm.—Mr LaCe said that tie articles had beers ordered by and delivered to Mr. Moores. Mrs Moores had really notlimg to do With the transaction at all. His honour said he would adjourn the case for a month so that evidence might be prodined by the plaintiffs to show that the articles had been nelivered to Mrs. Moores,</p>
        <p>PURCHASING ARTICLES WHEN ON HlRE.—Samuel Clef trailing as the Manchester Furnishing Company, High-street sued William Santley 3. Bridge-strest, watrmaa, tor 29 for a polished cheffonier which had been left on hire to one Walter 'limnerley.—Mr F A Lake appeared for the plaintiff and Mr J W Knowles for the defendant.—The facts of the case were that on the 22ad October last year a man named Walter Titnperley hired furniture including a chsff mier under a hired agreement. As he lied neglected to keep up the payments the plaintiff claimed for the return of the goods. Some of the things he recovered but the cheffonier was missing. It was afterwards traced to the defendant's and when plaintiff asked for its possession he was refused. On the 15th August he put it in the hands of his solicitors,Messrs Linaker. and Linaker. Mr Burton who then acted for the defendant panted out that It bad been bought in good faith and that full value had been given for it--Charles dpira deposed to selling tile goods aad stated that only El 13s 6d had been paid on the goods. lie asked defendant for the prme of furniture but he was relused.— Mr Knowles for the defence said that the defendant hadi tffered to return the article ia :question ; that a day had ' been appointed for him to take it away and that he never came for It —Bile .beth Santly, wife of the defendant said that on the 2nd August she had a letter trona Mr Spire saying he would call for the sideboard and appointing a time He never came but a week aftsr he called for It and she told him to take it away. He went away and never came back.—Cross-examined by Mr Lake : Her father-in law and brother-in-law were in the house at the first time of air Spire's visit and they were there for the pur- pose of helping him to get It out of the house. They were not Ithere to prevent it being taken away, Ashton Waterworth and Peter dentley, relatives ot the de- fen lantoleposed to being in the house on the first date mentioned. Plaintiff said he would call for it at 12 o'clock and they were in the home from 11 o'clock to 12.20. During that time he never called. The door was open all the time, Alice dantley, mother to the first witness, said that when Mr Spire called on the second occasion she heard her daugh- ter tell him to take the sideboard a way.--Ilis Honour held that the defendant had not made good their case. It was unreasonable to suppose that a man would go to the trouble of tr3ing to get it back and then refuse to take it away. Judgement for the plaintiff, the cheffonier to be brought away that day (Thursday). between two ant four o'clock.the oefendant giving every facility otherwise the suits of £3 to be paid by the defendant. Costs for the plaintiff on the 29.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-18_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>HORSE DEALING TRANSACTION.</head>
        <p>A HORSE DEALING TRANSAC- TION.</p>
        <p>At the Runcorn County Court beore His Honour Judge Bowen Rowlands ICC., Thomas Booth and Sons sued William Millington, Moore, for the sum of £l6 23 on a breach of warranty of a horse for which the plaintiffs had paid to the defendaot the sum of £2O se.—Mr Madden appeared for the plain- tiff and Mr Browne for the defendant. The facts of the case were that the horse had been bought by Thomas Booth from William Millington,and teat the horse had been represented as a tame animal, whereas it was a kicker. A warranty had been given that it was not a kicker --Thomas Booth, cab proprietor, ttuncorn,said that on the 10th June he went to buy a mare from the defendant, who lived at Moore. The first time he saw it his son put his hand on its buttock and it than kicked up its heels. Ws eon did not want him to have the horse,.but the defend- ant got him on one side and persuaded him to buy it for £2O ss. He said it was not a kicker and was a thorough good horse. After buying it witness found out that it was a very bad kicker which was not suitable for him at all. He wrote to the defendant acquainting him that the horse was not as good as represented and the defendant told him to put it out to grass and when Chester fair arrived to sJII it and thy would halve the loss between them. Ile did as suggested but could not sell it. Witness afterwards sent the mare to Lucas, Liverpool where it was sull for £6 153.</p>
        <p>Cross-3xamined by Mr Browne; The mare had not had a shiver but it had a swollen leg. Mr Bate attended the animal and bled it at the toe. He had never asked 35 guineas for it. When he bouzlit it the defendant said that he had bought it from .lesers Milling, Warrington, and that he could get its character from them.</p>
        <p>Arthur Booth, son of plaintiff, corroborated the previous witness' evidence and said that defendant said he would give £5 to the man who could make it kick. He would warrant it psrfectly quiet in all harness:—Cross-examined by Mr Browne :The mare was put on th. stand at the Runcorn station but it had not caught a 'cold through that. It had a swollen leg which was caused by dropsy and not through a cold. It had kicked previously to being bled by Mr Bate, farrier, who attended the mare. 'lorry Booth, cross-examined, denied emphati- cally that he had offered it to anyone at the Ship Inn " for the sum of £35. Fred Worrall and James Carruthers, corroborated to the kicking propensities of the mare. William Millington (defendant) denied that he used the words I warrant her quiet in any liar ness." Henry Booth told him at the " Ship Inn that the mare did not kick until it had a swollen foot.—Cross-examined by Mr Madden : The reason he wanted to sell her after having it only four weeks was that he had got his profit out of :ler. It had ploughed for him and had alsci been in the trap. It had never kicked while it was in his possession. William Devonport, foreman at Henry Milling Co , said he had bad the mare under supervision for eight years, it had never tricked. Joseph Foster, village farrier, Moore, said he had never seen the horse kick when in tha employ of ldr Millington. _ _</p>
        <p>John Malkin "Ship Inn" stated that he heard Rooth, jun., sot thas the horse had a cold which settled in the leg, which made it a kicker. Verdict was given for the plaintiff for the amount claimed with costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-18_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
