      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-18_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>TEM lIED LION, FULTON</head>
        <p>TEM lIED LION, FULTON</p>
        <p>The Inspector repn'ted that.nothiag had been done ,ragarding the viii ige ion nor with the nuisance exist- ing la the village, and that he had written to Mr Evans about the matter. Ile (the speaker) and the Superin- tendent inspected the premises, and they pointed out , that if there were na strpctural alterations the 11cen03 might be affected at the next, licensing day. The Chairman thought that the matter would be bcst left in the hands of the police and the chief brewery company, and a resolution to this effect was passed by the meeting. Mr Farrington announced that he had forwarded a sample of Helton water from the reservoir to the Laboratory of Chemical Pathology. The reply was to the effect that bacteriologically the water was a good supply which had becom3 slightly contaminated ia au accidental manner. It was decided to recommend the Riril Council to write the Urban Council of Runcorn asking that the reservoir should be cleansed. 1 PARISH COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>- ------ ----- A meeting of the above Council was held on Tuesday evenheg.—Present, toe Inemb2rs of th 3 flalton Parochial Commit:ee. W A Friar and A Woodier (clerk). The Clerk reported that nothing had been heard from the County Council respectiaet the proposed encroachment by Sir Richard Brooke. He expected a message from them shortly. Mr. Povey pointed out that the improvements at the cemetery had been satisfactorily accomplished and the place:nowipresect d a splendid appearance. Arrangement; had been made with the keeper to cus the grass &amp;3. Forty trees had been planted on the previous Saturday. The Clerk reported that sinc, tne cemetery had been opened there had been 274 interments. The aver- age call upon the overseers for the last six years had been £25 10i. Tao floancial statement for the Parish Council for the past year showed contribu- tions to the overseers £2B ; fees for the cemetery £24 164 61 which with the balance in hand mace a total of 52 10; 61. The payments included establishment charges £1 173; part principal repaid £l5 19; 21 ; interest £l3 93 2d ; gas £2 43 ; footpath £2 133 31 ; maintenance of grounds £l6 183, leaving in band a balance of £4 14s 81. The accounts for the overseers for the half year ending March 25th showed that the amounts received from the poor rates were £303 123 which i h the balance in hand of. £24 is 101 made a total of £327 133 101. The payments were 3—Contributions paid to the treasurer of the Union £154 ; to the Rural Council £100; to the Parish Council, £2B ; parliamentary regis- tration, £8 ; assistant overseer's salary, £25 ; sundries £1 163 81, leaving a balance of £lO 17s 41.</p>
