        <p>'WARRINGTON A Cabman's Sudden Death</p>
        <p>A cab driver named George 6nelding, :15 years of age, died suddenly on Tuesday morning. About eight, months ago he had a very severe attack of pneumonia which left him in a very weak state, and he had been unable to follow his employment since. On Monday evening his landlady left him sitting in the armchair in the kitchen. The folbw- ing morning on coming dowstuirs she found him still there and he was deal—An inquest was not consfdered neces:ary. Sir J. T. Brunner, M.P., and Education. 1</p>
        <p>Sir J 'l' Brunner, M.P, distributed the prizes to students attending, toe Leigh 'technical schools on iVednesday evening. In the course of his speech he said he was tilted with joy that he had lived in the day, which had witne-sed the awakening of the English people to the neceseity and great im- portance of an improvement in our system of edu- Cation. He pointed out that in Oa matter of edu- cation we were far behind Germany and the United States. He was a fanatical Englishman, and there- fore he hated being behind those two countries. He was of opinion that nothing was needed but to give the young people of Britain as good a chance as the Germane, and they could beat them. He strongly condemned the actioo of Parliament in only allow- ing them to levy a penny rate for a public library and a penny rate for technical instruction, and thought Close limitations ought in the interests of the country to be removed,</p>
        <p>Marriage of Mr F. G. Ruddock, F.I.C. A pretty wedding took place on ..sicurday at ths picturesque little church of St .Fetee's, Finstliwaite Windermere when Mr Frederick Orevile Ruddock F.I.C. of Wearington, was married to loliss Emily Mabel Keys-Wells,youngest daughter of the Rev W Keys-Wells, M.A., J.P.,of Scarborough,and formerly rector of Clifton, Westmorland. Itie officiating clergyman was the Rev S Inman, M.A., 'Vicar. The bride, who carried a shower bouquet of chry- santhemutns and white lilies, and was attired in white silk trimmed with white lace and chiffon j and real orange blossom, was given away by her brother, Mr Algernon Key-Wells, of Ne weastle-on- Ty ne in the unavoidable absence of her father. She was attended by her niece, Miss Macheil, of White- haveu,and the twin-daughters of Mr Alfred Rd dock, of Wolverhampton, as bridesmaids; the first- named laly being attired in a costume of red- flaked tweed, piped with white silk, and the twins in frocks of white corduroy. The bridegroom was supported by Mr James Young, of Warrington. The dejeuner took plucs at the Lakeshie Hotel after the ceremony. Amongst the guests were Colonel, and Mrs Machell, of Whitehaven ; Mr arid Mrs A E Ruddock, of Wolverhampton ; Mr T Newby-Wilson, J of the " Landing," Lakeside ; Mr J A NicGregor, of Warrington, &amp;c. Mr and Mrs F 0 Ruddock left for Scotland later in the day amidst many and hearty good wishes from their friends. The J presents were numerous and costly. Mr. Charles H. Pigot's Concerts. • ••• •</p>
        <p>As will b 3 seen from our a—dvertisiug columns Mr. C H Pigot has organised a high-class concert to be given in the Parr Hall on the 29:h bast. The list of artistes includes the weil-knowa names of Madams Alice Esty. Miss Marian McKenzie, Mr James Ley- land, and Mc. Watkin Mills as vocalists, and Miss Janotha as pianist, and Miss Nadia Sylva as violin- jet. An important and novel feature oi this concert will be the inclusion in the programme of Liza Lehmann's Sang Cycle," Ths Daisy Curtin," which will divide the usual programme of vocal and in- strumental music, thus making a very desirable , departure from the well-worn routine of the ballad concert programme. The opportunity will be given of hearing " lhe Daisy Chain" under the happiest conditions, the irreproachable quartette al- ready mentioned having been got together so that the work may be given to jorfeetion. Mi,s Liza Lehmann's exquisite new work is a delightful series of songs and quartettes woven together in the form of a cycle, anu tet to the lyrics of Robert Louis Stevenson, Alma Tademit, and others. It was publicly performed for the first time in England at the Q ieen's Hall, London, on January 13th, 1901, with tuu most brilliant success, th 3 composer being rapturously caged again and again to the platform. The cycle occupies in per- formance twenty-five minutes to half-an-hour, and is one of the most charming little works of recent times, and will doubtless captivate the many pro- vincial concert-goers who will ba privileged to hear it.</p>
        <p>Liberal Club Smoking Concert. A tea end smoking coucett was held at the Club last Saturday in connection with the presentation ot prizes (given by Mr A II Crostield) to the suc- cessful compeitors of the bowling main held on the 31st August. About 50 members at down to a knife and fork tea which was catered for by the manager of the Club and afterwards 'the concert was proceeded with. The chairman proposed a vote of thanks to the donor (A H Crostield) which was heartily carried by the singing of " He's a jolly good fellow." The programme was as follows:—Song. J Draper ; recitation, E A Sanders; song J Hum- phrey; whistling solo,W Whittaker ; comic sonz. W Whitham ;song,A Ureenhalgh. During an interval in the programme Councillor Tinnion presentsd the prizes as follows :—Annual main : 1, George Strange, lady's chain : 2, G 1-1 Shaw, marble clock ; 3 L Ackland, sets of bowls ;4,R W hit field,tratelling bag; 5, F Rigby, lady's ring; 6, R Marrow, cruet; 7, C H Dibbert, bedroom clock ; 8, 11 Daintetii, teapot; 9, T Critchley butter dish ; 10, Benjamin Hall, carvers ; 11, W Daintith, carvers ; 12, J Rigby, jam dish. Main on August 31st: 1, J Houghton, marble strik- ing clock; 2, A Greenhalgh, silver-plated kettle and stand ;3, G 11. Shaw, case of carvers ; 4, T dinner cruet ; 5, J Holmes, biscuit barrel ; 6, It Marrow, eig stand ; 7, C U Ilibbert, barometer. 8, EJ Mitchell carvers. Consolation prizes : 1, W Patrick, teapot; 2, W Rooke,jam dish ; 3,J Lawton, catvers :4, W Stevenson, breakfast cruet. H. M ASHTON'S UNE TONIC IS THE KING OF TONICS.-63. SANKET.STREET WatT1110011.—(AtIVI). UNDHH THE CLOCK FOR GOOD BOOFS.—Adv "The Senses."</p>
        <p>Professor W Stirling, M.D., of the Owens College Manchester, revisited Warrington on Tues- day evening and delivered the first of a series of lectures on " The Senses ; sight, hearing and I touch." It will be remembered that last year Pro- fesor Stirling delivered a course of lectures in the Cc-operative Hall on " Food and Digestion," under the auspices of the Combo Trust, and the trustees of that trust have again favoured Warrington by granting a second course of lectures. There was an encouraging attendance in the Co-operative Hall on Tuesday to hear tie opening lecture .The Mayor (Alderman Roberts) presided and tie stated that it gave him great pleasure to intro- duce Professor Stirling who intended ti lecture on the eye,—Professor Stirling, who had a cordial reception, expressed his pleasure at revisiting Warrington and stated that the subject of the present course of lectures was far removed from the subject of the last one. Prior to proceeding with the subject of the lecture " Bow the eye is protected,"the lecturer gave some general observations on the structure of the central nerv- ous syatem in vnau and pointed out tie functions of the sense organs and the nerves. By means of the sense organs he said, they had a whole series of avenues through which in•pulses of one kind or another were conveyed to the mind and hence they had been called in the quaint languag,e of Wilson "the gateways ot knowledge. The principle was that they bad organs on the surface on which light, pressure and sound could act. Trio light pressure and sound .did. not ' reach the brain, but they sat up v tbration in a mysterious and unknown way. In this way we had a nerve impuh:e and the seusatins we obtained through the eye were amongst the most important we possessed. Tnere were two distinct kinds of sensations obtained through the eye—the sensation of light and the power to distinguish colour. Our brain was a great receptacle into which, long before birth, impulses av, re steadily streaming in through the avenues of kinvvledge and the resultant was the building tin of knowledge in the individual who practically started with a clean slate and then had entered uvula it those impulses . which came through the sense organs. The cells of the brain were soft and plastic in early years and as one got older they became less plastic and less receptive Hence the necessity of obtaining knowledge in early youth when the brain cells were soft and plastic, and were able to receive impressions through the senses and retain them. That was the fundamental point in relation to education. The action of the nerves upon the mus- cles was demonstrated and the lecturer proceeded to speak of the eyeball and how it was protected.lfe showed the wisdom displayed in the construction of the eyelids so as to exclude light when necessary, and explained their structure. The eyelashes and the lachrymal, or tear apparatus, were fully de- scribed, The eye as an organ of expression and in portraiture was Shown by means of lantern views, and by the same aid the eyes of the lower animals were illustrated. The lecture was also illustrated (by means of diagrams and experiments. ,</p>
        <p>Fatal Result of a Fall. I Mr. _Brighouse, douuty coroner, conducted an inquiry at the Police SLacion on Wednesday evening respecting the death of Mary Davies, 71 years of age,who resided at 17, Lovely-lane.lt appears that at half-past e'ght o'cl ,ck on aturday morning de- ceased was in the kitchen,when she was seized with faintness and tell to the floor. Dr Joseph was called , in and he found her suffering from a broken thigh. He attended her until her death on Monday morn- ing. When asked by ner dau4hter what she had been doing to cause her to tall, she replied that she was putting Cal s on the the when she went mazy and tell.—A verdict of "Accidental death " was returned.</p>
        <p>Ren. F. Slater Leaves 0 ver.—A Chaplaincy -in</p>
        <p>Ge: many. The Rev F Slater, vicar of St Chad's, Over, Cheshire, has addressed a letter to his parishioners. intimating that he has accepted the chaplaincy of Heidelbsrg (Germany), aud in bidding farewell to his Cheshire friends, cflers to present to St Chad's a silver chalice and paten in memory of his wife and of his son-in-law's deliverance from Lady- smith. For upwards of twenty years, Mr blater was vicar of St James', Latchford, precioui to accepting the living of Over.--rue rev gentleman's letter to the congregation at Over is dated Heidelberg, October 13.11, and is addres,el to " 3ly dear good people of Ocer." Mr Slater has been appointed ti the permanent chaplaincy at Heidelberg by the :Society for the Propagation of the Gospel.</p>
        <p>A Farm Labourer's Shocking Death. Au inquest wee held at the Police t,tatioa, War- rington, on Wednesday evening; into the circum- stances atten ling the death of Timothy Harney, , Battery houses, Martinscroft, who died in the War- rington Union hospital on Monday. It appe.arel from the evidence that the deceased lived with a widow named lune Walsh. lie was employed as a farm labourer, and was 63 years of age. He went to his lodgings on Saturday night, and after having his supper retired for the purpose of going to his bedroom on the third storey. Ilia landlady heard I him ascending the second flight of stairs, and was then startled by the sound of a fall. She pro- ceeded up the stairs, And found him lying at tha bottom of the second staircase. He was conscious when picked up, andlfsaid "I am done." He was then put to tied, and on the following morning was crdered t) the Workhouse Ho•ipital by Dr Pea- cocke. In a latter to the Coroner, Dr Braide siated that he examined the deceased immediately alter his admittance to tie hospital and fund him suffering from a fractured epine.—Nurse Cronshaw stated that the man died at 25 on Monday morning.--The jary returned a verdict of 'Accidental death."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-18_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>CONGREGATIONAL UNION.</head>
        <p>THE CONGREGATIONAL UNION.</p>
        <p>Autumnal Assembly in Manchester.</p>
        <p>The meetings of the Autumnal Assemhly of the Con- gregations Union of England and Wales were opened on Tutiday in the Free-trade Hall, Mancbester. The Rev Dr Joseph Parker was the chairman. In Ilia ad_ dress Dr Parker spoke of the federation of Congregaa tionalisrn tnroughout he world under the generic tot of the United Congregational Church. He said that h wanted to interest the whole of the cnurche ins tta great work ot Cougrtgationalisrn. To do this tb churches wanted an ideal, some great uniting policy, and he thought they would din I it in the United Coogregational Ceurch. They must consult one another as to what was most likely to give Congregationalism a large place and a deeper influence in the new re ntury. The UeEed Church must be so constituted as to assume distinct respocsi- talky as to every one of its ministers. In creating the United Congregational Cnurch the autonomy of local churches need not be disturbed in any degree. As [nibs 'constitution of such a church, Dr Parker said that all their iustitutious, colleges, societies, and the like must be taken, to use a cornm,!rcial phrase, as "a going con- cern," and in the first instanct trio ministerial list must be taken as it stood. N) change in any department must be violently or needlessly made. The whele pre- liminary action mur be toe act and deed of eh 3 Cengre- gational Union of England and Wales. Tney must know nothing ab lilt externally super .eding the Union. They must create such a body of upinion as would justify the Union in going to the Chaiity Commissioners or other authority and asking for power to change the name and appropriate the funds of the Congregational Union. Tony simply continued and expanded the Work oh chose who went before them. A resolution was passed direct- icg, the Cornmlttee of the Union to bring the whole subject before the churches and the county unions, and to prepare a report to be submitted to the issembly of the Union at the earliest possible date. A letter of welcome signed by the Bishop of Manchester and one hundred members of the Established Ceurch was read, and a deputation representing the Association of Evangelical Free Churches of Manchester and Salford also roresented an address v.elcoming the members of the Union to Manchester. In the evening a public meeting was held in the Fr .e Trade Hall.when addresses were delivered by Mr Batty Langley, MP., Mrr c Taylor. M.P., and others on Congregationalism. On Wednesday a special ho iness meeting was held in the morning in the Central Hall. Dr Parker was the chairman. Mr George Spicer made a statement on the Congregational Pastors' Retiring Fund, and this gave rise to a discussion. A section of the Union maintained that the time had come for an amalgamation of the hands available for ministerial pensions and for arrangements wlsereby each subscribing minister shall be able to claim an annuity as a right on his retirement. The question was left over for further consideration and in order to see what comes of the more important movement towards consolidation breueht bAore the Union by Dr Parker, A resolution was submitted in favour of a reoreenisation of the Union with county associations as its units instead cf churcheS. The argument was that in this way the Union would be made more authoritative because more representative. After discussion the resolution and au amendment were withdrawn in response to a sugges- tion that nothing should be done to hinder the consid- eration of the proposal respecting a United Congrega- tional Church. Various sectional meetings were held in the afternoon. Tnese included a. womeu's m eting, a conference on the relation of the colleges to one another and to the Union, and one on the superannuation of ministers. In the evening there was a crowded meeting in the Free-trade Hall to bid farewell to 29 missionaries who are about to return to foreign mission werk or enter the mission field for the first time. Dr. Parker delivered the valedictory address.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-18_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>NOTES BY THE WAY AT EARLESTOWN.</head>
        <p>NOTES BY THE WAY AT EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>Pi A CORRESPONDRNT].</p>
        <p>FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>For the second week in succession not a match was down for cheision in this (bogie:, ou Saturday. Owing to the resignation of Doncaster Rovers from ' the Lancashire League, Earlestown ban an open date, as the Rovers should have visited the Mesneo To-morrow, Stalybridge Rovers visit the locils and taking into consideration the thrashing Haydock administered to the champion last .week, Earles- town ought to chronicle their first win ot the season. Scalybridge provided Earlestown with the solitary point they can lay claim to as the opening encounter of the season restitted in a pointless draw. if the locals manage to gain the manimum number of points, they will do more than the majority of the Lineishire League candidates will do and that is, take three points out of the cham- pions. Earlestown will have their best team out, although theylnave not yet decided as to who shall take the outside left position, Oreenhalgh has not given satisfaction and they are at present negotiat- ing for the transfer of a well-known fiver. Other— wise the forward line will be the same as usual, the right wing being Bartley and Barlow whilst Daw- son will act as the pivot, Leach taking his usual position at inside left. The only thing tlit is wanted is a record attendance and seeing that the local supporters have not up to the ptesent had an opportunity of siieing their pots perform,this ought to be assured them.</p>
        <p>Newton were again hunting on Welsh soil, and again were sent home pointless. . They managed to improve on their previous week's display ,however, as alter having quite as much ot the game as their opponents they were unluckily defeated by three goals to nil. Perhaps some will say that there is not much bad luck abut a thiee clear goals defeat, but anyone viewing the game would admit that. "Jack was as good as his master." Chirk made far better use of their openings, never hssitating to turn anything like a chance to good account. Their shooting all through the game was extremely dead- ly. Horrocks playing just as good a game as ha did • bad the previous week. To-morrow Rhyl provide the menu at Nursery Park.As Newton mean to avenge their crushing defeat of a fortnight ago, a rattling game may be anticipated. Last season Rhvl visited Nursery Park, they were going strong- ly for the championship and two points would have assured them of the cup,but Newton put the damp- er on their aspirations by defeating 'tune of three goals to nil. them to ttua</p>
        <p>.</p>
