      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-18_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES</head>
        <p>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES</p>
        <p>THEFT OF A BICYCLE AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Rlrough Police Court on Wednes- day, lo !fore Alderman ST:ton (in the chair) and Mr J R Young, James Crain Grant, an engineer, of no fixed abode, was charged wit a stealiuz a gentleman's bicycle valued at £5 the property of Frederick Jones, at War- rington on Monday afterno m. He pleaded guilty.— Superintendent Monaghan said the prosecutor was an assistant in the shop of Measrs Milling and Company, grocers. On Monday afternoon at one o'clock he put the bicycle at the rear of the premises. and on going for it at four o'clock found it was gone. He gave informa- tion to the police and the usual information was sent to other towns. The prisoner turned up at Chester at eight o'clock, and oilseed the bicycle for sale to William Webb, a bicycle dealer. Mr Webb was somewhat sus- pinions and asked pris met to leave the machine there until the following day. Prisoner did sl and when he went to the shop on Tuesday he was arrested. He was afterwards handed over to P C Clough of the Warring- ton police. He had had x month's imprisornent previously for stealing a bicycle at Liacard.—Prisoner was sent to gaol for a month.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-18_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>MINOR OASES.</head>
        <p>MINOR OASES.</p>
        <p>Warrington Borough Court.</p>
        <p>.11,1n5.V.—Before M. Monks (!ra the chair), Aldermen Heaton and Hutchinson, Councillor Smethursl,and Messrs Haywa-d. Wright and. Th ,rnton. AN OLD OFFIENDER SENT T.) GAOL.—Br;d4et White, 'alias Lily. was charged with stealing on Saturday night, a cardi4an jacket. the prwerty of Thomas Bowe. 54, Aorsemarket-street, It appears that r.C. Clough was on duty in the street when be saw the prisoner loite:ing about the shy in a suspicious manner. Knowing her to be an old offender he watoned her. and saw her go to the door and pick up the jacket pro ince I end walk away with it. He , followed her RSI,I charged her with the offence which she ' admitted —The Chief Constable said that this was her 54th appearance She had been before the Court many times ter thefh haying served the maximum penalty toe that offense. —She was now committed to gaol for a month with hard</p>
        <p>labour- SINGULAR OCCURRENCES IN BUTTERSSAMSET-sTREEC Thomas Baguley ana Sarah Ana 134guley, living In Tay:BB- - were charged with having been, guilty of disorderly b.haviour in Buttermarket-street on the 3rd October.—P C Rigby stat?cl that the defendants were behaving in a dis- orderly manner, and the woman pulled a gentlemen off a bicycle.—John Henry Fisher, solicitor's articled clerk, Fairfield House. Manchester-road, said that on the day In question he was cycling down Buttermarket-street on his way home, when he was Interfere i with by the prisoners. Ths woman was running awas from her huevuld and was shouting and screaming at the top ot her vole-% She celled to witness to save her, at the same time pulling him off his bicycle, which was slightly damaged, and knocking his hat off. The husband caught his wife. At this time the police csnse up and reauested witness to give his name, which he did—The Chairman She was appealing to you for nrotec• tion ? Witness replied in the affirmative. —The Chief Con- stable sal a that the male prisoner was one of the worst men they had In town, and he had been brought before the Bench no fewer theme 32 times. on the let ocsasion being sent to wt.:V. for a monto ie was now bound over to keep the peace and ordered to pay the costs, 12s. The woman was die-</p>
        <p>charged ONLY ONE SLTP.—TClirtheth Holbrook was brought up on remand. charget with neglecting her ehildren.—Mr Browne. on behalf of the N S.P.C.C. said the case was before the magistrates on the 19th August and adjourned at the re- quest of the husband and of the defendant, who promised to amend her/conduct. She had only made one slip during the adjournment. and the socity were prepared to give her another chance. If there wee no silo during the next month be would undertake on behalf or the Society to withdraw the case.—The case was again adjourned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-18_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>Warrington County Petty Sessions.</head>
        <p>Warrington County Petty Sessions.</p>
        <p>WEDNEADAY.—Before Messrs Comber On the chair)and UNIsHABITABLE CABIN.—SIessrII Burton and Son (owners) and James Lymm (servant) were summoned for having at Sankey, on the 17th September, alto sed a canal boat cabin to be in an uninhabitable condition.—Mr. Frank Kerfoot. inspector to the Rural District Council, stated that the boat was laden with manure. The cabin was defective as the woodwork was rotten and the rain came through into the lied berths. -A fine Of Ss aad casts Was inflicted in each rase. ASSAULT OSI • LITTLE Boy AT PENES:TH.—hash Lloyd by, was charged with assaulting another lad, George Car- rington, at Penlceth. on the 12th October:—The complainant was too young to give evidence.—Clara Thompson stated that si,e saw the defendant kick Carrington.—tdrs. Carring- ton said her son Caine in crying an 4 said 4efendent had iricke4 him. She went to defendant and he made use of bad language and said ••be would serve her the eame."—Def en- cant was lined in the costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-18_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>Runcorn Petty sessions.</head>
        <p>Runcorn Petty sessions.</p>
        <p>-- Monar —Before Messrs. AR. Norman (in the chair) and R. ft. Posnett. CHIMNEY ON FlFo7.—WilliaGl Dutton, Robert-street was ordered to pay the costs 4s 61 for having allowed nis chimney to be on fire on the 27th September. OEiCENE LANGUAGE —S.trouel Dutton lia§ fined 10s. in- cluding costs cc In default 7 days for using obscene language on the sth inst. A Comic SiNo-ca If TROUBLE.—A. one legged man named Thomas itspinall who wore a medal was charged with being drunk arid disordetly in Church-street at 11 p.m. on the 12th October.—The evidence showed that prisoner was a comic singer who travelled from town to town.—On priscner pro- mising to leave the town he was discharged. DRUNE.—EIIen Keats, married woman was charged with being drunk and disorderly on the lath inst.—Her husband a respectable looking man, said they were on their way to lifot.logham,and on promising to leave the town the prisoner was discharged. BEGGlNG.—Eliztbeth Chalwi;li was discharged for begging on the 13th inst on promising to leave the town,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-18_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>Local Harriers at Earlestown.—A Successful</head>
        <p>Local Harriers at Earlestown.—A Successful</p>
        <p>Meet.</p>
        <p>A successful meet of hell harr:ers t3ok plale at; Earlestown on Saturdsy. Prizes were offered by Mr Beatty, of the Griffin Hotel. from which place the run took place. Teams from most of the harrier clubs of the surrounding district entered, and repre- sentatives were to be seen from Sankey, Warring- ton, Earkostown, Sutton, and St. Helens Sacred Heart. A prize of a box of cigars was offered for the biggest turnout, and the count showed that Sankey were the successful club, turn- ing out 32, against 30 of the Sacred Heart, A start was made al-out four o'clock, and the muster, numbering 99, went off at an exzellent pace. Out of town the pace slackened, and it soon became evident that the runners did not mean to exert themselves until the time for ,the main item —the mils break—arrived. After a spin of about seven miles, the party lined up at the old Newton racecourse, for the dash home. Webber, of Sutton, went off as a rattling pace, followed by Mather,of Warrington. Webber maintained his lead and won somewhat easily by about fifteen yards. Whittle, of sacred Heart harriers, came next, followed by Mather (Warrington), and Devlin (Sankey). A big batch, practically all Sankey men followed, that club winning quite easily on points.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-18_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>REASONS FOR CONGREGATIONAL NONCONFORMITY.</head>
        <p>REASONS FOR CONGREGATIONAL NONCONFORMITY.</p>
        <p>Sermon by the Rev. John Yonge.</p>
        <p>The services at Wycliffe Congregational Church par- took of a special character on Sunday in celebration of the Jubilee ot Wycliffe Chapel. There were large con- gregations. At the morning service, the Rev. John Yunge based an excellent discourse on the words " Blessed is the people that know the joyful sound." Psalm 89, v. 15. The choir gave an exceptionally fine rendering ot " Gloria'' in 11,-,zart's XII. Mass. At the eveoing service, the choir sang the hallelujah Chorus. The pastor preached an able discourse, iu which he expounded the reasons tor Cmgreg,ational Noncon- formity. The text was taken from 1. Peter, 3 chapter part of verse 15:—" Be ready always to give an Answer to every man tha; asketb you a reason," &amp;o. They had given, h., said, a special character to that Sunday. Fit,y years ago this mouth—in October 1851— the first Wyciiffe Chapel was opened for worship. That was</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-18_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>DAY OY NEW BEGINNINGS</head>
        <p>A DAY OY NEW BEGINNINGS</p>
        <p>for Cingregatioaal Nonconformity in tnis town: They need not go back now to the history of the earlier Con- gregational movements, because they had come to aa end, and the new " Wycliffe" was an entirely fresh start. The new movement fifty years ago began under what were deemed, and what had since proved to have been very nopeful auspices. The actual circumstances had been recited so often that he did not propose to repeat them at any length now. This was the most unnecessary because the editor of the" Ex- amine," in the previous day's issue gave an admirable sketch of their Wycliffe history, and cne which was worth preserving log any who were not al,eady in posses- sion of the historical facts. The fact, however,deserved rep !ated mention that the Wyciiffe Cause, as they knew it tc-day,owed its origin to the enterprise and generosity to the Lancashire Congregational Union. That body,in 1847,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-18_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>COMMENCED PUBLIC .CONG REGITIONAL. SERVICES_</head>
        <p>COMMENCED PUBLIC .CONG REGITIONAL. SERVICES_</p>
        <p>in the Assemb:y room of the Nag's Head Inn, S mkey- street. Leading ministers of the denomination in that day, the late Dr Vaughan, Principal of Lai:mask:Use College. Dr Halley, of Cavendish Chapel. Manchester, R.ffi 3s, of Gres; George-street, L‘verpool, John IVfllf, also of Liverpool, and William Roaf, of Wigan, with others, were toe sponsors of the movement. Toe servicEs proving speedily successful, the Rev P A Hatnpson settled as minister, and during his ministry TIII3 FIRST T7YCLIFFIL " WAS RCTILT</p>
        <p>in 1851. A succession of ministers held the pastorate from 1851 to 1868, when the late Rev. G. S. Heaney, formerly a }Slut's% began his Congregational ministry. During his time, Wycliffe rapidly increased until its expansion compelled the erection of the Church wherein they were now assembled. Now, he repeated what he bad said on p•evioui ceeasions, th it Wycliffe as it had bees since 1873, and as it was at this day, was a monument to the vigorous ministry of Mr Reaney. aid also to the industry, devotedness, and munificence of the men who were then connected with the church, but many of whom were n 397 either removed to other parts or transferred to the Church above. It was to the memory et these latter friends that</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-18_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>NEW ECTILDINCii</head>
        <p>THE NEW ECTILDINCii</p>
        <p>were now in cows, of erection. In some senees it appeared ievidious to mentioe names. but they recalled inevitably the names of Mr and Mee and Miss Rieby,Mr McMinnies,Mr Wm.Walliogtoo,Mr J Stelfex,Mr R Roberts as having been generous supporters au e devoted work- ers in the interests of the church. I; would be easy and perhapl interesting to fill up time by a review of the present pastorate of 25 years, but it was question- able whether the present speaker was the best fitted for such an attempt. To him, at any rate, these 25 years had been the b?st and happiest of his Ifte, and he cherished the hope that tee had "not laboured in vain, nor spent his strength for nougat." There was one thing, however, which it might be permitted him on this subject to aid and it was this: that IF WYCLIFFE WAS TO MAINTAIN ITS PRESENT POSITION and if, as a cherch, it was to increase in iefluence and usefulness, it could only be by means of new and increased devotion and sacrifice on the part of all its People. Remember they had now to contend against greater competition (if in sech a connexion that word was administered at all) than in yeers gone by. There were more places of worship and mere Sunday schools,and although these might be only in proportion to the increase ofpopulation, still as a church in the midst of a town, Wycliffe had not only to reckon with these multiplied religious organizttione,but also with a steady and growing removal to the suburbs of those who had been among its best workers and most regular attend- ante. It was uaeless to protest or even to murmur against this tendency of the time. But they had Co face facts, and it would be one good use of this jubilee occasion, if their people who still continued to live around them,would resolve to augment their industry and the regularity of their attendance, so that Wycliffe might continue to fieuriah,acd even become more useful than ever. He pleaded with them that night, and! specially with the younger of them, to give their Church and their school their heartiest possible sup- port, and to do everything they could to further the welfare of Wycliffe, even if it should be at the cost of self-denial. Met as they were that day on the 50:h aneiversary of Wycliffe, it could not be untimely to re- mind themselves of</p>
        <p>THE REASONS FOR THE EXIiTENCE OF WYCLIFFE CIIERCII Specially it was well that their young people Should have adequate and ready knowledge on these matters because they would be asked from time to time by mem- bers of other religious communities why they belonged to Wycliffe ? Why they were Nonconformists ?Why they did not go to the Anglican Church ? Consequently they needed to be able to say why they were Nonconformists and not Churchpsople ; and why they were C -,ngrega- tionalists and not Methodists or Presbyterians, or Unitarians. They needed simp'e,clear intelligent views about these things not only to be able to answer enquiries but also for the better satisfaction and assur- ance of their own minds. He wanted Wycliffe people and especially the young4r among them to possess clear but strong convictions as to the principles that underlay Nonconformity and Congregationalism. WHY WERE THEY IVNCONF )RMISTS ?</p>
        <p>In the matter of religion the Protestant people of this country were divided into two great ecclesiastical divisions—membets of the church by law established and members or adherents of what were commonly de- soribi d both north and south of the Tweed as the free churches. Popular phraseology abbreviated these titles of diEtinction and spoke of Church people and Dissenters 'or Church people and Nonconformists. The term Dissenter strictly denoted a !Jerson who dissented from disagreed with, repudiated the principle of a State Eitablisbed Church. He might not, as matter Of opinion, disapprove of the Episcopal Church, either in respect of its ecclesiastical polity, its ceremonial ritual, or its doctrine. But he did disapprove of its being connected by legal enactment with the State. ar d he therefore dis- sented because he disbelieved that Corist intended His Church to be in any way joined to. bound, ruled, fetter- ed by any State political authority. He remembered that the Lord said "My Kingdom is not of this world," and he remembered also that the, Lnrd never gave His disciples any instruction whatever that justified their placiog themselves as His followers and ae:vants under any ecclesiastical subjection of any kind to the "powers of this world." Consequently he dissented from the State Church. as an institmion having no sanction from the Church's Head. moreover, he would dissent equally from the Congregational Church itself if by any means it could become connected with the state in the same way as the Episcopal Church was now. The intelligent and conscientious Dissenter would have nothing to do with a politically e3tablishei or politically endowed church. Thus in Scotland ever since the great disruption of the last century. had been seen the spec- tacle of dissenting churches, all or most of which had ec3lesiastically boon of the same faith amd polity as the sc-called National Church of Scotland. The dissent was from the principle of a state established church. The term Nonconformist went furtlvr, and included more than the term Dissenter—at any rate in England. The</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-18_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>ESTABLISHED STATE CHURCH</head>
        <p>ESTABLISHED STATE CHURCH</p>
        <p>in this country was the Episcopal. which was presided over by titled prelates with seats in the Houee of Lords, by priests with priestly and sacerdotal claims, and a very variegated and manifold ordor of clergy. It pre- scribed a very varied, elaborate, and ceremonial ritual of worship and ordinance. It required its members to observe this ritual ; to coofofm to its practices, and obey its mandates. It enjoined and enforced a fixed liturgy in the worship of God. It discouraged, if it did not prohibit, the use in public of free prayer. It gave spiritual supremacy to the clergy and bade the laity submit themselves thereto. The Nonconformist declined to conform to these rules and regulations, these rules and ceremonies, these claims and assumptions for various reasons, but con spicuously because he found no warrant for them in the word of God. He sought as his spiritual home a simpler dwelling—a simpler outward form and fashion, a simpler doctrine, which all tended in his belief to better help his worship and his life. When to the old and early days, statesmen and churchmen s-rove to conform, to force his acceptance of a cidurch contrary to his convictions of truth; the Nonconformist struck , for</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-18_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>SPIRITUAL LIBERTY</head>
        <p>SPIRITUAL LIBERTY</p>
        <p>and let come what might he refused conformity ; re- fused to surrender his conscience to the bidding of any , external power whatever ; refused to bow the knee in prayer b-fore any altar that contravened his belief ; refused to accept the ministry of any order of clergy that claimed supremacy over his convictions and very specially did he refuse to accept or sanction any ser- vices of a priestly order who presumed to stand between his soul and Christ. The preasher proceeded to reflex' to the splendid history of Dissent aril Non- conformity and asked the young people to remeinter that Nsnconfortnity in the centuries past meant fine, imprisonment, torture, death itself, in multitudes of instances. But the Nonconformist men and women of those days faced and dared and endured all these penal- ties in vindication of the rights of conscience and of spiritual liberty. Tc-day we incurred no such dangers.</p>
        <p>We were free to dissent, we were free to decline con- formity. Never, however, let it be forgotten that the principles of Dissent and toe reason for Nonconformity were as actual as ever. Proceeding to an exposition of Congregationalism, the preacher said</p>
        <p>THEY WERE CaNGREGATIONALISTS because they believed that the New Testament distinctly taught the essentially and exclusively spiritual charac- ter of the Christian Church. The New Testament said nothing to indicate that the church of Jesus Christ could consist of a nation or of a hierarchy, but it said everything to indicate that it did consist and consisted only of Christian believers. There was no precept ordain- ing any form of church erganization sr government, but the early church( s, foueded by the apostles, and their helpers were unmistakeably of the Congrega- tional character societies of convinced and converted believers in Jesus, meeting for wor- ship, for Christian work and spiritual fellowship' managing their own affairs, choosing their own mem- bers, and officers and ministers, without any external interference from any quarnr. This was the funda- mental reason for their Congregationalism—because- they were confident that it was nearest the apostolic model and the early Christian practice. Other reasons could be given. This, however, would serve the present purpose. As they Came more fully to realise the principles of d their C irwresri- tiorsalisin they would become more steadfast in their adhesion to them and more true to the great cause that Church represented.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-18_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>NEW PO-LICE COURT. ---- Mayor Receives White Gloves.</head>
        <p>THE NEW PO-LICE COURT. ---- Mayor Receives White Gloves.</p>
        <p>The State of Crime in Warrington:</p>
        <p>A ceremony of extrem,ly rare occurrence in Warring- ton, was enacted at the County Bmough Police Court on Saturday morning. The new court in Whoa Patten- street was used for the first time,and it was a pleasing circumstance that there were no ptisoners for trial. The event was marked by the presentation to the Mayor by the Chief Constable of a pair of white g'oves. The magistrates on the Bench were :—Aldermen Elute hinson and Heaton, Councillors Smethurst and John Ito bens. end Messrs J W Hayward T Fairclough, S Chan dley, J R Young, and H Thorn- ton. The Town Clerk and Deputy ro wn Clerk, the Magistrates' Clerk, the Chief Constable, Mr Taylor (police court missionary), and Councillor Bennett were a:s o present. The CHIEF CONSTABLE said his first duty in the new Police Court, he was happy to say, was a very pleasant one indeed, and it was to inform the magistrates that there was absolutely no case for trial, a circumstance wnich was very unusual on a Saturday morning in this town. And,then, in pursuance of is custom—which was somewhat unusual in Warrington, he believed, as it had not b :en carried out for 33 years at least, but which was common enough in other towns—he had to ask the Chairman's acceptance at his hands, through the Clerk of a pair of white gloves as a memento of the singularly happy combination of circumstances, namely, the open- ing of a now court of justice and the first magisterial sitting in it ; and an absolute absence of any case of crime to be brought under the notice of the sitting magistrates. He should like to add that he hoped the Mayor and all the magistrates would long be spared to discharge their duties in their improved surroundings. And, although one could scarcely hope that their duties would always be as light ar they were that morning, one might express the hope that they might be always such as would reflect no dis- credit upon the borough of Warrington.Warrington had bad a somewhat unenviable repWation for one thing or other of a shady character. He didn% know in the leas: why, b: cause though he had done polize duty in many towns he had never done duty in any town where the actual residents and natives were so law abiding as in Warrington. We, through our geographical position got a good deal of the riff-raff of ottier tow ns,and hence the business of the Warrington Court was unusual!, heavy. He thought, in justice to the actual residents of the town, he ought to say that 'hey were not of a law. lee character. He had now to ask the Mayor to accept, through the Magistrates' Clerk, a pair of white gloves as a memento of the occasion. (Applause.) The CLERK handed the gloves to the Mayor remarking that he hoped that what had taken place that morning would be a good omen for the future. The Mason, in reply said : I am sore it is a matter of very great pleasure indeed to congratulate you (the Cnief Constable) and the town upon the fact that this morning's business is practically nil. I think it speaks volumes for the condition of things in Warrington that we should have a meeting of this character under such pleasant circumstances ; I since: ely hope that what has taken place this morning will be repeated on many oc casione. I quite agree with Mr. Talbot that the people of Warrington will bear very favourable comparison with the residents of other populous dis- tricts in the United Kingdom in regard either to their personal character or conduct. A good many other things have been said ab 3 ut Warrington and War- rington people in days gene by, which in my judgment are very much exaggerated, and give an absolutely unfair impression of the character of the people of the town. 1 hope it may be the lot of each of my colleagues who may preside on different Occasions in this Court to receive at the hands of the Clerk through I the Chief Constable a similar memento to what I have received this morning. I shall prize these gloves, not because of their intrinsic value, but for what they re- present, a condition of things that is so agreeatle to all of us, and what, I hape, is a vert happy augury for the future condition of things in the town. I thank you very sincerely for this little memento, and lam pleased at the fact that the first sitting here is so pleasant and so satisfactory in all respects. (Applause). The magistrates then rose.</p>
