        <p>Hunslet v. Brighouse Rangers.</p>
        <p>At Hunslet, before a large gate. Result :—Hunsiet, three goals and four trios (18 points) ; Brighouse, one goal (two points). Salford v. Halifax. • -</p>
        <p>This match was played at New Barns, before a large number of spectators. Ki son kicked off for Halifax, and for a short time the game was well contested close to midfield. At length Little picked up the ball and dodged through a few Salford men, and then dropped a capital goal. A few minutes later, however, Lomas did a similar thing for Salford, which he followed up by kicking a penalty goal, and Salford led at half time by four points to two. L3mas threw the ball out to T i Williams, who Seared a try, which Lomas improved. Little dropped another goal for Halifax, and the game ended :—Salford, three goals and one try (nine points); Halifax, two goals (four points). Batley v. Broughton Rangers. This fixture was decided at Batley before only a moderate number a spectators. Both sides were strongly represented. The Rangers won the toes and ' decided to play down hill. Within three minutes from tht start Batley were penalised for off-side play, and W. James kicked a goal for the Rangers. Batley, mainly by forward rushes, kept the Rangers' back for a short time, but eventually the superior back play prevailed and Wilson scored a try for the Ranger , which W. James converted. At half time tha Rangers led by seven points to none. Batley played down hill in the second half, and at once commenced a determined attack. Auty ran to the Rangers' line, where he threw the ball wide to Goo :all, but it was intercepted by Hogg, and he and Wilson raced cff to the other end, and another try resulted to the Rangers. This reverse made Batley play with more determination than ever, and during the last portion of the game they kept the Rangers well on the defersive. T. Fitzvirald scored an unimproved try, but the Rangers' defence was not again penetrated. and the game ended, Broughton Rangers, two goals and two tries (ten points) ; Batley, one try (three points).</p>
