      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-18_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>NORTHERN LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>THE NORTHERN LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>Warrington's First Defeat at Home.</p>
        <p>Warrington sustained defeat at home on Saturday for semen, the victors being Swinton, on Warrington soil for three succes- sivedtwh ahey firstseasons.lwi aav e t w tnhcoilgther side waa at full strength on Satur- day, n .N w iioog ton being the worst sufferers with Fish, p,oardrnao, Fell. Morrison and Eden among the absen- tees. Swinton missed D. Davies at half, Critch coming in as subSZitute. The weather was all that could be desired for football and the orowd rolled up in large numbers. There would be fully 6,000 present when crerationS Commenced. Mr. Kidd, of Millom was:the reteree,and the teams lined out in the following order warrington : Hallam, full back ; Ratcliffe, Isber- wood, Dickenson and Harris, throe-quarters; Mien and Ilockenhali, half backs ; Edmondson, Jolley, J Swift, F Swilt, Dakin. 1112,ham, Cunningham, and Edwards, torwards. Swintoa , Gormley, full back ; Lewis, Messer, R Valentine and Eiampson, three-quarters ; Williamson and Critcti, half backs ; Preston, Daies, Evans, Bar- nett, ?oilier, Pomfret, Vigors, and Harris, forwards. warrington started, J Swift setting the ball in motion. To the surprine of the crowd the Warrington , front rank forced the pace and beat their heavier oppon- ents in the scrimmages. Swinton had at once to defend, I Allen, Beckerman, Isherwood and Dickenson passing across smartly. Another series of exchanges nearly brnke through the Swinton defence, Lewis jest collaring Ratcliffe by the shirt when he was almost clear. A knock-on by Dickenson gave Swinton relief, R Valentine Punting powerfully down the field. Off-side by tho visitors caused them to fall back, however, Hallam gaining further ground by a grand follow-up and tackle of Valentine. , The home full back tried at goal from a penalty in , front of the posts but failed badly. Then Swinton rushed down to the other end, Hallam mulling on the line, and Warrington being subjected to a fierce attack. Twice Messer fed Lewis beautifully and the sprinter seemed a certain scorer each time. Hallam brought him down magnificently on the first occasion, and on the second Allen just managed to push him in touch. These were narrow escapes for Warrington, another of which came from Williamson who only fell short of the line by inches after a dash from the scrimmage. Harris at last</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-18_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>Oldham v. Runcorn.</head>
        <p>Oldham v. Runcorn.</p>
        <p>At Oldham. The teams were fully representative,and Oldham had the worst of the exchanges at the opening, Runcorn finessing on their goal line continually, and the visitors bad the satisfaction of securing the first score, Richardson dashing over from a scrummage. Farmer failed at the easy kick. The visiting forwards played the kick and rush game, and bothered the Old- ham defence. Ruzzey made a good shot at goal from a penalty kick at centre, the ball striking the crossbar. Just before the interval Fluzzoy, after a pretty dash along the touch line, added a goal from a difficult posi- tion. There was little to choose between the teams in the second half, for both sides had a turn at the attack, but there was no soore on either side,Oldham directing their attention ehiefl7 to a sound defence, in which they were successful. Tney were handicapned toy Sam Lees getting his ankle badly wrenched, and he had to leave the field for a time ; when he returned he was sent full back, Thomas going centre and doine4 good service. Oldham won by a goal and a try (five points) to a try (three points).</p>
        <p>Hull v. Leigh.</p>
        <p>Leigh took their strongest team to Hull, exeepting Nojrbury. Parkinson started for Hull against a slight wind and Leigh at once attacked strongly, their for- wards controlling the scrummages, and after seven minutes' play a grand round of pasF.ing on the part of Davies, Johnson, and Dunbavin ended in Moiyneux scoring near the posts, Dunbavin placing the goal. A lot of exchange kicking followed. Piay was very ca.:- citing, Leigh having rather the best of matters until just before the interval Townend cross-kicked to his forwards. The ball was kicked over the Leigh line, and after a hard race between several players on each side Jacques scored a try, no goal resulting, and at the in- terval Leigh led by five points to three. A lot of kicking and marking characterised the second half until a flying kick by Ja, ques was mulled 'by a Leigh forward and Jacques rushed up and scored, the place-kick failing. Then Ganley in trying to field the ball was knocked over, and Parkinson scored. Result :—Hull,threc tries (nine points); Leigh, one goal and one try (five points).</p>
        <p>Huddersfield v Bradford; r At Huddersfield before 5,000 spectators. By good 'footwork Huddetsfleld advanced to tno Bradford quarter and Kitchen scored, Readyhough failing. Half-time : Huddersfield, ono try (three points) ; Bradford nothing. After the interval Broadlev restarted, aril Huddersfield at once gc t into Bradford's territory, Kitchen having hard lines, but Wimpenny got over after a splendid round of passing, Beadyhough failir7. Huddersfield continued to overplay their opponents, Wimpenny scor- ing again, Sutcliffe failiag. From a scramble Pollard scored for Bradford. Marsden converting. Result : Huddersfield, four tries (12 points) ; Bradford, one goal and one try (five points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-18_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>LANCASHIRE SENIOR COMPETITION.</head>
        <p>LANCASHIRE SENIOR COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>Results up to and including aitober 12th.</p>
        <p>For. Agst. P. W. L. D. G. T. P. G. T. P. P. Widnes 6... 6.. 0... 0...12 18— 78... 1. 2 8...12 Wigan Haro w 5... 4.. 1... 0...13 19— 83... 6 2 18... 8 Birkenhead 6... 4... 2... 0... 5 13— 49... 6 9 39... 8 *stock port Millom 6... 3... 2... 1... 8 11— 43.. 8 10— 46... 7 St Helens Hull K. Rovers Rochdale 11'neta 6... 1... 5... 0... 6 2 18... 5 13— 49... 2 Lancaster 6. 0... 5... 1— 4 2 14... 8 12— h 2... 1 'Radcliffe 5. 1... 4... G... 3 4 18...13 21— 89... 0 Morecambe 6... 0. 6. 0... 2 4 16...12 10— 54... 1 *Altrinchnm</p>
