      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-18_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>EXAMINER. FRIDAY, OCTOBER 18, 1901. WARRINGTON'S ELECTRIC LIGHTING STATION. A Visit of Inspection.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER. FRIDAY, OCTOBER 18, 1901. WARRINGTON'S ELECTRIC LIGHTING STATION. A Visit of Inspection.</p>
        <p>At the invitation of the Chairman and members of the Electric Lighting Committee an official visit was paid by the members of the Warrington Corporation to the generating station at Howley on Thursday last week. The oppor- tunity thus afforded of seeing the electric lighting plant in full operation was readily taken advantage of, and the members were agree- ably surprised to find the Corporation in possession of an electric station, which for completeness and adaptability must rank second to none in the country. The steadily increasing demand from private consumers for electricity for lighting,and in a less degree for power purposes, has fully justified the action of the Corporation in establishing an elec- tric supply undertaking. Of its ultimate financial success there seems no reason to doubt. Already it is more than paying working expenses notwith- standing the fact that the lighting load is never required more than two or three hours per day at its rawamum. When electric tramways are sup- plied, the staff and the plant will be more fully utilizad and the financial result still more satisfac- tory. The plant which was shown in operation on Thursday evening is capable of supplying a demand of about 15,000 eight candle-power lamps. The present demand from customers is equivalent to 12,000 leight candle power lamps and each month wit- nesses an increase in the demand. The committee are already undertaking extensions of plant and mains and a satisfactory feature of this develop- ment is the fact that the plant can be increased without involving any capital expenditure on buildings, as ample room was provided at the outset in the boiler and engine houses for the laying of additional plant. Mains are now being laid in Church-street, Bewsey- street, Springfield-street and round the Market and from this new area of supply a greatly increased demand for electricity is anticipated. Since the in- stallation of electric light in Warrington on the 23rd of December last the supply has been continu- ous and of a high quality, anti considering the difficulties which always beset a new undertaking, the uninterrupted nature of the supply reflects the greatest credit upon the management. Mr W H GRIMSDALE, the electrical engineer,explained in detail the construction and working of the plant,and if the reader cares to follow us in our round of inspec- tion we will endeavour to describe some of the chief matters of interest. In the first place it is necessary to state that in connection with the generating station the destruction of house refuse is carried on. The waste heat produced is profitably utilised in the generating of steam for driving the electric dynamos. Two destructors have been erected and at the present time these are used alternately week by week. The carts conveying, the refuse on arriving at the station by way of Parr-street, proceed to the tipping platform, where the rubbish is depasited. The attendant at the destructor throws the stuff into a hopper which feeds the furnace. Every partic'e of refuse finds its way into the capacious mouth of the destructor and the visitor may see an innocent cabbage leaf bearing company with a sadly-used lobster can in its progress to the tier; furnace which renders all and sundry to the common level of a clinker. Still further use is made of the refuse after it reaches the clinker stage for the best portion of it is used in the construction of the concrete foundations of streets in the borough. Whilst referring to this department of Corporation work it may be here stated that in close proximity to the generating station the stables and repairing shops of the sanitary depart- ment have been erected with all modern conveniences and in the generating station itself ,machinery has been provided for supplying the necessary pressure to force tee excreta from the intercepting depot at Howley to Longford depot. Corning more particularly to the electricity supply undertaking, the visitor finds the boilerhouse equipped with five boilers, three of which are hand fired and the other two are used in connection with the refuse des- tructors. The first named are capable of evaporating 6,0)01bs of water per hour at 180 lbs pressure whilst the two destructor boilers will evaporate a like amount, but they work at 2001b3 pressure. In anticipation of an increased demand for electricity, another hand fired Babcock and Wilcox boiler is in course of erections Between the boilers and chimney an economizer ha. been provided to heat the feed water for the boilers and by this means the heat of the water is increased to 210 degrees. Proceeding towards the engine house, condenser pumps are to be seen in operation, but chief interest centres in the engine house itself. here are fixed two of Willians direct coupled engines and dynamos of 250 kilowatts each and one of 100 kilowatts. The smaller set is for use during light load and to make up to the full load in the evening. At present the engines do not start running until about four o'clock in the afternoon and continue until about ten ; but when the day load is increased in consequence of the tramways undertak- ing and a further demand by private consumers for electricity for power purposes, the machinery will be worked at its maximum efficiency for a longer period during the day. The switchboard with all the necessary apparatus for regulating and testing the suprly of lar- trinity occupies a place in the engine house and compels attention. The fingers of the (1)0k-work apparatus indicated that the pressure was 235 vats at the time of the visit,and those who are interested in the impendine electrocution of the murderer of President M.Kirley wil probabla tit surprised to learn that a volaage of ten times that pressure is necessary to k.ll a healthy man by one shock. Immediately behind the switchboard is the. battery room By means of these batteries the Corporation is able to store a quantity of electricity, equal to 100 amperes, or in ocher words, a sufficient quantity to supply the demand during the small hours of the night and morning when the dynamos are not running. In order to give the visitors a better idea of the working of the arc larnp3 used for street lighting, Mr Grimsdale showed one in action without the accompanying globe. The carbon, it appears, has to be renewed every sixteen hours. There are 31 such lamps at present used for street lighting in Warrington and the illuminating power of each lamp is Equal to 2,000 candle power. Demonstrations wero also given of the value of electric light for rapid photographic printing and of the use of electricity for heating purposes by means of radiators. At the conclusion of the inspection, Mr A J Jolley tendered the thanks of the visitors to Alderman Monks and the members of the Electric Lighting Cox.mittee for aho opportunity given of inspecting the premises. In referring te the indestructibility of,matter,Councillor A I Jolley pointed out that they had witnessed that evening the tranaformation of objectionable refuse into a brilliant light. Alderman MONKS acknowledged the compliment. He stated that the committee was anxious to increase the supply of electricity for power parpeses in order that the plant might be morn fully employed in the day time. The undertaking, he said, was now paying more than the working expenses and before long they hoped to be able to meet the annual charge for sinking fund and , interest. Lie paid a tribute to the excellent work done by Mr Grimsdale and his staff and stated that be be- lieved there was not another electricity undertaking in ' the country which had been so successful in providing L an uninterrupted supply from the commencement,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-18_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>WOODMAN'S WEAKNESS.</head>
        <p>A WOODMAN'S WEAKNESS.</p>
        <p>Mr Phillip Harden, of 29, Stourton-lane, near Bath, is a woodman by occupation, and his work frequentiy entails the lifting of heavy weigbtr. To lift heavy weights, one must be sound in wind, back and limb. For this reason Mr Elurden found him- self unable to follow his work. Be suffered from pain in the back. "It was impossible for me to lilt weights he says, " for had I done so, I should have tumbled under the.ma, my back was so weak." He came to the conclusion that the weakness of the back was due to the kidneys, and began to take Dodd's Kidney Pills. The first box benefitted him, and he was soon well again. The pain left his back, and he is now thoroughly strong again. Backache is the surest sign of kidney trouble, and you cannot cure it without curirg the kidneys. Dodd's Kidney Pills cure the kidneys. They are made to do so, and they never fail. Once the kidneys are alright, the body:is well too, and disease cannot enter. Once get the kidneys: acting, and backache disappears lika magic. Dodd's Kidney Pills are 2e. 9d. per box, of all Chemists, or post froe on receipt of price from The Dodds Medicine Co., 23, F•irringdon Avenue, London, E.C. Be sure of the name,</p>
        <p>Cheshire IVlazistrates and the "Clio " Training Ship. At a meeting of the Cheshire County magistrates, held on Monday, the chairman, Sir Horatio Lloyd, called attention to the comparatively small num- ber of boys sent by Cheshire to the training-ship Clio, and urged that more boys should be sent. Earl Egerton, Lori Lieutenant of Cheshire, said many of our shins were manned by foreign sailors, and it would be a great advantage if our own race were trained more largely to man our navy and merchantile service.</p>
      </div>
