      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-18_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>MAYOR'S BIRTHDAY.</head>
        <p>THE MAYOR'S BIRTHDAY.</p>
        <p>Presentation from the Council.</p>
        <p>During an interval in the proceedings at the lunch in the! Town Hall, on Friday, opportunity was taken to present to the Mayor, whese birthday it wa., a very handsome solid silver rose bowl which bore the following inscription ;—'• To Alder- man Henry Roberts, J.P., Mayor of the County Borough of Warrington 1899-190*from the mem- bers of the Corporation in commemoration of the opening by him of the new Police Premises, and of the Birthday of his Worship—llth October, 1901.'</p>
        <p>Alderman ;LUTON said that as the senior member of the Council he had been deputed by the subscribers to present to his Worship the silver flower bowl in reccg- nition of his services as Mayor and Chief Magistrate of the County Borough for the last two years, but more particularly in commemoration: of the opening by him of the new police courts on the anniversary of his birth day. (Hear, bear, and cries of 48 and 28.) All that he (Alderman Sutton) could say was that the Mayor was young for his age. (Laughter.) He need not say that be felt honoured in having been selected to perform that duty. The ceremony which the Mayor had that day performed was an important one in the history of Warrington, for the reason that the new Police Courts were to supply a long standing want. The old buildings in Irlam-steeet had for many years past been denounced as t otally inadequate and unhealthy for the incarceratien for trial or:prisoners and the magistrates had often complained of the crowded court and suffocating atmosphere. Her late Majesty's Inspector of Constabulary had on various occ isions intimated that the police grant was in danger of being discontinued. Under these circumstances it became absolutely necessary that additional accommo- dation should be provided and the erection of these new and more commodious premises, which it was be- lieved would suffice for the requirements of the;borough for a very long period was accordingly proceeded with. For the interest he had shown and the prominent part he had taken the Mayor was entitled to their very best thanks. Be had therefore pleasure. in the name of the subscribers, in asking his acceptance of that piece of plate, and he desired to express the hope that the Mayoress and he would long live to enjoy the use of it and that it might afterwards be handed down to his family for many geoerations as a in sinento of the important event which bad taken pl eie that day. (applause). The Alderman concluded by asking the support of Dr White. Mr CHARLES WHITE, who was most warmly received, said he had had a great honour conferred upon him in being asked to propose the health of the Mayor. Be supposed he was asked to do it because he was the oidest person in the room. If not the oldest person he had the largest experience as regarded the Mayors of the 13crough. They had all been good ones, but with varying quilities, yet never to his thinking had the duties been more ably, mere gracefully discharged than within the last few years—never with greater eloquence or greater expre;sion, not even in the days of " Peter the Great." (Laughter). They could easily conceive that any honourable person would dis- charge the duties with credit to himself and his fellow townsmen. But the last few years had n,t been common years. Very few persons there would recollect the circumstances attending the Crimean War. He recollected them well, and when he looked back upon what the Mayor had done for Warrington in pro- viding for "those left behind " and in every way furthering the patriotic instinct which had imbued the people of these islands, he felt thankful they had had such a person with such tact, such savoir-faire, and who had beers able to express their feelings in every way. (Hear, hear.) In speaking of what Mr Roberts had done, they could not separate the Mayor from the Mayoress. She had discharged her duties in a way which hi.d given satisfaction to everybody, and made them feel glad they bad such a Mayoress. So he asked them and he was sure they would do it with the greatest pleasure, to drink to the health of the Mayor and wish him many happy returns of the day. (Ap- I plan se) The MAYOR, who was cordially received, thanked the subscribers to that very beautiful presenc very sin- cerely, and he could assure them that both he and his wife would cherish that testimonial of their good feel- ing as long as they lived and hand it down to those who had to succeed thorn, where he hoped it might remain for many generations as some indication of the respect which was entertained by that Corporation towards his wife and himself. (Hear, hear.) The present was an exceedingly acceptable One, especially from the point of view of art and utility. It would always have a prominent place in hi house, and be was sure that his wife would be cquallylproud with himself to show it to all and sundry. (Hear. hear.) He thanked Dr White for the manner in which he had proposed his health, and those present for the way in which they had received it. His had been an exceptional Mayoralty. He was Mayor of the last year of the old century and of the first year of the new one, Mayor of Warrington during the last year of the last reign, and of the first year of the new reign ; Mayor during the last year of the old borough and of the first year of the new one. (Hear, hear.) These circumstances alone would have Marked it as a specially interesting period in the history of the town and in the history of the country. but on the top of that there had been this lamentable war a war which he thought could not have been avoided, a war which in its extent and its vastness completely deceived everybody;and which, so far ae the interests of the Ern piles were concerned, had get to be prosecuted to the bitter end. (Hear, hear.) But he was not in any sense wishing to introduce what might be looked upon as 'natty polities. Lie was only mentioning it as an incident which had thrown respon- sibilities upon the town and upon the Mayor for the time being which had been exceptional in their character and very arduous in their nature. (Hear, hear.) But while he had had a 000 d de :1 of work in connectioe with that, he was bound to nay what he had said before and what he should say again, that no man could have had more loral and consistent support in the discharge of those dut'es than he had received,not only from his col- leagues in the Council, but from the townspeople generally—(hear. bear)—and he had the satisfaction of feeling this at any ratie—he hoped he should not be considered too egotistical in saying it—he had en- deavoured to keep up the best traditions of the chair. (Hear, hear.) He had nothing particularly to regret. He had made mistakes—he would he a clever man who did not, hat he asked his friends to believe those mistakes were made with the beer intentiong. He hoped his mistaken would not be hPpt as A permanent record against him but would be blotted out along with many other thins one would be glad to forget as one got older. He was sure his amicessor would receive from the Corporation and the town the same support he had received. It was a proud position to be Mayor of a town like Warriugton, and he did not conceal the fact that he had been proud to have h.ld it. The Mayor concluded by thanking Dr White for the reference to his wife. She had, he said. been a splendid helpmeet and he deserved all that had been said of her and a great deal more. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>• WARRINGTON. Outbreak of Fire on Sunday Evening. The Fire Brigade was, at 11,30 on Sunday even- ing summoned to a fire which had broken out on the premises of Benry Weston, clothier' Bridge- street. Superintendent Turner and a good mus'er of firemen were early on the scene with the hose reel, and the fire was soon extinguished. It orizinated in one of the bedrooms, where the cur- tains of one of the windows had become ignited,</p>
