      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-18_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>I.ettere; to the ebitor. A Question of Education.</head>
        <p>I.ettere; to the ebitor. A Question of Education.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Progress e,d Elucation are synonymous terms. In the same proportion as a people is physically or mentally superior or both so much are the progress and position of that people among the nations. But educa- tion does not mean the same thing in every age of the world. Different epochs have different ideals—different goals in view—the basis of civilization in ene age may be entirely distinct Worn that of another. But whatever the spirit of an age or country—whatever the ideal , towards which it is striving, progress is made in prc- ' pertion as the equipment of its individual unit is more or less perfect, and this equipment is termed education! To the man in a state of nature, education is more phy- sical than mental—a practical development of -the material forcet—an attainment of excellence in war and ia the chase—a perfecting of the lower faculties of the mind which WP have in common with the brute. So, also in a feudal age war is the grand ideal for the attainment of which a corres- ponding education is required in the individual. Is it art or science for which a people endeavours ? Education must accordingly be artistic or scientific. Is it commerce ? The progress ef a country corresponds ta the commercial equipment of its individual member ! So much for the meaning of the word education ! Now the spirit of every civilized country of this age is undoubtedly commercial. Tie attainment of wealth is man's grand ideal ! Trade is his first consideration ! Philosophy and the arts take a secondary place. and even science is considered an iously only as it furthers the atteinment of his trading aspirations. Everything has conspired to further this idea. Painting and sculr- turn have reached their highest excellence in the past. Philosophy has gone to its furthest limit, and come to the conclusion thet it knows nothing, and can know nothing. Materialism has been pushed into prominence by scientific successes. 'the ends of the earth have been discovered and brought together. The super- -natural hardly exists in most minds except as a sentis merit. Material power is all in all, and material pOwer crystallise, in money. Money is obtained by trade. Civilisation therefore consists of one universal scheme of commerce. upon which everything in our present existence depends—by which everything is moved raised, or prostituted, as the case may be—through which everything is done that is done ! In this general commercial scheme England, until the present, has taken the leading and foremost place. By reason of her small and compact form—bs reason of her geographicsi position as centre of the Easeern, Wes- tern, and Southern hemispheres—by virtue of the mechanical genius of her people arts their maritime daring—Britain has, until now, been the pioneer of this modern worli. fa painting and music she has not grew ly excelled—in philopophy her elf nes have been only of a mecnndary order—in deep rrsearch and classical learning Germany has left her far behind. in refinement, good-taste, and the domestic arts, France has been her able teacher; but in mechanics and that practical, science which is the distinctive basis of modern life Ensiand, we repeat, has always led the way and been foreirest. Now comes the question. Does she still con- tinue in tbat high and enviable position ? Alas no ! It is admitted on all sides,by the ablest thinkers, by static- ticiaus and experts, by statesmen, authors and ministers that Germany from having a superior knowledge of chemistry upon which most manufactures depend, is running her neck to neck, and that America has taken the lead in coal, steel and iron! It is unnecessary to quote instances to prove thi3 Tor every newspaper and magazine recently has made the fact the chief of its leading subjects for discussion and corn- mew. Englande therefore, is no longer the supreme commercial country. She who has been, from the peculiar nature of her gifts the missionary, the coloniser, and the financier of the worldes falling back- ward, and, will soon, without some supreme effort in the right 'direction, be left far behind. As everything has its cause we will now endeavour to ascertain the meaning of this ! Let us,first of alecompare England the most Westerly, with China the most Easterly country of tho old world Continent. China is shut in, on every side, by mcuntains and the ocean. She has never been subject until recently to any serious influence of foreign nations. She has remained for' centuries undisturbed. There has been no outside action upon her. Toe result has been—stagnation ! China is the most Conservative country on earth and Conservatism is another name for stagnation. Oa the other hand, England, being sur- rounded by the sea, has, to a lesser extent from her leirer area, been insulated and therefore isolated. England. therefore, in spite of the vcrsatility of her people, is. in the main, Conservative. Conservatism is the preservation of what one already possesser. Con- servatism is a species of contentment—a lack of per- gress—a state of stereotyne—an inability or disinclin- ation to get rut of a certain; beaten trick. And this is a prominent characteristic of the English people. They fancy themselves republican, democratic, and radical. It is a mistake. There is no 'people in the world so monarchical. so enamoured of aristocracy, so lacking in revolutionary principles which, after all, are the prin- ciples of change and progress. Conservatismetheref ore. has been one of the chief causes of England's present condition's. Having achieved- commercial leadership, she has rested on her oars, and is still:resting ; whilst other nations, to whom ehe has supplied first principles' of mechanics and trade, rush forward witteundiministied energy towards perfection. The minde of such nations are in advance of their practice—their thought leads their action—tbey.believe that nothiag pays so we'll as knowledge—that commercial progress is, in a word, in proportion to its education! Now, England, considering her importance is perhaps the worst-educated country in Eirspe. She is, in this respect, the astonishment of every foreigner. The Con- tinental visits this country with exaggerated ideas of British greatness and, expecting to find the people cor- respondingly well educated, is surprised to discover that this is not the case. I have slacken with German,French, Russians and Norwegians, and find the same complaint uttered by each of them. But, Men in this country,the fact bas now become recognized by leading thinkers, writers and statesmen. The speech of Lord Rosebery at Glasgow dealing with the subject is ton recent to be already firgotten. About the same time Mr Bircheneuth an authority on continental trade And trade methods wrote in the Nineteenth Century believe that the most threatening danger to British trade lies partly in the inadequate and antiquated educational provision which we make for onr people but most of all in the absence of the spirit (progressive) asbich,:alone, makes education of any value." Sir John Gorst has said that the present condition of English education is chaos. The. Duke of Devonshire affirmed that next to a powerful navy, the future of England depends upon bee school B—— they are, and what they teach. Sir W Harcourt spoke of our national education as being a most defec- tive system. Thus thinkers, in and out of the country, are alive to our echicetiooal shortcomings. We have littlo systematic prof, ssional training. I can illustrate this ;n a few words. Lit us take the photographic profession. In Ger- many, Austria, and, for anythirg I know, in France also, the photographer must bo a professional, tchai- cally and professionally trained. In this country, on the other hand, anybody who can develop a negative and tono a print may, and does make a business of it. Without any chemical or a•ti.tic koowledge, knowing nothing scientifieally of the action of light, an English photographer goes on fiddling, until he arrives, by practice, at some degree of perfection. Now apnly this difference, in some degree to every trade end profession, to every part of commerce and manufacture in this country, and we shall soon perceive the rearm of the weakness of Britain. There is no systematrc prefer- sional and technical training. But a further explans- tic n of its cause. founded upon our first position of Con- servatism, I shall r,serve for my next letter. Believe me, Yours fAittfury.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-18_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>L'HOMME QUI RIT.</head>
        <p>L'HOMME QUI RIT.</p>
        <p>ST EDWARDS:.CATHOLIC CHURCH.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>In connection with the above church the patronal festival was observed on Sunday. Both the, morning and evening services were well attended,the sermons be- ing preached by the Very Rev Father Mark. C.P., recta of St Armes Retreat, Sutton, St Helens. In the morning the preacher delivered a very instructive discourse from the text, " That we may walk worthy of God in al, things pleasing, brine f.rithful in every good work, and incress;ng in the knowledge of God." After speaking of the value of faith as one of the eeEentials to religion, and lamenting its decline at the present day, be touch- ed on the education queation,as it affected their children. Catholics, he said, had it As a sacred charge that their children should be instructed in the law of God, so that in their youth they might lay the basis of a structure of a moral and religionlife. They could not leave so important a matter to the philanthropist of the state. The philanthropist might lighten the burdens of society arid the state miEht grant free Godless education but upon the Church and the parents were imposed the sacred 'charle of the religious education of their little ones. The rung mind could not bejoetter trained, owing to its susceptibility, than in their own schools where,'Sepa rated from evil influences, their minds were oreo to receive that religious instruction which would afterwards bear fruit.</p>
        <p>The choir had been con,iderably argumented and the Runcorn Orchestral Society also contributed their quota to the musical portion of the service. The " Kyrie." " and " Credo" were sung in splen- did style. The " Sanetw," " Benedictua " " Agous Dei." and " Drmine Sa!vun " also beirg faithfully interprcted. The soloists, sr Perry George (bass) and C. Watson (tenor), and Mi*SeB Devine (soprani and Neary (contralto), each performed their ;snorter; portions with commendable ability. Mr, S. Beckett contrihuted to the success ot the services by his credit- able handling of tbe organ and the influence of Mr. J. W. Dockry, conductor was everywhere ar.varent. The nieces in tbe evening .were " Salutaris," " Tantum Ergo '' and " Ave Marla." Tbe services were con- ducted by the Rev. Father Chambers priest-in-ebarge. The serviceS were a thorough auccese.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-18_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>EXAMINER FRI DAY, OCTOBER 18, 1901 LATEST NEWS. THE FORTHCOMING MUNICIPAL ELECTIONS IN The Pope Seriously IIL WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER FRI DAY, OCTOBER 18, 1901 LATEST NEWS. THE FORTHCOMING MUNICIPAL ELECTIONS IN The Pope Seriously IIL WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Tnunmx.</p>
        <p>The " Gaulois," to-day states it has received private information confirming the report that the Pope has been suffering from incrsposition, which, on account of his ago and constant mental aclivity, may become serious at any moment. His Flohneat, holds his recep- tions as usual, but his medical aclvi9?ra forbid him to converse with visitors.•</p>
        <p>A later edition says his holiness recently received a shock which may possibly lead to imminent and serious developments.</p>
        <p>WANTED BY A CHEMICAL MANURE MANUFA.C- TURING COMPANY in the West .of linglaro' a COmPgTfINT FORRMAN who understands the manufac- ture of Sulphuric Acid and is able to do repair to leaf' work In and connected with Aeld Chambers and to undertake the supervision of the manufacture of Manures. To reside in house adjoining Factory. Apply stating age. exnerience salary required and when disengaged to Z. K.. 294. Miss DEACONS' Leadenhail-street, London.</p>
        <p>PO BR SOLD, three good !Defers, one clos, et hand,..,, Apyy JNO GooDwrx, Bloomsberry-lane, Timp.riey.</p>
        <p>QOUTHPORT.—GBNBRAL and HOUSRMAID wanted. wo objection to sisters ; no registries,— Send particulars Mrs 111LswnsLET, Nelson Lodge, Trafalgar-rood, Blrkdale, Southport.</p>
        <p>The Town Clerk has issued the usual statutory notices with regard to the elections which will take place on Friday. the let November. The nomination papers must be delivered by the candidate himself, or by his proposer and secooder,at the Town Clerk's office before five °clock on the afternoon of, Tours. day, the 24th October. The next day the Mayor will attend at the Town Hall for a sufficient time between two and four to hear and decide objections to nomination papers. The followinz 'members of the Town Cmincil retire and all seek re-election :</p>
        <p>Town Hall Ward, W J Forshaw (C). Whitecroik, Ward, W Broueh (L). Bewsey Ward, W Roßers (I): Orford Ward, J Wilkinson (L): St John's Ward, J Baird (C). Fairfield Ward, D S Duncan (L). Howley Ward, 8 Davies (C). St Austin's Ward, T Baron (L). Latchford Ward. L Ellion (Li), MEETING OF THE CONSERVATIVE COUNCIL. A DISPOSITION TO FIGHT. . • COMPROMISE CONDEMNEDo</p>
        <p>A meeting of the council ot the Conservative Associa- tion was held in the Assembly Room at the Conserva- tive Club, on Monday evening, "to consider what action shall be taken by tNe association in the ap- proaching municioal elections on November le, auci other buainese." Mr John Whits (chairman of: the association) presided, and amongst those present were* Alderman Heaton, Councillors Smethurst, John Roberts, Greening, A J Jol'ey, A Jolley, Baird, Forshaw, Davies, and Flutse, Mr William Holbrook (agent), and Mr Herbert Hayes (manager of the Jpnior Conservative Club)._</p>
        <p>Th:i CHAIRMAN said ths principal business ef the meeting was to consider what action shoul 1 be taken with regard to the municipal elections on the Ist November. Their retiring members were Town Hall ward, Counciller ; Bewsey ward, Councillor Rogers (Ind.) • 8: ward. C iunctlior Baird ; Howley ward, Councillor Davies. The retiring Liberals</p>
        <p>ware—Whit-moss, Councillor Srougla ; Orford. Coun- cillor Wilkinson ; S Aultin's, Councillor Barton ; Latchford, Councillcr Ellison; and Palrficid ward,Coun- cillor Duncan. Be believed that all the retiring mem- bers of the Council would seek officu again. Councillor SMETHURST said be was of tha same opinion:</p>
        <p>"Mr T H ASHCROFT called attention to the fact that only three Conservative ward meetings had been held, and he should like to know why all the ward commlttees had not been called toee4ser to give their opinions on the approaching elections. (applause.) Were the wards to be completely ignored ? That was the question which had to Ise considered. fle had been asked in his own ward" What are you going to do ?" He cot- tended that this meeting esould have been called to sanction what the ward committees had done. (Hear, hear ) The time bad passed for anything like this to have been done. He had been a strong Cmservative for over 23 years, but he did not think it was right for a few Conservatives to have called this meeting in the circumstances. If the Conserative Asiiciation did not 'fight be steal&amp; (Applause. The CHAIRMAN I tikes the whole responsibility on my own shoulders and all the blame. I instructed Mr. Holbrook to call the meetings for thee wArds where the candidates were retiring, and to leave the other wares until after this meeting. I most take the seep msibility and if it is wrong I am sorry, Mr. ASHCROFT . Would it not have been better to have called all :he wards first?</p>
        <p>Toe CHAIRAIRN • We can call the meting s stll'. Mr. AEHCROFT iR at this meeting that we have to Flanction whether we fight or not. Alderman 13Ereron said Mr. Baird had been selected for S. John's ward.</p>
        <p>Colincillor JOHN ROBERT 3 thollelt Mt. Ashcroft was quite right. Tee correct thins would have been to have had a meetiog in each ward previous to the presynt meeting. (Hear, hear.) It was only right that the wards should have some choice in the selection of the candidees.</p>
        <p>Mr. PErER CARRUTHERS said it was felt in Se Austin's ward that the Conservative workers had been Ignored. Mr. ISHERWOOD said he though the discussion had arisen under a misapprehension. If the members of the council would read the circulai ceoveeing the meet- leg they would see thet it read as follows:—" To con- sister what action shall he taken by this asseCiation in the approaching manicipal elections on November let, and other buiness." That meant, he took it, that whatever the council decided would •have to be done. If it was decided to fight they would have to fight. (Hear, hear.) Mr. W. J. Witscur thought there should be a fight all round, and that they should not-accept a compromise. Mr. EDWIN WOODS said he had had a chance during the past few months of judging the feeling of the b oroueh wi h regard to the approachine elections, ad he knew it was the opinion of the different workers 'that they had been Slighted. He might ü well tellthe workers present that he quite agreed with them that they had been slighted. If they asked hie ehinion be would give It that the borough had been elisfranchised, so far as the Town Council was coec,rned, for the past three years and no one bad felt that more strongly than he had done. To the man who asked who Elwin Woods was, he reelied " Elwin Woods is a ' mane-who has not sought office, nor does he intend to do so, neither has be had anything out of the contracts of the Corporation." (Cheers). He (Mr. Woods) defied any man to say that he did this, for any aggrandisement of his own, and be cculd look every man straight in the fact' and say se, Be had no mere to say except that he was speaking for the Peoria of Warriogton . he might also go to this extent, and say that he Was speak- ing for some of the Liberals. (Applause.) Mr W L Wxereews wished to make a few passing re- marks with reference to the ward in which he dwelt, the Fairfield Ward. 'After the strong remarks which were made by Mr Woods at the annual meeting of the Junior Conservative Aseoclation, he thought the time had gone by when these compromises should take place. Be could assure the meeting that tee working men of Warrington when they canvassed them for their votes said : " Why do you want us now ? Yon have not been to see us for three years : you come to see us now Oust it is a, general election." These remarks were pissed all round. The Conserva- tive party was a fighting party ; that eel been proved In the past, and he hoped thee would prove it in the future. Be hoped they would be able to do something without creating il!-feeling. . Mr. Jeffries qteite agreed with the general feeling that the compromise was not a wise one. He had not aid much about it. but in moving about the town, and übbing against old Conserystiv workers, he beard xpeessjene that the compromise was unwise. He agreed with Mr. Williams that their duty that night was not to find holes in each othet's methods of artien,but to unite in one effort to strengthen the hands of the party. The Chairman had kindly told the meeting that he took the whole responsibility open himself, and where was I the human being who did net err sometimes. If there had been mistakes in the past, now was the time to mend them by united effort. (Hear, hear, and applause) Cnineillor JOHN ROBERTS thought the meeting bught to dscile whether there should be a compromise or' not as had existed for the east three yeare. He had heard very disagreeable remarks about the compromise ; it was a very serious matter to delfranchlse any Party, and he did not agree with it. They' would never catch him entoring into another cotnpromise, for , they were able to fight their own battles. A compromise showed a weakness, and working men did not wish it. ' The CHAIRMAN 'said there had been no intention to committee. In In the yesre gone by it 5, knew, to have ward Met- ward' slight any reutghtesomeArds to select the candidates and e ihnle .b et: andt hf eo submit the names to the Council meeting, but on more than one occasion it had been found necessary to say "We cannot contest the election" for one reason or other. Therefore it toad been thought better to have this meeting first. He thought that if they con- sidered the matter they would agree with him that he was justified in the course he had taken; he had certainly taken the most straight- forward course. There WAS no compromise. Several Speakers had talked about " putting an end to it." It had been at an end 12 metes, and no com- promise existed. Be bad not been in negotiation with any member of the Liberal party, and it was perfectly within the province of the meeting, if it thought wise —ha did not think it would be wise—to Fay tbat there should be • contest in every mat&amp; (applause); It was for the meeting to say whether they would crentert every ward or agree for the ptessnt, for this year at all events, to ceatent themselves with the • seats they had got. His advice was, take it or not use they Ilhed,, " lot the thing be for the let November." They had a.,.ked Mr. Parr to take an unusual Step, a step which had never been taken before, namely, to enter the Council as an outside:. and take the mayoralty, and it would be veryiunpleasant if they were to begin on the lat Nevem- het and have ill-ferling in every ward of the town. Let them have things smooth this time; without the (-lightest friction. , Councillor FORSFIAW said they would all accept the Chairman's explanation that there had been us dis- respect intended to the ward committees as to the working men. They considered his explanaeion a MU.- factory one, but there had been such a strong exprestela of feeling that evening that he considered it would he wise for the meeting to stand adjourned until next Monday at the nam. time and plac-. In the meantime the Ward Committees who had not held their meet ings could consider what they could do. He moved a Mr. CARRUTHERi teconled, resolution to that effect.</p>
        <p>Councillor GIIFENING supported, and said it would have been as well it: the 'various ward couvnittees had</p>
        <p>met and Made their selections, but what was the good of having a leader of the capacity of Mr White if they did not follow his sound advice ? He moved as ao amendment that they accept the advice of the Chair- man and not fight this Nwember but neat N&gt;vernber have ago all round. It would be a Dice compliment to Pile Parr to have a united Council,without the bother of an electioo. He was not speaking persooally,because he was not coming out, but it was due to Mr White atter his long servicrs that they should accept his advice. Councillor Jens lionstris agreed, provided they could return their retiring candidates. Mr ASRCROFT • rake the feeling of each ward first. Councillor Jour; ROBERTS seconded Mr Greening's amendment.</p>
        <p>INir JENKINS : If it is pasted, and any Conservative chooses to tight any of the wards, would he have the sympathetic support of the Cmaeroative party ? The CHAIRMAN replied in the affirmative. ...</p>
        <p>Me rWiLtrenrs : Will the org-iniz*tiqn-Of the party be placed at his disposal provided he is prepared to pay his pspenava ? _ _</p>
        <p>The Crum:wax : Hbe paid his own expenses the organization of the party wouli be placed as his dis- posal.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN asked Mr Greening ta withdraw his amendment and he did so. The meeting was then ad- journed until Monday ever idg nomt,and it was arrangPd that meetings should be held in the other wards in the nrat.t lose. On the mntion of Councillor FORSILAW, aeconrkd by Mr. WILLIAMS, a van of thanks was passed to Mr White for presiding. _</p>
        <p>The Cusirtnrsar, in replF, said he did not take excep- tion to what na I ben said. He liked a little lifa incro• duced into the work of ths amciation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-18_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>WAR.</head>
        <p>THE WAR.</p>
        <p>LATEST TELEGRAMS.</p>
        <p>More Death Sentences Confirmed.</p>
        <p>CRADOCE, WEDNESDAY</p>
        <p>Two more death sentences on men of Lotter's com- mando have been mininuted to penal servitude for life. The sentence of death by hanging passed upon J Kruger has been confirmed as bas also a similiar sent- ence passed upon Lieutenant Breda, of Bethulie, for train wrecking and murdering a native. Dr Anthony, formerly of Clarmont has been lodged in gtol on a charge of high treason.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-18_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>Lieutenant Kane's Successor.</head>
        <p>Lieutenant Kane's Successor.</p>
        <p>The following military appointment has heen grzetted: —Piince of Wales's Volunteers (Sauth Lincashire R.gi- ment).—Second Lieutenant G H Garratt ta be lieu- tenant, vice 11 It Kane, killed in action.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-18_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>FATAL ACCIDENT AT VULCAN FOUNDRY, EARLESrOWN.</head>
        <p>FATAL ACCIDENT AT VULCAN FOUNDRY, EARLESrOWN.</p>
        <p>Inquest at Warrington.</p>
        <p>At the Police Station, Warrington, on Wednesday evening, Mr Brighouse, county coroner, conducted an inquiry relative to the death of a craneman named Charles Heaves of 3, Liverpool Row, • Vulcan, Earles. town, who died at the Warrington Infirmary on Monday morning, from injuries nceived at the Vulcan Fmndry on the previous Thursday aftornoon. Mr Smith, one of Ella Majesty's inspectors of factories, watched the case on behalf of the liovernment and Mf Richards, the assistant manager of the works, appeared on behalf of the firm. TO(' Coroner, in operline the inquiry said it appeared that on Thursday, the 10th inst, the deceased was following his usual employment at the Vulcan Foundry, Earlestown, as a craneman. His duty was to rake or lower a coupling rod which was beicg forged. Pile forgeman, Thomas E Roberts gave the deceased in- structions to" lower "and whilst his back was turned he heard the crane handle whizzing raund. On turning round, Roberts saw the deceased on the floor and found that he was suffering from a fractured skull. Dr Moursev was sent for and he ordered the man's removal to the Warrington Infirmary where he died. The first witness called was alary Haaves. mother of the deceased. She said her eon was 29 years of age and had been in the employ of the firm practically all his lifetime. Be left home about one o'clock on Thursday last to go to his work. Later in the day she heard that he had been removed to me Warrington Infirmary. She saw her son in the Infirmary but be was not able to calk to her. _</p>
        <p>In answer to the Coroner, Mr, Richards, the assistant manager, said the deceased had been engaged on this particular work &amp;hint nine weeks,. Thomas Evans Roberts, forgeinan at the foundry said he bad been employed there about twelve years. Oa Thursday last he was making a coupling rod which weighed 4 cwts, 1 qr. Ile found it was bent a little across the middle and in order to straighten it be wanted it placing across the anvil. The deceased was in charge of the crane and he gave him instruction to " lovn.r." Witness had his back turned to the craneman at the time, but on bearing the crane handle whizzigg round he turned round and saw the deceased lying on the around. He did not see the accident but in all probs., Wilts, the handle ot the crane had struck the dezeased on the head. Everything possible was dlne for the man. He had never known a doctor arrive on the scene in so short a space of time as on this occasion. in answer to the Corocer,witnesa said it was possible the hammer might have caught the pulley chain and jerked the handle,of the crane. By the Inspector: Did you examine the crane im- mediately after the accident P Witness : Not until balf-axe-hour after. _</p>
        <p>The Inspector: Cau you tell me 'how far the pulley block was away from the hammer 2—Witness: About six Inches.</p>
        <p>.—...... Thelnapector : Would the pin be sufficently close to the hammer for the cotter to catch the pin as it der- cenced P—Witness Not in the state I found it. Tbe Inspector: Can you tell rpe whether the crane was provided with a catch P—Witness : Yes; the catch was all right.</p>
        <p>Nurse Dobson, engaged at the Warrington Infirmary 35id tho'deceased was brought to the institution at seven o'clock on Thursday evening suffering from a fractured bead. He did not recover consciousness and died on Monday. Inspector Smith said he had visited the works and from what he had seen the probability was that the cotter of the hammer caught rbe projecting pin which supported the pulley. "This would cause extra tension on the chain and cause the handle to rock.</p>
        <p>-The Cnroner : Dies it follow that there has been any neglect ? The Inspector : No sir. I have made no recommen- dations to the firm. The Jury then considered their verdict. The Fore- man (Mr Forshaw) said the jury were of opinion that deceased met his death ig.the ordinary discharge of his duties and there was no use to blame or censure-any- one connected with the accident. The jury thought it was purely accidental. They also wished him to exprees their sympathy with the mother of the deceased in her sad loss.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-18_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>ST. HELENS AND THE SCHOOL BOARD QUESTION. A largely-attended meeting; of (he school managers of St. Helena and district was held at the Town Sall on Monday, under the presidency of the Mayor, Alderman Forster. The proceedings were somewhat prolonged, and it is understood that the questionvqamicably arranging for the introduction of a school boar.l into the borough was discussed, with a view to this being done at an early date. In years pas: Canon Willintc, vicar of Sr. Helens, has proved a formidable opponent of a school board, and the Roman Catholics, who form a large section of the community, will have nothing to do with it, even if formed. A great deal is hoped by the friends of voluntary schools from the present Government, but the conviction is growing even among the clerey'of the town that a school board is inevitable, And that It will, after all, provide the easiest and most satisfactory solution of the whole question as it affects</p>
        <p>the locality. • THE ST. HELENS TRAMWAY MANAGER RESIGNS.</p>
        <p>Mr Isaac F Cotter, who has been manager for the S. Helens and District Tramways Company for over ten years, hay, sent In his resignation to the directory. Mr Cutler's occupancy of the position has been marked by a comoleto revolution in the tramways. Ai mznager he has worked early and late with the greatest zeal, ever striving to further the interests of the company. At the time of his appointment the motive power was supplied by a number of more or less reliable lumbering steam engines' but in the past few years these have been replaced by electric cars, all the Hoes and track have been relairi, and the nnmber of sactiona more than doubled. On Mr Cutter's suggestion the directors have appointed a former asoistant of Mr Cuttlel'a from Bradford—viz, Mr Walter Van:.</p>
        <p>, A free fight took place on Tuesday night at Aldershot tonween men of the Durham Light Infantry and the INnre(pster Regiment. Bayonets and rfloe were used i and some of the combatants were severely injured.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-18_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>HARVEST FESTIVALS.</head>
        <p>HARVEST FESTIVALS.</p>
        <p>Baptist Church, Earlestown. vic-s in connection with the harvest thanksgiving were held in the above place of worship on Sunday. The interior of the church was very tastefully decorated with flowers and plants, -aviailFt a plentiful supply of fruit and vegetables were artistically arranged in various parts. The preacher for the thy Was the Rev E K Everett, of Eccles, who delivered aepropriate dis- courses morning and evening to numerous congregs- tions. Da the ;afternoon a musical service was given by the cbnir, the f ilowing programme being submitted : Chorus, " Out in the golden cornfields,".scla, "Con- sider the lilies," Alias Lillie Jones ; sell and chorus,. Wheatsheaves." Miss A Grissold ; trio, "The King of Love my. Shepherd is." Misses AI anti E Partingten and Mr Harvey ; sub, " Nazareth!" Mr W Liniond ; chorus "We are little a ildiers." children and choir ; solo, " The Holy City,' Miss iii Partington ; duet, '630 praise the Lord," Miss A Grissotd and Mr TA Lirnond : solo, " Early in the moraine," MiS9 bottle Jones ; Men's voices, " Who are these from the Eastern monntains," M,S3TS E Parsons, J Isherwood, E A and W Lirnond ; chorus, " Flow lovely. are the messengers " ; solo and chorus, " Give peace in our time," Miss L Holr.—On Monday evening a tea and s vial was held in the school- room. These was a good number ',resent and a very el .asant meeting WAS ht 11, en excall .nt Programme of music being submitted by the Mazawattee Glee Party and members of the choir,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-18_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>Orford.</head>
        <p>Orford.</p>
        <p>The harvest thankstiving service were continued at Orford on Sunday last. lo the morning the preacher was the Rev HR Wild, of W.rriogton.-Simper's anthem " He giveth food to all flesh," was well renderPd oy the ehoir. Io tbs evening the pulpit was occupied by the Rv G V sle-0 wen. curate-in-charge. The decorations were very:effec:i</p>
        <p>Ragged School Mission, Warrington.</p>
        <p>Harvest festival services were conducted in this mission on Sunday afternoon and evening, the preacher inthe afternoon being Councillor James Evans, and in the evening Mr T Barton. The room had been taste- fully decorated with harvest products. Under the conductorship of Mr T Tones.spec,al hymns were credl - ably rendered by the children. The comtnitt,e aoknowledge with thanks the assistance of several Instrumentalists, of Mrs Crorfleld, Mr J 0 Parr, and Mr R A Naylor for fruit and flowers, and of numerous kind friends in the marker.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-18_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>St. Hilda's Mission, Latchford.</head>
        <p>St. Hilda's Mission, Latchford.</p>
        <p>The hardest thanksgiving SerViCFS were continued on Sunday. There was a choral celebration of the Holy Communion at 10.30, the preacher being the Vicar, who spoke upon" the five barley loaves and two fishes." Choral evens.mg was at 6.30, Mr WFI Stansfield,B. being the preacher. The anthem "I will f sod my flock." was creditable rendered, the alio being sung by ise,Grounli, and the quartet by the Misses Alice Vroulars, Neili Slcelhorhe and MnLisrs Longalaffe and Hoctb. Mr E D tVIPS, F.V:C.M,. ably presided at the organ. At this service the church was completely fled, several being unable to obtain admission. Stretton. •</p>
        <p>The harvest festival ssrvioes were held in St Matthew's Church, on Sunday.. The services were all well at- tended, pirticularly that in the evenine. The oreachor In the morning was the V cu. the Flon, and Rev Cl F Grnag ; in the afternoon, the Rv F E Evans, of Apple- ton Thorn ; and ia the evening, the 11. v C tdayhall, Little Button. The church had been beautifully decor- ated, as on former occasions, by a band of willing workers who had spared no ,trouble, the work being much admired. The choir sang three anthems during the day, very creditably ; and the usual harvest hymns were heartily j iined in by rue congregation. The amount collected, whlch was for the benefit of the Choir Fated, was about £l4. Mr Parkinson presided at the organ.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-18_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>St. Clement's Mission, Warrington.</head>
        <p>St. Clement's Mission, Warrington.</p>
        <p>The thanksgiving services were concluded ailb the above church on Sunday. The church had been laeauti- fully decsrated for the occasion by some of be niembefs of the congregatioe. The pulpit, reading desk, choir stalls, and altar rails were covered with beautiful flowers, inter:aced with corn, tro, whils near the door a stand had been placed, filled with fruit and vegetables. and surmounted vi h a miniature hay stack. Bunches of grapes were hung about the church, and altogether a pleasing effect wee produced. Holy Communion was celebrated in the morning, • genii number of communi- cants blare present. The celebrant was the Rev A Cimeren M.A., (curafr-ic-tharge), who was assisted by the Rev G W Dickenson. A children's servics was held in the afternoon, when the curet- in-charge gave a very interesting address on thanksgiving. The children were very attentive during the service. The Rev A 9 Cernereen conducted the set- vise in the evening, when the church was crowded, extra seats having to be requisitioned to seat the con- eregatiov. The pieather was the Bev Harry Cookson (St Agnes', Liverpool), who took for his text, "Giving thanks al waya for all things, unto Gorl and the Fatbar, in the name of our Lord Jesus Christ," Ephesians,v. 20. The preacher spoke on the great importance of giving thanks to God not only for the harvest but always for all things. rho anthem was "Ye shall dwell in the land," and it was exec lently rendered by the choir, the SAO being taken by Miss Annie Warburtoo. Mies Board- man presided at the organ. The offertories were on behalf on the Universities Mission t 0 Central Africa, a good sum being realized.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-18_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>HOCKEY.</head>
        <p>HOCKEY.</p>
        <p>Latchford v. Eccles.</p>
        <p>Latchford visited Eccles on Satuday and suffered another defeat. Two thirty-flves were arranged, and from the bully off Latchford assumed the aggressive,but were qoahle to penetrate the home defence, which was very stong. After some give-and-take play in mid field, Eccle3 rushed and scored a very fluky goal, the ball glancing into the net from the player's pas. This re- verse roused the visitors to make determined attacks on tiv home back, but they were always equal to the occtslon, clearing beautifully. Half-time, Eccles one, Latchford nil. Prom the re start, the visitors tried hard to equalize, and after several attempts Sallosies succeed- ed in netting the ball. From this point of tbe , games both sides struggled bard for the winning point, and Eccles succeeded in notching anothu• goal a few minute off time, and so a very even and keenly contested game resulted in a win for the home team by two goals to ode. Stockton Heath v. Sefton.</p>
        <p>S:ockton Heath let were at home to Sefton let on Saturday and enjoyed a close,if somewhat nugh game. Sefton winning the toss, the home team wore left to defend the lower goal which gave their opponents the advantage of the slight elope of which they availed themselves scoring after about twenty minutes' play from a scrimmage in the goal mouth. Art exceedingly tight game continued until half time when the visitors I crossed over lending by one goal to nil. Stockton Beath then pulled themselves together having by far the best of the game, the visitors' backs being con- tinually called upon to clear, owing to the determined attack of the opposing forwards which was at ingth rewarded by a stinging shot from Gandy which found the net shortly afterwards. A. pretty goal was sctred for Stockton Heath by Bond. who taking the ball half the length of the field beat the goal keeper standing far out. Sefton tried hard to quagga but found the home defence ton sound. The game ending in favour cf Stockton Heath by two goals to one.</p>
        <p>Local Commission. The "Loudon Gszette," of Friday night contained the folio ovinq :—Line Battalions : The Prince of wales's Volunteers (South Lincashire Ilsgiment), Second Lieutenant G H Wilkinson to be lieutenant, vice WV Hug, seconded for service under the Colonial Office, dated let May last.</p>
        <p>The CAEN-SPEY DISTILLERY, Situate in the finest Whisky-producing District of Scotland, is the property of W. &amp;A. Glthey. This Whisky is made entirely from home-grown Barley and Is kept absolutely nnblended In his Majesty's Bonded Warehouses to mature, and sold by W. &amp; A. Gilbey's agents in every town to .31. 6el per bottle,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-18_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>NEW BOOKS, MAGAZINES,</head>
        <p>NEW BOOKS, MAGAZINES,</p>
        <p>ETC.</p>
        <p>__— MACMILLAN AND CO.—A'large proportion of the I country's book-publishing is done by Messrs Mac- millan and Co, Ltd, who have in hand the works of some of the best known authors of the day. particolarly in the realm of fiction. Rudyard Kipling's book works as a rule make their first ap pearance under the auspices of the Macmillan Co, whose name is a guarantee of all that is up-to- date and excellent in the publishing line: Among some of the recent works from the press of this firm we have the following :—" KIM," by Rudyard KiPling, pp 413, Os; "KATHERINE LANDERDALE." by F Marion Crawford, pp 365, in the now well- known and popular 33 6d series of Crasvford's works ; "TEE EECRZT ORCHARD," by Agnes anti Egerton Castle, pp 316, 63; "TEE BENEFACTRESS." by the author of " Elizabeth and her German Garden?' pp 418, Os; "A KENTUCKY CARNIVAL, and the AFTERBIA.TH," by James Lane Allen, pp 286, 6s ; "HERB OF GRACE." by Rosa Nouehette Carey, pp 475, , Os; " THE SINNER AND THE PitoßLEm," by Sric Parker, pp 282, 63 • "VANITY FAIR," by: W l‘f.Abac, keray. op 746, 33 61, the first of a new issue of Tbackeray's works reprinted from the first editions, and. containing the original illus- trations by the author; neatly bound and printed in open-faced letterpress. Should be a populir edition altogether and one that will tiff n•d many of "Chackerafs admirers a splendid opportunity of possessing themselves of all his works uniform at a reasonable figure.</p>
        <p>_ Agnes anf Egerton Castle as literary collabora- teurs are making—i r they have not already maide— n name for themselves as writers of fiction which lis always a bit above the average. Their latest joint production, " THE SECRET ORCHARD," a story which corre-ponds to the play by the same authors produced by Mr and Mrs Kendal, is one I more than a bit above the average. Indeed it ranks high as a nbvel possessing singular dramatic power, and also in a high degree the elementary quality of arousing and'sustaining the interest of the reader. Otieinality and boldness of conception too are characteristics of the work not to be over- looked. The "secret 'orchard "corresponds to the occasional lapses from virtue and strict marital faithfulness of an unquestionably loving, but rest- less, emotional husband. Toe scene passes in a great French chateau, whose owner, the Duke of Cluny is a descendant of the Stuarts and inherits their personal charm and their faithlessness. Ile is adored by his beautiful wife Helen,and passionately loved by the young girl whom chance has thrown in his way 'on a visit to the " secret orchard " and who eventually comes unexpectedly under his own roof as his wife's adoptel daughter. Such an event as this naturally causes complizations, which are admirably handled, and which finally culminate in violent tragedy. The sequel might have been differ- ently worked out, but one cannot help but admit that the note of tragedy at the end accords well with the style of the book. There are some excel- lent characters,the Duke of Cluny and M. Favereau Minister of Education and family adviser, being especially well ponrtrayed. The easy-going deboietair Stuart is a faithful picture of the males of that unfortunate race. Helen seems to us a rather exaggerated conception of the loving unsuF- pecting wife, but may-be the world woula get on better if there were a few such women and wives. "Joy" is a misnomer for the young girl whose Ly.le vent blasts the happiness of the Cluny household her type is-happily a rare one, if it exists at • all. Certainly young girls with depraved 80111 s, faces like angels and eyes possessing a latent devilish fire,are altogether uncommon. Mowever,thore is no- reason why the pages of fiction should not contain such. Agnes and Egerton Cairle have achieved a triumph in "The SECRET ORCHARD," any extra- vagances notwithstanding. - "X at" is Kipling's latest, and is short for Kim- ball O'Hare, the cute son of a soldier in an Irish regiment in India His parents being desd, the young orphan's Childhood is spent among the natives of Lahore. " Kat " has been well reviewed and the critics have been kind. Apparently the worst that can be said about the book is that as a novel pure and simple it is a failure, and there is some truth in this observation. The weaving of a! plot and the coherent stringing together of incident and character in a lengthy novel are not Rudyard Kipling's strong points. He is more of a painter both in verse and prose than a novelist. This fact does not in the least, however, detract from the chnrm of " Ken "as a literary production. No other man could have written it: It contains Kip- hug and Kipling's India in every page. "Kim "is a likeable y oungster from the time we meet him perched on the breach of a gun at Lahore, onwards. I From this point our hero, already a street arab of wide experience, starts on a fantastic pilgrimage with an aneient) Tibetan lama, as his che/a or • disciple. The wanderings of the two strange companions, sometimes by train, oftener by foot from the plains to the highest hills, give Mr Kipling the finest opportunity he has yet found to show shifting phases of Indian life, man and woman, priest and soldier,Hindu and Mahommedan native and European; and in none of Ms books has there been so wide or so picturesque an out- look, or so varied an interest. Later on our hero's adventures quicken as he follows the dangerous, but fascinating, employment of a Government, secret,service agent. The book will if postaible, increase the author's reputation. It is great masterly, and among boy characters of fiction Kim should snpersede even such favourites as Stalky and Clete Kelly. The pages are illustrated by the author's father, Mr J Lockwood Kipling, in a decidedly novel Ivey. His pictures are, we believe first modelled in clay, and then produced on paper. We cannot express entire approval of the result from the point of view of the reader who desires his people and scenes to be pourtrayed as the author describes them and as he imagines them him- pelf. Mr Kipling's illustrations are faithful but they lack the life and vigour obtained by the best pen and ink illustrators. Eric Parker's latest work " The SINNER AND THE PROBLEM " deals with two small schoolboys who attach themselves by sympathy to an artist stay- ing as a guest at a private school in a pretty country place. They themselves are "the sinner" and "the problem.' One of the boys is the cousin of the beautiful young lady who owns a neighbour- ing place with a lake and swans, and through the boys the artist becomes acquainted with the Lady of the Lake. There ie little plot; the book is really concerned with presenting the ways' and works of • two pictuieseque and rather pathetic youngsters, and an artist's' enjoyment of nature-as a back- ground to his own idyll. It is a pretty, pleasing story, and that is all.</p>
        <p>_ Many lovers of fiction will welcome another story from the author of " Elizabeth and her Ger- man Garden." One has just been published entitled "The Benefactress,"which delmetbes the experiences of Anna Estcourt, a pretty and well-born English girl, whose life is made a burden to her at home bp tbe rich and vulgar sister-in-law at whose exnense she lives. Her chief sympathiser is a certain Uncle Joachim, a Prussian landowner, and tbe crisis of her fate comes when Uncle Joachim dying, leaves her a house and estate near Strelsund ; in the flat bleak country by this North Sea. so familiar to readers of "Elizabeth and her German Garden." Miss Est- court's first impulse is to assist those afflicted, as she has been, with unconettnial surroundings; and she offsrs, by advertisement, a home to twelve ladies of good birth and breeding, who through no fault of theirs are unhappy at home. In the out- come only three are accepted as inmates; but these with the practical and independent Princess, who 'has been taken on as Anna's hnusekesper, are suffi- cient to diversify greatly an existence already com- plicated by relations with the pastor, the curate, the steward or agent, and other German neighbours —to say nothing of Anna's niece Letty end her governess, and the attractive German gentleman, Alex von Lobnas The teeling for the North German scenery, the humorous delight in international con- trasts of character and manners, and the charm of style which made this lady's hooks so im.nensely popular. are all present in "The Benefactress." Row) N Carey is a prolific wieter,but unlike some of her contemporaries she is always careful in her , diction and gramful in style. Mira Carey's newest book " FIEBB OF Glue's "Is a god sample of her work. It coretns the fortunes of Malcolm Herrick and a young friend Cedric Templeton, to whom he acts as good providence—rescuing him first from an eulanelement of gaming debts, then from an unde- sirable engagement. In the end, but after many disappointments, Herrick is rewarded with the affections of Cedric's sister. Many other people enter into the story and the rsader's interast is maintained in a very great variety of personages. HEINEMANN.—There ammo doubt many Judi viduale who have not yet read Hall Caine's " ExenteAr. CITY," published by Mr William Heineitannn. To them we have only two words of Advin to offer. They are, " Read it !" "The ETERNAL CITY " Is not the best of Hall Caine's novels in our opinion, but it is a remarkable book</p>
        <p>Warrington.—Printed and Published by JO/Tif Co. at The Examiner General Printing Works,: new: riel street, and published! by them at the nine Place,o also at the The Examiner' Offices Victoria-road.WO' eet 32, George-street, ST. HELENS Offices. i.igaricet-s_tr. HARLESTOWN." 1U the County of Lancaster ends/to ^„„t r'S at. RUNCORN ,in the County of Chester, OCT. 18</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-18_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <p>and worthy the perusal of all who lay claim ter being abretst of the times. To have not read "Tile</p>
        <p>ETERNAL CITY" now is to confess ignorance. ISO matter whether you think it is good, bad or indifferent ycu are alright so long' as you have read "The Examoraa, CITY." Mr W T Stead condemns it</p>
        <p>_ utterly. 'Chat is another 'wagon why you should read it. The Pope is doubtful how to regard it, the Bing of Italy does rnt feel flittered by ir, and Catholics generally think there is much in it thEt.t.</p>
        <p>might have been left out—so do we, These are all further good reaionv in its favour. To reduce argu- ment to a lower level still we might, mention that</p>
        <p>the reader will get "plenty for his money." There are tome 600 pages and there is a gool deal of read- .</p>
        <p>lag on each. -I is not 'our purpose to add one more to the criticisms of " THE ETERNAL Crrr." An analysie,of_such a work is no little task and as</p>
        <p>no one would be any better for our labour if Vra spent it thus we remain content to reiterate, that the Work is no:: Hr Hall Caine's best,. but it ought to be read. There is Much in it that is new and much that is not—some people miht call the latter padding—but there is also an amplitude of emo- tional narrative and that after all is what the majority like. 1r Hall Caina has catered for the majority and catered well. The" STRAND MAGAZINE " far the currint month is, as must, full of good readinz well. illustrated, Dr Conan Doyle's serial "The Round of the Baakervillos Is proving a great draw, and in this number there are three other very readable stories. "An hour with a, bird doctoi is a specially taking paper fop a special pubic. " Ssndow in plaster of Paris "shows how this famous athelete went through the process of being moulded— by instalments —for the making of a complete cat of "Is the law too dear?" may attract a host of sympathetic readers, not a few of them victims, in their own judgment, of extra vagaut charges. T6i9 paper,too. Is illustrated. Cvelists will enjoy •• foe Jones-fiiiliard Bicycle Sensation ;" but we recommend to everybody the very ab'eland inatructive description of how the Fes.- 'wash(); away bits of our land,and how some memorable</p>
        <p>toots and all upon them' have dipappeared.—The "Winn WORLD MAGAZINE" starts with another portion of Dr Cooan Doyle's balk on the War in South Africa. republished in., this magezi op by arrangement ;• Mes,.rs ,• Abbas and Lee load us a stirring search for wild sheep in Mongo3a, evcry episode steeped in romance the ' pictures, too, filling as much space as the tee. Young folks will find in "Four Little . Joana of Arc" a• story to suit them well.— fr The SUNDAY STRAND c,nt4ins a pleasant contribution historical' and descriptive, entitled • "Religion and i Wipe,' by Mr Dinorven Griffiths, which is really an ace mint of Beautue, the metropolis of the wine district of Bureun iy, with special notes of its "palace for the poor," the hospital provided by Nicholas Rolin, Chan- cellor of the Duchy of Burgundy 500 years ago. The "Voice from the Past" of tnis month, is that of Naomi; it is excellently illustrated. There are stories, too„whiob have a favour not of Sunday only but of week-lay life , and its romances,—'new volumkand three serials corn I mince with this number of Tim aiming, which enters , upon volume seven. Is might be enough to transcribe this announcement to add to, the popularity of % this monthly for boys' of all ages from ten to A three score years And ten ; but we have just. 7 roma enough to add that some of Its shorter and completed contents are quite as attractiy! , as any of the serials. "In the clutch of the Kalemirs,' V GI flow to make a clock," .1 picture Post Cards," and ,? " About Touring Equipment " are brief, instruc;icie 4, papers which intelligent boys will appreciate and profit 7 ny,—.l simple statement ot the completion of the Vera' ' ably written and inexpensive "Live of a Century." 1800 to 1900, must suffice. The twelfth and last num- ber lies before us, with a copious index. It has been admirably and profusely illustrated.—Two other serial publieations by Messrs N 35,1308 are their " 20th Gentory Atlas," folio SiZ3, of wpich part I. is More us, appsr- entlV a wel,-lcsigned, weli-engraved series of maps ; and "Britannia's Bulwarks," which relates the herei.c, deeds of our seamen and" the honours of our ships. The latter work is near its completion ; both are issued In fortnightly marts at'sixoetice per parr,</p>
